__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaSutrasEn",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:"1.1.1",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0925\u093e\u0924\u094b \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0902 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092e\u0903 \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"Now, therefore, we shall explain dharma.",topic:"Opening of the Treatise",commentary:"The opening word 'atha' signals that this treatise presupposes prior learning and now turns to a new subject. Kanada announces that the entire work to follow is an exposition of dharma, framing the whole system of categories as ultimately in service of that goal."},{id:"1.1.2",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u092f\u0924\u094b\u093d\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0941\u0926\u092f\u0928\u093f\u0903\u0936\u094d\u0930\u0947\u092f\u0938\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u093f\u0903 \u0938 \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0903 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"That by which the attainment of worldly prosperity and final liberation is accomplished is dharma.",topic:"Definition of Dharma",commentary:"Kanada defines dharma not by listing prescribed acts but functionally, by its twofold fruit: abhyudaya (temporal flourishing) and nihsreyasa (ultimate liberation). This makes dharma an instrumental cause rather than a fixed code of ritual conduct."},{id:"1.1.3",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u094d\u0935\u091a\u0928\u093e\u0926\u093e\u092e\u094d\u0928\u093e\u092f\u0938\u094d\u092f \u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0923\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"The authority of the scriptural tradition rests on the fact that it teaches this dharma.",topic:"Authority of the Veda",commentary:"The validity (pramanya) of the Vedic tradition is grounded pragmatically: it is authoritative because it successfully communicates dharma as just defined, not on account of any independent claim to revealed status."},{id:"1.1.4",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u0942\u0924\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u092a\u0926\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0938\u093e\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u0935\u0948\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092d\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0902 \u0924\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0903\u0936\u094d\u0930\u0947\u092f\u0938\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Liberation follows from true knowledge \u2014 arrived at through the similarities and differences among substance, attribute, action, generality, particularity and inherence \u2014 a knowledge that is itself born of a special merit of dharma.",topic:"The Aim of the Treatise",commentary:"This programmatic sutra sets out the entire remaining project: liberation is reached through accurate knowledge of the six padarthas (later a seventh, absence, is added by the tradition), gained by discerning what they share and how they differ. Everything that follows in the work elaborates this single claim."},{id:"1.1.5",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092a\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0947\u091c\u094b \u0935\u093e\u092f\u0941\u0930\u093e\u0915\u093e\u0936\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0932\u094b \u0926\u093f\u0917\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u093e \u092e\u0928 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Earth, water, fire, air, ether, time, direction, self and mind \u2014 these are the substances (dravyas).",topic:"The Nine Substances",commentary:"Kanada enumerates the nine fundamental substances that serve as the substrata for all attributes and actions. Five (earth, water, fire, air, ether) are physical elements, while time, direction, self and mind are the non-material substrata needed to account for temporal, spatial, conscious and cognitive phenomena."},{id:"1.1.6",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0930\u0942\u092a\u0930\u0938\u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u092a\u0930\u094d\u0936\u093e\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0903 \u092a\u0930\u093f\u092e\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0928\u093f \u092a\u0943\u0925\u0915\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0902 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u094c \u092a\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u092a\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u092f\u0903 \u0938\u0941\u0916\u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916\u0947 \u0907\u091a\u094d\u091b\u093e\u0926\u094d\u0935\u0947\u0937\u094c \u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0917\u0941\u0923\u093e\u0903 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Colour, taste, smell and touch; number; magnitude; distinctness; conjunction and disjunction; remoteness and nearness; cognitions; pleasure and pain; desire and aversion; and effort \u2014 these are the attributes (gunas).",topic:"The Attributes (Gunas)",commentary:"The initial list of gunas spans physical qualities (colour, taste, smell, touch), relational and quantitative properties (number, magnitude, conjunction/disjunction), and mental qualities (cognition, pleasure, desire, effort) \u2014 all of which inhere in substances rather than existing independently."},{id:"1.1.7",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0909\u0924\u094d\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0947\u092a\u0923\u092e\u0935\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0947\u092a\u0923\u092e\u093e\u0915\u0941\u091e\u094d\u091a\u0928\u0902 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0902 \u0917\u092e\u0928\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"Throwing upward, throwing downward, contracting, expanding, and going \u2014 these are the actions (karmas).",topic:"The Actions (Karmas)",commentary:"All physical motion is reduced to five basic types. 'Going' (gamana) is treated as a broad category that later commentators subdivide further, but these five suffice to classify any instance of movement."},{id:"1.1.8",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0926\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0935\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0902 \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0935\u0926\u093f\u0924\u093f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u092e\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Being existent yet non-eternal, having the character of substance, being both effect and cause, and possessing generality and particularity \u2014 these are features common to substance, attribute, and action alike.",topic:"Shared Features of the First Three Categories",commentary:"Before distinguishing dravya, guna and karma from one another, Kanada first notes what unites them: all three are existent, impermanent, and stand as both causes and effects, and all three fall under the twin lenses of generality and particularity."},{id:"1.1.9",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u092f\u094b\u0903 \u0938\u091c\u093e\u0924\u0940\u092f\u093e\u0930\u092e\u094d\u092d\u0915\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0902 \u0938\u093e\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"That substance and attribute each generate further members of their own kind is a property they share.",topic:"Properties of Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"A substance gives rise to new substances and an attribute gives rise to new attributes of the same category \u2014 a parallel behavior that marks a genuine similarity between these two padarthas."},{id:"1.1.10",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092e\u093e\u0930\u092d\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0947 \u0917\u0941\u0923\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0917\u0941\u0923\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"Substances produce other substances, and attributes likewise produce other attributes.",topic:"Properties of Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"This elaborates the previous sutra: threads (substances) combine to produce cloth (a substance), while the colour of the threads (an attribute) produces the colour of the cloth (an attribute) \u2014 cause and effect stay within the same ontological category."},{id:"1.1.11",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u093e\u0927\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0928 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"Action, however, is never produced by another action.",topic:"Properties of Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"Unlike substance and attribute, action does not reproduce itself: one motion never directly generates a second motion as its effect, marking a key point of difference (vaidharmya) between karma and the other two categories."},{id:"1.1.12",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0928 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0902 \u091a \u092c\u093e\u0927\u0924\u093f \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"A substance is not destroyed by either its effect or its cause.",topic:"Properties of Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"The persistence of substance is asserted here: the arising of an effect-substance or the presence of a cause-substance does not annihilate a given substance, distinguishing its mode of continuity from that of attributes."},{id:"1.1.13",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u0909\u092d\u092f\u0925\u093e \u0917\u0941\u0923\u093e\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"Attributes, by contrast, are destroyed in both ways \u2014 by their effect and by their cause.",topic:"Properties of Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"Where substance survives both its cause and effect, an attribute (such as a particular colour) can be superseded by the arising of a new attribute or undone along with the cause that supported it, showing a genuine asymmetry between dravya and guna."},{id:"1.1.14",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:14,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0930\u094b\u0927\u093f \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u0967\u096a \u0965",text:"Action stands opposed to its own effect.",topic:"Properties of Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"The occurrence of an action (motion) is incompatible with the state its effect produces \u2014 for instance the motion of falling ceases the moment its effect, contact with the ground, is achieved \u2014 so action is inherently self-terminating in a way substance and attribute are not."},{id:"1.1.15",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0915\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0935\u0924\u094d \u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0932\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"Possessing action and attribute, and serving as the inherent (material) cause \u2014 this is the defining mark of substance.",topic:"Definition of Dravya",commentary:"Having established the shared features and differences among the three, Kanada now gives formal definitions. Substance is marked out as whatever can bear both action and attribute and function as the material cause (samavayi-karana) from which an effect inheres."},{id:"1.1.16",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:16,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0936\u094d\u0930\u092f\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0935\u093e\u0928\u094d \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u0947\u0937\u094d\u0935\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092e\u0928\u092a\u0947\u0915\u094d\u0937 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0932\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096c \u0965",text:"Residing in a substance, itself devoid of further attributes, and not serving as an independent cause of conjunction or disjunction \u2014 this is the defining mark of attribute.",topic:"Definition of Guna",commentary:"Attribute is defined negatively as much as positively: it depends on a substrate substance, does not itself carry further attributes, and \u2014 unlike action \u2014 never independently causes contact or separation between things."},{id:"1.1.17",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:17,sanskrit:"\u090f\u0915\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u092e\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0902 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u0947\u0937\u094d\u0935\u0928\u092a\u0947\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0932\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096d \u0965",text:"Residing in a single substance, being without attributes, and serving as the independent cause of conjunction and disjunction \u2014 this is the defining mark of action.",topic:"Definition of Karma",commentary:"Completing the trio of definitions, action is what inheres in exactly one substance at a time, carries no attribute of its own, and unlike guna, is precisely what does independently cause conjunction and disjunction."},{id:"1.1.18",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:18,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0902 \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096e \u0965",text:"Substance is the common cause of substance, attribute and action.",topic:"Common Causality",commentary:"Because attributes and actions cannot exist without a substance to inhere in, substance functions as a general (samanya) cause underlying the production of all three categories."},{id:"1.1.19",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:19,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096f \u0965",text:"So too is attribute a common cause of substance, attribute and action.",topic:"Common Causality",commentary:"Just as substance underlies all three, an attribute such as colour or conjunction likewise plays a causal role across the production of substances, further attributes, and actions."},{id:"1.1.20",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:20,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u0935\u0947\u0917\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0938\u092e\u093e\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u0966 \u0965",text:"Action is the common cause of conjunction, disjunction and velocity (further motion).",topic:"Common Causality",commentary:"Rounding out the picture of general causality, action's own distinctive contribution is that it is the shared cause behind contact, separation, and continued motion."},{id:"1.1.21",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:21,sanskrit:"\u0928 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u0968\u0967 \u0965",text:"Action is not a cause of substances.",topic:"Limits of Karma as Cause",commentary:"Having shown what action does cause, Kanada now marks its limit: action alone never gives rise to a new substance, correcting any temptation to treat motion as a material cause in its own right."},{id:"1.1.22",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:22,sanskrit:"\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f\u0930\u0947\u0915\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u0968 \u0965",text:"Because it is excluded \u2014 action is absent at the moment a substance actually arises.",topic:"Limits of Karma as Cause",commentary:"The reason for the previous claim is given tersely: whenever the effect-substance actually comes into being, the causal action has already ceased, so the two are never co-present, showing action cannot be the substance's producing cause."},{id:"1.1.23",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:23,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u0969 \u0965",text:"Substance is the common effect of substances.",topic:"Limits of Karma as Cause",commentary:"In contrast to action, substances do generally produce further substances as their effect \u2014 reaffirming, from the side of effect rather than cause, the special causal role of dravya established earlier."},{id:"1.1.24",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:24,sanskrit:"\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0935\u0948\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0928 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u0968\u096a \u0965",text:"Because of its dissimilarity to attribute, action is not the cause of further actions.",topic:"Limits of Karma as Cause",commentary:"This closes the sub-argument begun at 1.1.9: although attributes reproduce further attributes, action's dissimilar nature (established across sutras 11-14) means it cannot likewise reproduce further actions \u2014 setting up the fuller causal analysis of numbers, conjunctions and throwing that follows."},{id:"1.1.25",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:25,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092d\u0943\u0924\u092f\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0903 \u092a\u0943\u0925\u0915\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917 \u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0968\u096b \u0965",text:"Numbers from two upwards, separateness, conjunctions, and disjunctions (are originated by more than one substance).",topic:"Causality of Attributes",commentary:"Kanada specifies that certain attributes uniquely require multiple substances to exist. While a single attribute like color can reside in one object, you cannot have the number 'two' or the attribute of 'conjunction' with only a single, isolated object."},{id:"1.1.26",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:26,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0928 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u0968\u096c \u0965",text:"Action, which is the joint effect (of an aggregate), is not known, because of its non-combinative nature.",topic:"Nature of Action",commentary:"Unlike attributes such as conjunction, a single action cannot reside in multiple substances simultaneously. If two objects are moving, there are two separate actions occurring, not one shared action, because action only ever inheres in a single substance at a time."},{id:"1.1.27",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:27,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u096d \u0965",text:"A substance is the effect of conjunctions.",topic:"Causality of Substance",commentary:"When constituent parts (like threads) are brought together, that specific attribute of conjunction acts as the non-inherent cause (asamavayi karana) for the production of the new composite substance (like a cloth)."},{id:"1.1.28",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:28,sanskrit:"\u0930\u0942\u092a\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0930\u0942\u092a\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u096e \u0965",text:"Colour is the effect of colours.",topic:"Causality of Attributes",commentary:"The attributes of a composite whole are produced by the corresponding attributes of its parts. The color of the constituent threads directly causes and determines the color of the woven cloth."},{id:"1.1.29",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:29,sanskrit:"\u0917\u0941\u0930\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0935\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0928\u093e\u092e\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0947\u092a\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u096f \u0965",text:"Throwing upwards (is the effect) of gravity, volition, and conjunction.",topic:"Causality of Action",commentary:"Physical actions are complex effects born from multiple causes. To lift an object, the inherent gravity of the object, the conscious effort (volition) of the person, and the physical contact (conjunction) of the hand all act together as necessary causes."},{id:"1.1.30",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:30,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969\u0966 \u0965",text:"And conjunctions and disjunctions are the effects of actions.",topic:"Effects of Action",commentary:"This reiterates that the direct, primary result of any physical motion (Karma) is separating from a previous point in space (disjunction) and connecting with a new point (conjunction)."},{id:"1.1.31",book:1,chapter:1,sutra:31,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0947 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092e\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969\u0967 \u0965",text:"In respect of the general causality of substances and actions, it has been stated that action is not a cause (of substances and actions).",topic:"Limits of Action",commentary:"Concluding the chapter, Kanada summarizes a key physics rule: while substances and attributes can be the material or non-material causes of new substances or actions, an action itself is never the direct material or combinative cause of a substance or another action."},{id:"1.2.1",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"In the absence of the cause, there is the absence of the effect.",topic:"Nature of Cause and Effect",commentary:"Opening the second Ahnika, Kanada states the basic asymmetric dependence of effect upon cause: an effect simply cannot occur unless its cause is present."},{id:"1.2.2",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0928 \u0924\u0941 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"But the absence of the effect does not entail the absence of the cause.",topic:"Nature of Cause and Effect",commentary:"The dependency runs only one way: a cause can exist prior to, or independent of, whether its effect has yet arisen \u2014 clay exists before any pot is made from it."},{id:"1.2.3",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u094d\u092f\u092a\u0947\u0915\u094d\u0937\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"'Generality' and 'particularity' are notions that depend upon the cognizing intellect.",topic:"Nature of Cause and Effect; Samanya and Visesa",commentary:"Kanada introduces the crucial pair samanya (generality) and visesa (particularity) by noting that which aspect of a thing strikes the mind as general or as particular is relative to the act of cognition itself \u2014 the same property can be treated either way depending on the comparison being made."},{id:"1.2.4",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u092d\u093e\u0935\u094b\u093d\u0928\u0941\u0935\u0943\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0947\u0930\u0947\u0935 \u0939\u0947\u0924\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u092e\u0947\u0935 \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Because it is solely the cause of a recurring, uniform cognition, 'being/existence' (bhava) is generality alone.",topic:"Generality and Specificity in Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"Whereas most properties can function as either samanya or visesa depending on context, the highest property \u2014 existence itself \u2014 only ever produces the cognition 'it is', uniformly, and so counts as pure generality, never particularity."},{id:"1.2.5",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0902 \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0902 \u091a \u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u093f \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Substanceness, attributeness, and actionness are each both a generality and a particularity.",topic:"Generality and Specificity in Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"Unlike bare existence, the category-defining universals \u2014 substanceness, attributeness, actionness \u2014 are general relative to the many individuals they unite, yet particular relative to the higher universal of existence itself that comprehends them all."},{id:"1.2.6",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0947\u092d\u094d\u092f\u094b \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0947\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Except in the case of the ultimate particulars (visesas), which are particularity alone.",topic:"Generality and Specificity in Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"The one exception to the dual (general-and-particular) character described above is the class of ultimate, atomic particulars (visesa proper) \u2014 these serve only to differentiate and never to unify, so they function purely as particularity, independent of any perceiving mind."},{id:"1.2.7",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0926\u093f\u0924\u093f \u092f\u0924\u094b \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0941 \u0938\u093e \u0938\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093e \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"That by virtue of which substance, attribute and action are each cognized as 'existent' \u2014 that is Existence (satta).",topic:"Definition of Satta",commentary:"Kanada now names and defines the single highest universal: satta, or Existence, is whatever grounds the shared cognition 'it is' across all three of the first categories."},{id:"1.2.8",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u092d\u094d\u092f\u094b\u093d\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0902 \u0938\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093e \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Existence is something distinct from substance, attribute, and action.",topic:"Satta in Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"Although existence pervades substance, attribute and action, it is not identical with any of them \u2014 it is a separate, seventh-order property that inheres in all three while remaining conceptually distinct from each."},{id:"1.2.9",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0941 \u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0928 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0928 \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0903 \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"Since it merely occurs in attribute and action (rather than being one with them), Existence is itself neither an attribute nor an action.",topic:"Satta in Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"Existence's presence in guna and karma is a relation of residing-in, not identity, so despite inhering there it cannot itself be classified as a guna or a karma \u2014 it stands apart as its own kind of universal."},{id:"1.2.10",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947\u0928 \u091a \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"And also because it has no further generality or particularity of its own.",topic:"Satta in Dravya, Guna and Karma",commentary:"A second reason Existence cannot be reduced to guna or karma is that, being the highest universal, it has no higher genus above it and no differentiating particulars within it \u2014 a criterion the tradition will shortly apply to dravyatva, gunatva and karmatva as well."},{id:"1.2.11",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u0947\u0915\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0935\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947\u0928 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u092e\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"'Substanceness' has been explained by the fact of its being applicable to many substances.",topic:"The Universal 'Substanceness'",commentary:"'Substanceness' (Dravyatva) is a valid universal (Samanya) because it comprehensively applies to all the nine disparate substances (Earth, Soul, Time, etc.), uniting them under a single cognitive classification."},{id:"1.2.12",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947\u0928 \u091a \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"And because there is no (higher) generality or particularity in it.",topic:"The Universal 'Substanceness'",commentary:"Substanceness is a strict universal. It does not possess its own further sub-universals or particularities in the way physical objects do; otherwise, it would lead to an infinite, illogical regress of categories."},{id:"1.2.13",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0924\u094d\u0935\u092e\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"Similarly, 'Attributeness' has been explained by its existence in all attributes.",topic:"The Universal 'Attributeness'",commentary:"Just as substanceness unites all substances, the universal 'Attributeness' (Gunatva) unites all 24 disparate qualities (color, taste, desire, etc.) under one categorical umbrella."},{id:"1.2.14",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:14,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947\u0928 \u091a \u0965 \u0967\u096a \u0965",text:"And because there is no generality or particularity in it.",topic:"The Universal 'Attributeness'",commentary:"Like substanceness, attributeness is an abstract universal category and does not possess other generalities or particularities within itself."},{id:"1.2.15",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0941 \u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0924\u094d\u0935\u092e\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"'Actionness' has been explained by its existence in all actions.",topic:"The Universal 'Actionness'",commentary:"The universal 'Karmatva' unites all forms of motion (upward, downward, expansion, etc.) into the single category of Action."},{id:"1.2.16",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:16,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947\u0928 \u091a \u0965 \u0967\u096c \u0965",text:"And because there is no generality or particularity in it.",topic:"The Universal 'Actionness'",commentary:"Actionness is purely a universal concept inhering in all actions, free from possessing its own further abstract properties."},{id:"1.2.17",book:1,chapter:2,sutra:17,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0926\u093f\u0924\u093f\u0932\u093f\u0919\u094d\u0917\u093e\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0932\u093f\u0919\u094d\u0917\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u091a\u094d\u091a\u0948\u0915\u094b \u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096d \u0965",text:"Existence (Bhava/Satta) is one, because the mark of 'being existent' is undifferentiated, and because there is no mark of particularity.",topic:"Absolute Existence (Satta)",commentary:"Concluding Book 1, Kanada asserts that 'Existence' or 'Being' (Satta) is the highest, singular universal. Whether we say 'a pot is' or 'a soul is', the cognition of existence is identical across all categories, proving Satta is one, indivisible, and universal."},{id:"3.1.1",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u093e \u0907\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0903 \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"The objects of the senses are well known (uncontroversial).",topic:"Senses and Objects as Marks of Atma",commentary:"Opening Book 3, Kanada starts from common ground: nobody disputes that the senses and their objects (colour, sound, and so on) exist. The question to be built from here is what, beyond these, must also exist."},{id:"3.1.2",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0907\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u093f\u0930\u093f\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0947\u092d\u094d\u092f\u094b\u093d\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0939\u0947\u0924\u0941\u0903 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"The very fact that senses and their objects are cognized is itself the ground for inferring something other than the senses and objects.",topic:"Senses and Objects as Marks of Atma",commentary:"Kanada's key move: the act of cognizing sense-objects cannot itself be explained by the senses and objects alone \u2014 something further (ultimately, atma) must be posited as what does the cognizing."},{id:"3.1.3",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0938\u094b\u093d\u0928\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0903 \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"That argument (which claims cognition belongs to the body alone) is invalid.",topic:"Senses and Objects as Marks of Atma",commentary:"Kanada anticipates and rejects the materialist rival view that the body itself, without any further self, could account for cognition."},{id:"3.1.4",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Because there is no cognition in the cause (the mere material body).",topic:"Senses and Objects as Marks of Atma",commentary:"The material elements that compose the body show no sign of cognition on their own, so cognition cannot be attributed simply to the causal matter of the body."},{id:"3.1.5",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"And yet there would (on the rival view) have to be cognition in every effect (of that matter).",topic:"Senses and Objects as Marks of Atma",commentary:"If cognition were simply an inherent property of matter, absurd consequences follow \u2014 every material effect would need to be cognizant, which plainly is not observed."},{id:"3.1.6",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0905\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"And also because of the (observed) absence of cognition (in matter generally).",topic:"Senses and Objects as Marks of Atma",commentary:"The plain, ordinary observation that inert matter does not cognize anything is offered as further, direct confirmation against the materialist position."},{id:"3.1.7",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0926\u0947\u0935 \u0939\u0947\u0924\u0941\u0930\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0928\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0903 \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"'Some other entity is the cause' \u2014 this alternative argument too is invalid, as stated.",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"Kanada begins a methodological digression on inference itself, first dismissing an under-specified appeal to 'some other cause' as too vague to count as a valid proof."},{id:"3.1.8",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0902 \u0939\u094d\u092f\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0903 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"A wholly unconnected entity offered as evidence for another wholly unconnected entity is likewise not a valid proof.",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"For an inference to work, the mark and what it proves must stand in a genuine relation; citing something entirely unrelated proves nothing."},{id:"3.1.9",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093f\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093f\u0935\u093f\u0930\u094b\u0927\u093f \u091a \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"What is conjoined, what is inherent, what co-inheres in one and the same thing, and what stands in opposition \u2014 these (relations ground valid marks).",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"Kanada catalogues the legitimate relations (conjunction, inherence, co-inherence, and opposition/contradiction) by which one thing can validly serve as the mark or proof of another."},{id:"3.1.10",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"An effect can be the mark (proving the existence) of another effect.",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"Where two effects share a common cause, the presence of one licenses an inference to the other \u2014 smoke, for instance, as a mark of fire's other effects."},{id:"3.1.11",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0935\u093f\u0930\u094b\u0927\u094d\u092f\u092d\u0942\u0924\u0902 \u092d\u0942\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"A non-existent contradictory is the mark of what exists.",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"The absence of what would contradict a thing can itself serve as valid evidence for that thing's existence."},{id:"3.1.12",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u092d\u0942\u0924\u092e \u092d\u0942\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"What exists is (also) the mark of what does not (yet) exist.",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"An existing cause can serve as evidence for an effect not yet manifest, since the cause's presence points toward the effect it is capable of producing."},{id:"3.1.13",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u092d\u0942\u0924\u094b \u092d\u0942\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"What exists is also the mark of what has (already) existed.",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"A present existent can equally point back to a prior existent it depended on, rounding out the temporal directions in which valid marks can operate."},{id:"3.1.14",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:14,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u093f\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0915\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0926\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0965 \u0967\u096a \u0965",text:"Because the citing of a mark is always preceded by already-established knowledge (of the relation between mark and marked).",topic:"Nature and Types of Proofs",commentary:"All of the above inference-patterns are valid precisely because the underlying connection between mark and conclusion was already reliably known beforehand \u2014 inference builds on prior certainty, not guesswork."},{id:"3.1.15",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u094b\u093d\u0928\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"What is unestablished is a fallacious (invalid) mark.",topic:"Nature and Types of Fallacious Arguments",commentary:"Turning to what goes wrong in inference, Kanada first names the fallacy of citing a mark whose connection to the conclusion was never actually established."},{id:"3.1.16",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:16,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u0928\u094d \u0938\u0902\u0926\u093f\u0917\u094d\u0927\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u093d\u0928\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096c \u0965",text:"The non-existent and the doubtful are (both types of) fallacious marks.",topic:"Nature and Types of Fallacious Arguments",commentary:"Two further fallacies are named: citing a mark that doesn't actually exist in the case at hand, and citing one whose presence is itself uncertain."},{id:"3.1.17",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:17,sanskrit:"\u092f\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0935\u093f\u0937\u093e\u0923\u0940 \u0924\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0926\u0936\u094d\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096d \u0965",text:"'Because it has horns, therefore it is a horse' \u2014 (an example of a baseless, fallacious mark).",topic:"Nature and Types of Fallacious Arguments",commentary:"A concrete absurd example illustrates the first type of fallacy: horns bear no established connection to horse-hood, so no valid inference to 'horse' follows."},{id:"3.1.18",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:18,sanskrit:"\u092f\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0935\u093f\u0937\u093e\u0923\u0940 \u0924\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0917\u094c\u0930\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0928\u0948\u0915\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0915\u0938\u094d\u092f\u094b\u0926\u093e\u0939\u0930\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096e \u0965",text:"'Because it has horns, therefore it is a cow' \u2014 an example of the fallacy of an inconclusive (too-broad) mark.",topic:"Nature and Types of Fallacious Arguments",commentary:"Here the mark (horns) is at least genuinely associated with cows, but since other horned animals exist too, the inference remains inconclusive \u2014 a subtler fallacy than the horse example."},{id:"3.1.19",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:19,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0947\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u0937\u093e\u0926\u092f\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0924\u0926\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096f \u0965",text:"That (knowledge) which is produced through the proximity of atma, sense, and object is a different (valid) matter, distinct from these fallacious cases.",topic:"Existence of Other Atmas",commentary:"Having catalogued valid and fallacious inference, Kanada returns to the main line of argument: genuine cognition, properly arising from the conjunction of self, sense-organ, and object, is not vulnerable to the fallacies just described."},{id:"3.1.20",book:3,chapter:1,sutra:20,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0935\u0943\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0928\u093f\u0935\u0943\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0940 \u091a \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0917\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0928\u093f \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0947 \u092a\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0930 \u0932\u093f\u0902\u0917\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u0966 \u0965",text:"Activity and cessation of activity, observed in relation to one's own atma, serve as the mark (of atma) in others as well.",topic:"Existence of Other Atmas",commentary:"Closing the first Ahnika, Kanada extends the argument from one's own case to other selves: the same behavioral marks (purposive activity and its cessation) that reveal one's own atma license the inference that other bodies, too, are governed by their own atmas."},{id:"3.2.1",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0947\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u0937\u0947 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0938\u094d\u092f \u092d\u093e\u0935\u094b\u093d\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0936\u094d\u091a \u092e\u0928\u0938\u094b \u0932\u093f\u0902\u0917\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"In cases of proximity between atma, sense, and object, the presence or absence of resulting cognition is the mark of manas (mind).",topic:"Mana \u2014 Its Mark and Its Being a Dravya",commentary:"Turning to mind, Kanada notes a puzzle: self, sense-organ and object can all be present together and yet cognition sometimes fails to occur (as in distraction) \u2014 this variability itself proves a further factor, mana, that must also be present for cognition to arise."},{id:"3.2.2",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u0935\u093e\u092f\u0941\u0928\u093e \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"Its substancehood and eternality are explained as with vayu.",topic:"Mana \u2014 Its Mark and Its Being a Dravya",commentary:"Mind's status as a genuine, eternal substance is established via the same argument-pattern already used for air, rather than requiring separate proof from scratch."},{id:"3.2.3",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u093e\u092f\u094c\u0917\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e\u091c\u094d\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u092f\u094c\u0917\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a\u0948\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"Because efforts do not occur simultaneously, and cognitions do not occur simultaneously, mind is (established to be) one (in each person).",topic:"Mana \u2014 Its Mark and Its Being a Dravya",commentary:"The fact that a person can only attend to, or exert effort toward, one thing at a time is explained by mind being a single, minute substance rather than a diffuse plurality \u2014 its singularity is what bottlenecks cognition into a strict sequence."},{id:"3.2.4",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0923\u093e\u092a\u093e\u0928\u0928\u093f\u092e\u0947\u0937\u094b\u0928\u094d\u092e\u0947\u0937\u091c\u0940\u0935\u0928\u092e\u0928\u094b\u0917\u0924\u0940\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0935\u093f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093e\u0903 \u0938\u0941\u0916\u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916\u0947\u091a\u094d\u091b\u093e\u0926\u094d\u0935\u0947\u0937\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0928\u094b \u0932\u093f\u0902\u0917\u093e\u0928\u093f \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"The ascent and descent of vital breath, the closing and opening of the eyes, life, the movements of mind and the other senses, together with pleasure, pain, desire, aversion and effort \u2014 these are the marks of atma.",topic:"Atma \u2014 Marks and Substancehood",commentary:"Kanada now assembles a comprehensive list of vital and mental phenomena \u2014 breathing, blinking, life itself, sensation, feeling and volition \u2014 all of which point beyond mere matter to the presence of a self."},{id:"3.2.5",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u0935\u093e\u092f\u0941\u0928\u093e \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Its substancehood and eternality are explained as with vayu.",topic:"Atma \u2014 Marks and Substancehood",commentary:"Just as with air and mind before it, atma's status as a genuine eternal substance follows the same established pattern of argument."},{id:"3.2.6",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u092f\u091c\u094d\u091e\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0938\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u0937\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0902 \u0932\u093f\u0902\u0917\u0902 \u0928 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"In the case of (knowing another's atma as) 'this is Yajnadatta', there is no directly perceptible mark even where the senses and cognition are in proximity.",topic:"Atma is Known through Tradition, Not Direct Perception",commentary:"Kanada notes a genuine limit: while one's own atma is known through internal marks, another person's atma cannot be directly perceived by us at all \u2014 the felt certainty 'this is Yajnadatta' does not rest on seeing his soul."},{id:"3.2.7",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094b\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903 \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"On the basis of general observation alone, it (another's atma) cannot be inferred as a particular.",topic:"Atma is Known through Tradition, Not Direct Perception",commentary:"Even general inference from common patterns of behavior can establish that other selves exist in general, but not pin down the particular identity of any one of them."},{id:"3.2.8",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0926\u093e\u0917\u092e\u093f\u0915\u0903 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Therefore, this (recognition of a particular other self) rests upon tradition/testimony (agama).",topic:"Atma is Known through Tradition, Not Direct Perception",commentary:"Since neither perception nor general inference can identify a specific other person's atma, our practical certainty about who someone is must ultimately depend on communicated testimony \u2014 being told a name, and trusting it."},{id:"3.2.9",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0939\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f\u0936\u092c\u094d\u0926\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f\u0930\u0947\u0915\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093e\u0917\u092e\u093f\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"Since the word 'I' differs for each person, (knowledge of one's own atma) is not merely a matter of tradition.",topic:"The Word 'I' and Knowledge of Atma",commentary:"Unlike knowledge of others, the first-person 'I' cannot be a borrowed, testimony-based label, since each person's use of it picks out something different and immediate to themselves."},{id:"3.2.10",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u092f\u0926\u093f \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u092e\u0928\u094d\u0935\u0915\u094d\u0937\u092e\u0939\u0902 \u0926\u0947\u0935\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094b\u093d\u0939\u0902 \u092f\u091c\u094d\u091e\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"If it were (merely) a matter of perception (one could simply say) 'I am Devadatta, I am Yajnadatta.'",topic:"The Word 'I' and Knowledge of Atma",commentary:"Kanada raises a testing case: if the self were straightforwardly perceived like any object, there would be nothing to stop anyone from directly perceiving and claiming to be any other named person \u2014 an absurd consequence."},{id:"3.2.11",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f \u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0928\u093f \u0932\u093f\u0919\u094d\u0917\u0947 \u090f\u0915 \u090f\u0935 \u0926\u0943\u0922\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0935\u0924\u094d \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"When the mark is seen with reference to one's own atma alone, the resulting cognition, being firm, is as good as direct perception.",topic:"The Word 'I' and Knowledge of Atma",commentary:"The certainty of self-awareness, though technically arrived at through inference from internal marks, is so immediate and firm that it functions psychologically just like direct perception."},{id:"3.2.12",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0926\u0947\u0935\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094b \u0917\u091a\u094d\u091b\u0924\u093f \u092f\u091c\u094d\u091e\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094b \u0917\u091a\u094d\u091b\u0924\u0940\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0941\u092a\u091a\u093e\u0930\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091b\u0930\u0940\u0930\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"'Devadatta goes, Yajnadatta goes' \u2014 this could, through a figurative usage, be understood as referring merely to the body.",topic:"The Word 'I' and Knowledge of Atma",commentary:"Kanada considers whether everyday statements about a person 'going' might really just be shorthand for bodily motion, without implying reference to atma at all."},{id:"3.2.13",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0926\u093f\u0917\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0942\u092a\u091a\u093e\u0930\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"But this figurative reading itself remains doubtful.",topic:"The Word 'I' and Knowledge of Atma",commentary:"Kanada flags that reducing 'Devadatta goes' to mere bodily reference is not obviously correct either \u2014 the matter is left genuinely undecided at this point in the argument."},{id:"3.2.14",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:14,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0939\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0917\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0928\u093f \u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092a\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0930\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096a \u0965",text:"Because the cognition 'I' occurs with reference to one's own atma and not with reference to another's, it is clearly perceived as a distinct entity.",topic:"Ahankara and the Distinctness of Each Atma",commentary:"The fact that 'I' is never mistakenly applied to someone else's self shows that each person's use of 'I' picks out their own particular atma as something genuinely distinct from every other."},{id:"3.2.15",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0926\u0947\u0935\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094b \u0917\u091a\u094d\u091b\u0924\u0940\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0941\u092a\u091a\u093e\u0930\u093e\u0926\u092d\u093f\u092e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093e\u0935\u091a\u094d\u091b\u0930\u0940\u0930\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u094b\u093d\u0939\u0902\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"'Devadatta goes' \u2014 through this figurative usage born of self-identification (abhimana), the ego-sense ('I') is, to that extent, perceived with reference to the body.",topic:"Ahankara and the Distinctness of Each Atma",commentary:"Ordinary self-identification (ahankara) does loosely bind the felt sense of 'I' to the body in everyday speech, even though a deeper analysis will distinguish body from atma proper."},{id:"3.2.16",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:16,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0926\u093f\u0917\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0942\u092a\u091a\u093e\u0930\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096c \u0965",text:"But this figurative usage, too, remains doubtful.",topic:"Ahankara and the Distinctness of Each Atma",commentary:"Kanada repeats the earlier caution: identifying 'I' with the body via ahankara is only a loose, everyday manner of speaking, not a settled philosophical conclusion."},{id:"3.2.17",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:17,sanskrit:"\u0928 \u0924\u0941 \u0936\u0930\u0940\u0930\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0926\u094d \u092f\u091c\u094d\u091e\u0926\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0935\u093f\u0937\u094d\u0923\u0941\u092e\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0930\u092f\u094b\u0930\u094d\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0935\u093f\u0937\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096d \u0965",text:"But the cognitions of Yajnadatta and Visnumitra do not, by virtue of any particular feature of their bodies, become objects of each other's awareness.",topic:"Ahankara and the Distinctness of Each Atma",commentary:"Whatever loose connection ahankara draws between 'I' and body, one person's bodily particulars never grant them access to another person's private cognition \u2014 confirming that cognition tracks the distinct self, not merely the body."},{id:"3.2.18",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:18,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0939\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u092e\u0941\u0916\u094d\u092f\u092f\u094b\u0917\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092d\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0902 \u0936\u092c\u094d\u0926\u0935\u0924\u094d \u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f\u0930\u0947\u0915\u093e\u0935\u094d\u092f\u092d\u093f\u091a\u093e\u0930\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u0947\u0930\u094d\u0928\u093e\u0917\u092e\u093f\u0915\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096e \u0965",text:"The cognition of 'I', established individually through its primary and its associated sense \u2014 like sabda \u2014 is, by its consistent particularity across persons, not dependent on tradition alone.",topic:"Ahankara and the Distinctness of Each Atma",commentary:"Kanada concludes this sub-argument: the reliable, person-specific way 'I' functions parallels how sound (sabda) was earlier shown to be reliably known through inference, not testimony alone \u2014 so self-awareness stands on its own inferential footing."},{id:"3.2.19",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:19,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0941\u0916\u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092a\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0926\u0948\u0915\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096f \u0965",text:"Because of the undifferentiated way pleasure, pain and cognition arise (in every body), atma is (in this respect) one.",topic:"Unity and Plurality of Atma",commentary:"Considered purely in terms of the general kind of thing it is, atma appears as a single universal type, since the same basic process of pleasure, pain and cognition arises identically in every instance."},{id:"3.2.20",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:20,sanskrit:"\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0935\u0938\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0924\u094b \u0928\u093e\u0928\u093e \u0965 \u0968\u0966 \u0965",text:"But owing to particular circumstances and limitations, it is many.",topic:"Unity and Plurality of Atma",commentary:"Despite that underlying uniformity, individual atmas are distinguished from one another by their particular embodied circumstances, giving rise to a real plurality of selves in practice."},{id:"3.2.21",book:3,chapter:2,sutra:21,sanskrit:"\u0936\u093e\u0938\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0930\u0938\u093e\u092e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u094d\u092f\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0968\u0967 \u0965",text:"And also on the strength of the authority of the sastras (scriptures).",topic:"Unity and Plurality of Atma",commentary:"Closing Book 3, Kanada adds scriptural authority as a further, independent support for the doctrine of a plurality of individual selves, completing the examination of atma and mana."}]},1135,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaSutrasBooks4To8",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:"4.1.1",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0926\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0935\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"That (which is) eternal and uncaused is nitya.",topic:"Nityat\u0101",commentary:"Defines eternality. The ultimate particles of matter (atoms) are existent but have no material cause, making them indestructible and eternal."},{id:"4.1.2",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f \u0932\u093f\u0919\u094d\u0917\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"Its effect is its mark.",topic:"Nityat\u0101",commentary:"The existence of eternal, invisible atoms is logically inferred by observing their visible, composite effects (the physical world)."},{id:"4.1.3",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"(From) existence of cause is existence of effect.",topic:"Nityat\u0101",commentary:"Reiterates the fundamental rule of causation: an effect can only manifest if its foundational causes (the eternal atoms) already exist."},{id:"4.1.4",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0924\u0903 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u093f\u0937\u0947\u0927\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Anitya is the negation of existence of the vi\u015be\u015fa.",topic:"Nityat\u0101",commentary:"To call something 'non-eternal' logically implies the existence of something eternal to serve as its conceptual counterpart."},{id:"4.1.5",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"(The supposition of anityata only rises out of) ignorance.",topic:"Nityat\u0101",commentary:"The belief that absolutely everything is destructible and impermanent stems from an ignorance (avidya) of the eternal atomic substratum."},{id:"4.1.6",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u092e\u0939\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0928\u0947\u0915\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0935\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0930\u0942\u092a\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a\u094b\u092a\u0932\u092c\u094d\u0927\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Perception is possible only of objects of magnitude (provided) they have more than one dravya and have colour/form.",topic:"Perception of Objects of Magnitude",commentary:"Three conditions must be met for human visual perception: the object must be a composite of parts, have adequate physical size, and possess color."},{id:"4.1.7",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092a\u093f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u092e\u0939\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u0930\u0942\u092a\u0938\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0935\u093e\u092f\u094b\u0930\u0928\u0941\u092a\u0932\u092c\u094d\u0927\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"Though a compound of many dravyas and being of substantial magnitude, v\u0101yu is not perceptible because of the non-presence of colour.",topic:"Perception of Objects of Magnitude",commentary:"Explains why wind is invisible despite having physical mass and size: it intrinsically lacks the attribute of color."},{id:"4.1.8",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u0947\u0915\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0930\u0942\u092a\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0930\u0942\u092a\u094b\u092a\u0932\u092c\u094d\u0927\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Colour is perceived only when there is a particular colour or there is coinherence of more than one dravya.",topic:"Sensory Apperception as Knowledge",commentary:"Visual perception specifically requires the manifestation of color inhering within a composite physical structure."},{id:"4.1.9",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0947\u0928 \u0930\u0938\u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u092a\u0930\u094d\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"By this the cognition of taste, odour and touch, is explained.",topic:"Sensory Apperception as Knowledge",commentary:"The same physical rules of composite magnitude apply to our ability to taste, smell, and physically feel objects."},{id:"4.1.10",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u0935\u094d\u092f\u092d\u093f\u091a\u093e\u0930\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"(In case of) its (taste, smell and touch) non existence there will be no deviation.",topic:"Sensory Apperception as Knowledge",commentary:"If the necessary magnitude or elemental qualities are absent, the corresponding sensory perception will strictly fail to occur."},{id:"4.1.11",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0903 \u092a\u0930\u093f\u092e\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0928\u093f \u092a\u0943\u0925\u0915\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0902 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u094c \u092a\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u092a\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u091a \u0930\u0942\u092a\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0941\u0937\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"(The gunas) number, measure, separateness, conjunction, disjunction, otherness, non-otherness, and karma are visible to the eye (only if they) possess colour.",topic:"Sensory Apperception as Knowledge",commentary:"Abstract qualities and actions (like motion or numbers) are only visually perceived if the substance they inhere within is visible."},{id:"4.1.12",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0930\u0942\u092a\u093f\u0937\u094d\u0935\u091a\u093e\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0941\u0937\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"In uncoloured (things) they (the gunas mentioned above) are not visible to the eye.",topic:"Sensory Apperception as Knowledge",commentary:"You cannot visually see the 'motion' or 'number' of wind or empty space, because those substances lack color."},{id:"4.1.13",book:4,chapter:1,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u090f\u0924\u0947\u0928 \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u091a \u0938\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0947\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u0902 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u093e\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"By this the existence of the gunas and the cognition of all the senses is explained.",topic:"Sensory Apperception as Knowledge",commentary:"Concludes the chapter by establishing that all sensory cognitions follow these structured physical rules of inherence and magnitude."},{id:"4.2.1",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d \u092a\u0941\u0928\u0903 \u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0926\u093f\u0915\u093e\u092f\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0924\u094d\u0930\u093f\u0935\u093f\u0927\u0902 \u0936\u0930\u0940\u0930\u0947\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0937\u092f \u0938\u0902\u091c\u094d\u091e\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"That again prthiv\u012b, etc., dravyas exist in three forms - body, sense and objects.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"All composite elemental matter forms one of three things: physical bodies, the sensory organs within them, or external objects."},{id:"4.2.2",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093e\u0923\u093e\u0902 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092a\u091e\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0915\u0902 \u0928 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"(If) the conjunction of perceptible and imperceptible (dravyas) is not visible then it (body) is not the compound of five elements.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Kanada rejects the idea that biological bodies are equal compounds of all five elements; if they were, the imperceptible ether/air would render the compound unstable or invisible."},{id:"4.2.3",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0917\u0941\u0923\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0926\u0941\u0930\u094d\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"(If) other gunas are unmanifested, then too (prthvi, etc., other effective dravyas cannot be considered as the compound of five).",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"If bodies were perfectly mixed from all five elements, they would manifest equal distinct qualities of all five elements simultaneously, which they do not."},{id:"4.2.4",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0928 \u0924\u094d\u0930\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Not of three elements (prthv\u012b, \u0101pa and teja - is body composed).",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Further restricting the composition, rejecting the theory that the body is an equal tripartite mixture of Earth, Water, and Fire."},{id:"4.2.5",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0923\u0941\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0938\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u093f\u0937\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u0903 \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"(Though a) conjunction of atoms is not denied.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"While bodies have a primary dominant element (like Earth for humans), the structural conjunction of atoms from other elements to support it is acknowledged."},{id:"4.2.6",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d\u0930 \u0936\u0930\u0940\u0930\u0902 \u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0935\u093f\u0927 \u092f\u094b\u0928\u093f\u091c\u092e\u092f\u094b\u0928\u093f\u091c\u0902 \u091a \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Of these bodies are of two types - yonijas and ayonijas.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Classifies organic life forms into those born of a womb (mammals) and those not born of a womb."},{id:"4.2.7",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u093f\u092f\u0924\u0926\u093f\u0917\u094d\u0926\u0947\u0936\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0915\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"(Because early ayonija bodies) have antecedents in indeterminate time and space.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Non-womb born forms (like insects from sweat, or primordial beings) do not rely on standard procreative coordinates."},{id:"4.2.8",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"And (following) a particular dharma.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"The specific form a body takes is dictated by the specific accumulated merit and demerit (dharma/karma) of the soul inhabiting it."},{id:"4.2.9",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0938\u092e\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"Also (because) linguistic evidence have existed.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Traditional texts and linguistics support the existence of various categories of elemental and non-womb-born lifeforms."},{id:"4.2.10",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u092f\u093e \u0905\u0928\u093e\u0926\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"Since naming (or language) is without a beginning.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"The continuity of names and concepts throughout history points to an eternal cycle of creation, supporting these varied body types."},{id:"4.2.11",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092f\u094b\u0928\u093f\u091c\u093e\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"(Thus) ayonija bodies exist.",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Concludes the argument affirming that non-mammalian, unconventional forms of biological embodiment are real."},{id:"4.2.12",book:4,chapter:2,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0935\u0947\u0926\u0932\u093f\u0919\u094d\u0917\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"Also Vedas are the marker (proof).",topic:"Nature, Composition and Types of Bodies",commentary:"Appeals to the authority of the Vedas, which describe ethereal and primordial beings born without standard wombs."},{id:"5.1.1",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u093e\u092d\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0902 \u0939\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0947 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"The conjunction with and the effort of the \u0101tm\u0101 results in the karma of the hand.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"Voluntary physical motion begins with the conscious effort (volition) of the Soul, transmitted through its connection to the physical body."},{id:"5.1.2",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0939\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u092e\u0941\u0938\u0932\u0947 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"Similarly, conjunction with the hand (and its being heavy) results in karma in the pestle.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"Tools move because the motion initiated by the soul in the hand is transferred to the tool via physical contact."},{id:"5.1.3",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092d\u093f\u0918\u093e\u0924\u091c\u0947 \u092e\u0941\u0938\u0932\u093e\u0926\u0940 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0923 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f\u0930\u0947\u0915\u093e\u0926\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0902 \u0939\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0903 \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"But, the karma (produced) in the pestle is due to the impact and not because of its conjunction with hand but otherwise (is seen even without the conjunction).",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"When a pestle strikes a mortar and rebounds, that specific rebounding action is caused by physical impact, not by the conscious effort of the hand."},{id:"5.1.4",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u094b \u0939\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923 \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Similarly, conjunction of the \u0101tm\u0101 (is not the cause of) karma in the hand.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"During a sudden physical impact or reflex, the resulting motion of the hand is mechanical, overriding deliberate volition."},{id:"5.1.5",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092d\u093f\u0918\u093e\u0924\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092e\u0941\u0938\u0932\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0926\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0947 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Karma in the hand is due to the impact and conjunction with the pestle.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The recoil felt in the hand when striking an object demonstrates that mechanical force transfers backward into the body."},{id:"5.1.6",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0939\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Karma of \u0101tm\u0101 is also due to conjunction with the hand and (motion).",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The Soul moves in tandem with the body because they are conjoined; where the physical vessel goes, the animating Soul must follow."},{id:"5.1.7",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u0917\u0941\u0930\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092a\u0924\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"In the non-presence of contact (the pestle) falls due to its heaviness.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"A direct description of gravity. When physical support (conjunction) is removed, an object falls inherently due to its mass (gurutva)."},{id:"5.1.8",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0928\u094b\u0926\u0928\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0928\u094b\u0935\u0902 \u0928 \u0924\u093f\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0917\u094d\u0917\u092e\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"(In the) absence of a paticular impulse the upward or sideward movement is not possible.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"Motion contrary to gravity (upwards or sideways) requires the application of external force or impulse (nodana)."},{id:"5.1.9",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0928\u094b\u0926\u0928\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903 \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"From particular effort results particular impulse.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The magnitude and direction of the physical force generated is directly proportional to the conscious effort applied."},{id:"5.1.10",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0928\u094b\u0926\u0928\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0926\u0941\u0926\u0938\u0928\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"From particular impulse results the particular jumping (upwards).",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The height and velocity of an object thrown or jumping are determined by the specific strength of the impulse that launched it."},{id:"5.1.11",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0939\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e \u0926\u093e\u0930\u0915\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"By the karma of the hand the karma of a child is explained.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The mechanics of volition and movement applied to a hand also explain the complex, erratic movements of a child."},{id:"5.1.12",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0926\u0917\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u092f\u0935\u093f\u0938\u094d\u092b\u094b\u091f\u0928\u0947 \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"(And) so too, the bursting open of a burning object.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The sudden popping or movement of burning seeds is caused by internal forces acting similarly to sudden impulses."},{id:"5.1.13",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0928\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u0941\u092a\u094d\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f \u091a\u0932\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"In the absence of effort sleep-walking results.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"Movements during sleep occur without conscious, deliberate volition (prayatna), driven by subconscious mental connections."},{id:"5.1.14",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:14,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0943\u0923\u0947 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0935\u093e\u092f\u0941\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096a \u0965",text:"Karma in the grass is due to its contact with v\u0101yu.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The rustling of grass is an involuntary motion caused by the mechanical contact of wind particles."},{id:"5.1.15",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u092e\u0923\u093f\u0917\u092e\u0928\u0902 \u0938\u0942\u091a\u094d\u092f\u092d\u093f\u0938\u0930\u094d\u092a\u0923\u092e\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"Movement of the gem (towards the thief), movement of the needle (towards the lodestone) are due to unseen causes.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"Phenomena like magnetism, where motion occurs without any visible physical impulse or contact, are attributed to 'Adrishta' (invisible forces)."},{id:"5.1.16",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:16,sanskrit:"\u0907\u0937\u093e\u0935\u092f\u0941\u0917\u092a\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0903 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u0939\u0947\u0924\u0941\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096c \u0965",text:"In an arrow, many a time, particular conjunctions cause different karma.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The trajectory of an arrow changes as it experiences various physical resistances (conjunctions with air or targets) over time."},{id:"5.1.17",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:17,sanskrit:"\u0928\u094b\u0926\u0928\u093e\u0926\u093e\u0926\u094d\u092f\u092e\u093f\u0937\u094b\u0903 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0924\u0924\u094d\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093f\u0924\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0938\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093e\u0926\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0902 \u0924\u0925\u094b\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092e\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0924\u0930\u091e\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0967\u096d \u0965",text:"From impulse (results) the arrow's first karma the next from the inherent samsk\u0101ra which the arrow gains (from the first) and so on.",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"The foundational principle of Momentum (Samskara). The initial push generates motion, but that motion generates an internal quality that sustains ongoing flight."},{id:"5.1.18",book:5,chapter:1,sutra:18,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u0917\u0941\u0930\u0941\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092a\u0924\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u096e \u0965",text:"The absence of samsk\u0101ra and (the presence of) heaviness results in its (arrow's) fall (falling).",topic:"Description of Karma",commentary:"Once the momentum (samskara) is completely depleted by air resistance, gravity takes over and pulls the arrow down."},{id:"5.2.1",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0928\u094b\u0926\u0928\u092d\u093f\u0918\u093e\u0924\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u092f\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"From the impact of impulse and united contact (of entities) results the karma in prthv\u012b.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Earth elements move primarily through direct physical impact, pushing, or being dragged by other moving objects."},{id:"5.2.2",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0947\u0923\u093e\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093f\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"These particulars are caused by unseen causes.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"However, massive earthly events like earthquakes occur without visible push, hence they are driven by unseen cosmic forces (Adrishta)."},{id:"5.2.3",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092a \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u0917\u0941\u0930\u0942\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092a\u0924\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"Water falls due to absence of contact and due to (the presence) its heaviness.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Rain and water drops fall naturally due to gravity when they lose connection with the clouds holding them."},{id:"5.2.4",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u094d\u092f\u0928\u094d\u0926\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Fluidity (being the nature of liquids) results in flow.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"While gravity makes water fall, its specific attribute of fluidity (dravatva) allows it to spread and flow horizontally."},{id:"5.2.5",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0928\u094b\u0921\u094d\u092f\u094b\u0935\u093e\u092f\u0941\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0926\u093e\u0930\u094b\u0939\u0923\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"The rays of the sun in contact with v\u0101yu (causes the) rise of water.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Evaporation occurs because heat (sun rays) and air physically interact with water, driving it upwards against gravity."},{id:"5.2.6",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0928\u094b\u0926\u0928\u092a\u0940\u0921\u0928\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u092f\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"(Water rises up due to) the united contact, and the impulse of compression.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Water can also be forced upward (like in a pump) through physical pressure and being squeezed between other materials."},{id:"5.2.7",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0935\u0943\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093f\u0938\u0930\u094d\u092a\u0923\u092e\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093f\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"Circulation in trees is due to an unseen cause.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"The upward capillary action of sap in plants, defying gravity without visible mechanical pumps, is attributed to Adrishta."},{id:"5.2.8",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092a\u093e\u0902 \u0938\u0902\u0918\u093e\u0924\u094b \u0935\u093f\u0932\u092f\u0928\u0902 \u091a \u0924\u0947\u091c\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Freezing and melting of water, too is the result of its contact with teja.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Phase changes in matter are strictly controlled by the presence or absence of the fire/heat element (tejas)."},{id:"5.2.9",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d\u0930 \u0935\u093f\u0938\u094d\u092b\u0942\u0930\u094d\u091c\u092f\u0941\u0930\u094d\u0932\u093f\u0919\u094d\u0917\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"The mark (of the presence of teja) is the lightning and thunder.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Atmospheric fire is visibly confirmed by lightning strikes within the water-filled clouds."},{id:"5.2.11",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092a\u093e\u0902 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0926 \u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u091a\u094d\u091a\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0928\u092f\u093f\u0928\u094b\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"From the conjunction and disjunction of water (in the form of clouds) results lightning.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"The violent crashing and separating of massive cloud bodies physically generates lightning."},{id:"5.2.13",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0917\u094d\u0928\u0947 \u0930\u094d\u0927\u094d\u0935\u091c\u094d\u0935\u0932\u0928\u0902 \u0935\u093e\u092f\u094b\u0938\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0930\u094d\u092f\u092a\u0935\u0928\u092e\u0923\u0942\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u092e\u0928\u0938\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093f\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"The upward flaming of fire, the sideward movement of v\u0101yu, the first karma of atoms and mana are due to unseen causes.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Fundamental natural behaviors\u2014fire rising, wind blowing sideways, and the initial cosmic motion of atoms\u2014are intrinsic laws assigned to Adrishta."},{id:"5.2.15",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0947\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u092e\u0928\u094b\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u0937\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0941\u0916\u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916\u0947 \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"From the conjunction of \u0101tm\u0101, senses, mana and the object result sukha and duhkha.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Psychological experiences of pleasure and pain occur only when the chain of Soul, Mind, Senses, and Object is fully connected."},{id:"5.2.16",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:16,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u0928\u093e\u0930\u092e\u094d\u092d \u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0938\u094d\u0925\u0947 \u092e\u0928\u0938\u093f \u0936\u0930\u0940\u0930\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0938\u0903 \u092f\u094b\u0917\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096c \u0965",text:"When mana gets situated in the \u0101tm\u0101 there is absense of its beginning (of sukha-duhkha) then there is non-existence of duhkha in the body, that is yoga.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Yoga is defined as withdrawing the Mind from the senses and resting it purely in the Soul, halting all sensory pain and pleasure."},{id:"5.2.18",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:18,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u094b\u093d\u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0926\u0941\u0930\u094d\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0936\u094d\u091a \u092e\u094b\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096e \u0965",text:"When there is non-existence of this (adrsta), there is non-existence of conjunction and non-existence of manifestation that is mok\u1e63a.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Liberation (Moksha) is achieved when all unseen karma (Adrishta) is exhausted, permanently preventing the soul from conjoining with a new body."},{id:"5.2.19",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:19,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092a\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0935\u0948\u0927\u0926\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0938\u094d\u0924\u092e\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096f \u0965",text:"Darkness is a non-presence being dissimilar to the production of dravya, guna and karma.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Darkness is not an active substance or entity; it is merely the absence of light."},{id:"5.2.20",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:20,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0947\u091c\u0938\u094b \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0947\u0923\u093e\u0935\u0930\u0923\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0968\u0966 \u0965",text:"And because of the obscuring of teja (light) by other dravya.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Shadows and darkness manifest physically only when solid objects block the path of light."},{id:"5.2.21",book:5,chapter:2,sutra:21,sanskrit:"\u0926\u093f\u0915\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0932\u093e\u0935\u0915\u093e\u0936 \u091a \u0915\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0935\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u0915\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0965 \u0968\u0967 \u0965",text:"Di\u015b\u0101, k\u0101la and \u0101k\u0101\u015ba are also opposed to action thus they are non-dynamic.",topic:"Karma in Dravyas due to Particular Impulse",commentary:"Space, Time, and Ether are omnipresent backgrounds; because they are everywhere simultaneously, they cannot logically 'move' from one place to another."},{id:"6.1.1",book:6,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u093f\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0935\u093e \u0935\u093e\u0915\u094d\u092f\u0915\u0943\u0924\u093f\u0935\u0947\u0926\u0947 \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"The statements of Vedas are created knowledgeably.",topic:"Concepts of D\u0101na, Pratigraha and Ty\u0101ga",commentary:"Affirms that Vedic injunctions regarding morality and duty were composed with deliberate, supreme intelligence, not randomly."},{id:"6.1.3",book:6,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u093f\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0935\u094b \u0926\u0926\u093e\u0924\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"Giving is wise.",topic:"Concepts of D\u0101na, Pratigraha and Ty\u0101ga",commentary:"Charity (Dana) creates merit (dharma) when executed with intention, awareness, and correct understanding."},{id:"6.1.4",book:6,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u093f\u0917\u094d\u0930\u0939\u0903 \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"And so is receiving.",topic:"Concepts of D\u0101na, Pratigraha and Ty\u0101ga",commentary:"Accepting gifts must also be done with ethical awareness to avoid accruing demerit."},{id:"6.1.6",book:6,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u094d \u0926\u0941\u0937\u094d\u091f\u092d\u094b\u091c\u0928\u0947 \u0928 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"That (wisdom) is not present in impure food.",topic:"Concepts of D\u0101na, Pratigraha and Ty\u0101ga",commentary:"Merit cannot be generated from actions involving impure sources or unethical consumption."},{id:"6.1.7",book:6,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0926\u0941\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0902 \u0939\u093f\u0902\u0938\u093e\u092f\u093e\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"In impurity is hims\u0101.",topic:"Concepts of D\u0101na, Pratigraha and Ty\u0101ga",commentary:"The root of impurity and demerit fundamentally lies in violence (himsa) toward other living beings."},{id:"6.2.1",book:6,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u094b\u091c\u0928\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u094b\u091c\u0928\u092e\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0941\u0926\u093e\u092f \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"Actions of motives are seen or unseen, where it is unseen tends to abhyudaya.",topic:"On Seen and Unseen Deeds Which Produce Merit",commentary:"Virtuous actions performed without an immediate, visible worldly reward generate unseen spiritual merit (exaltation)."},{id:"6.2.2",book:6,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092d\u093f\u0937\u0947\u091a\u0928\u094b\u092a\u0935\u093e\u0938\u092c\u094d\u0930\u0939\u094d\u092e\u091a\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0930\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0915\u0941\u0932\u0935\u093e\u0938\u0935\u093e\u0928 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0938\u094d\u0925\u092f\u091c\u094d\u091e\u0926\u093e\u0928\u092a\u094d\u0930\u094b\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0923\u0926\u093f \u0928\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0924\u094d\u0930 \u092e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0930\u0915\u093e\u0932\u0928\u093f\u092f\u092e\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u092f \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"Fasting, continence, residence in the gurukula, life in the forest, giving, oblation... conduce to invisible result.",topic:"On Seen and Unseen Deeds Which Produce Merit",commentary:"Lists specific traditional austerities and practices that build spiritual merit rather than material wealth."},{id:"6.2.11",book:6,chapter:2,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0941\u0916\u093e\u0926 \u0930\u093e\u0917\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"From pleasure arises desire.",topic:"Activities of Anupadha that Produce Merit",commentary:"Experiencing pleasure inevitably breeds a clinging attachment and a desire for its repetition, driving the cycle of karma."},{id:"6.2.15",book:6,chapter:2,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0907\u091a\u094d\u091b\u093e\u0926\u094d\u0935\u0947\u0937\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0915\u093e \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0935\u0943\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"Due to desire or aversion man performs dharma or adharma.",topic:"Activities of Anupadha that Produce Merit",commentary:"All moral and immoral actions are ultimately motivated by the psychological drive to attain what we like or destroy what we hate."},{id:"6.2.17",book:6,chapter:2,sutra:17,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0941 \u092e\u094b\u0915\u094d\u0937\u094b \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u096d \u0965",text:"Mok\u1e63a is explained as being dependent on the karma of the \u0101tm\u0101.",topic:"Activities of Anupadha that Produce Merit",commentary:"Final liberation relies entirely on the soul's own disciplined actions to eliminate desires and transcend the material cycle."},{id:"7.1.2",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0926\u093f\u0930\u0942\u092a\u0930\u0938\u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u092a\u0930\u094d\u0936\u093e \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0926\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"The colour, taste, odour and touch of prthiv\u012b, etc., are anitya (since they are) anitya dravyas.",topic:"Nitya and Anitya Gu\u0146as",commentary:"When elements exist in their composite, visible forms (like a pot), their qualities are temporary, just as the pot is temporary."},{id:"7.1.4",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092a\u094d\u0938 \u0924\u0947\u091c\u0938\u093f \u0935\u093e\u092f\u094c \u091a \u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"They (the gunas) are nitya in \u0101p, teja and v\u0101yu as they (the dravyas) are nitya.",topic:"Nitya and Anitya Gu\u0146as",commentary:"However, the inherent qualities of elements at their atomic, uncaused level remain eternal along with the atoms themselves."},{id:"7.1.6",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0917\u0941\u0923\u092a\u0942\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0915\u093e\u0903 \u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0902 \u092a\u093e\u0915\u091c\u093e\u0903 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"The change of gunas produced by heat in prthiv\u012b has its beginning in the cause.",topic:"Nitya and Anitya Gu\u0146as",commentary:"Chemical changes in earth elements (like baking a clay pot) occur because the heat alters the qualities at the fundamental causal/atomic level first."},{id:"7.1.8",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0923\u094b\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0939\u0924\u0936\u094d\u091a\u094b\u092a\u0932\u0927\u094d\u092f\u0928\u0941\u092a\u0932\u092c\u094d\u0927\u0940 \u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0947 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"The perception and non-perception of anu and mahat has been explained to be nitya.",topic:"A\u1e47utva and Mahattva",commentary:"The rules governing what magnitudes (atomic vs massive) can and cannot be perceived are absolute and eternal laws."},{id:"7.1.20",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:20,sanskrit:"\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u092a\u0930\u093f\u092e\u0923\u094d\u0921\u0932\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0968\u0966 \u0965",text:"The roundness (of atom) is nitya.",topic:"A\u1e47utva and Mahattva",commentary:"Kanada asserts that atoms possess an eternal, perfect spherical shape (parimandala)."},{id:"7.1.22",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:22,sanskrit:"\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092e\u0939\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0915\u0936\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0925\u093e \u091a\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u093e \u0965 \u0968\u0968 \u0965",text:"\u0100k\u0101\u015ba and \u0101tm\u0101 by merit of their size are called pervasive.",topic:"Size of \u0100k\u0101\u015ba, Mana, Di\u015b\u0101 and Kala",commentary:"Ether and Soul are infinite in magnitude, meaning they exist everywhere simultaneously without boundaries."},{id:"7.1.23",book:7,chapter:1,sutra:23,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u0923\u0941\u092e\u0928\u0903 \u0965 \u0968\u0969 \u0965",text:"In (consequence of) non-presence of that (pervasiveness) mana is small.",topic:"Size of \u0100k\u0101\u015ba, Mana, Di\u015b\u0101 and Kala",commentary:"Because the Mind processes thoughts sequentially and selectively, it cannot be infinite; it must be atomic (point-sized) to connect with only one sense at a time."},{id:"7.2.9",book:7,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0930\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u091c\u0903 \u0909\u092d\u092f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u091c\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0936\u094d\u091a\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0903 \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"A conjunct is produced by the karma of one of the two, by the karma of both or by conjunction.",topic:"Conjunction and Disjunction",commentary:"Physical contact happens when one object hits a stationary one, when two moving objects collide, or when secondary contact is forced."},{id:"7.2.13",book:7,chapter:2,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u092f\u0941\u0924\u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u092f\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092f\u094b\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0917\u094c \u0928 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"There is neither conjunction or disjunction in cause and effect since they do not exist independently.",topic:"Conjunction and Disjunction",commentary:"A thread and the cloth it weaves cannot 'collide' or 'separate' like two independent objects, because the effect inherently relies on the cause."},{id:"7.2.20",book:7,chapter:2,sutra:20,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u092f\u093f\u0915\u0903 \u0936\u092c\u094d\u0926\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u0968\u0966 \u0965",text:"Relation between the \u015babda and its meaning is significatory.",topic:"Conjunction and Disjunction",commentary:"Language is conventional. There is no natural, physical connection between a spoken word and the object it describes; it is based on human agreement."},{id:"7.2.26",book:7,chapter:2,sutra:26,sanskrit:"\u0907\u0939\u0947\u0926\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u092f\u0924\u0903 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092f\u094b\u0903 \u0938 \u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u0968\u096c \u0965",text:"Samav\u0101ya is that by the virtue of which cause and effect may be said to be in one another.",topic:"Samav\u0101yah as a Dravya",commentary:"Defines the sixth category: Inherence (Samavaya). It is the invisible glue that intimately binds a whole to its parts, or an attribute to its substance."},{id:"8.1.2",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u093e \u092e\u0928\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0947 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"In them \u0101tm\u0101 and mana are not visible.",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"While we can perceive external matter, the Soul and the Mind are internal mechanisms that cannot be perceived by the outward-facing senses."},{id:"8.1.11",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u093d\u092f\u094b\u0917\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u0915\u094d\u0930\u092e\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0918\u091f\u092a\u091f\u093e\u0926\u093f \u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u0940\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u094d\u0930\u092e\u094b \u0928 \u0939\u0947\u0924\u094d\u092b\u0932\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"The non-simultaneous coming into being of cause and the causes being sequential in nature give rise to in the sequence of cognition of pot, cloth, etc., not just as (direct) result of a cause.",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"We perceive the world sequentially (one thought after another) because our atomic mind connects with causes sequentially, not simultaneously."},{id:"8.2.5",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u092d\u0942\u092f\u0938\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0935\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u0940 \u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0915\u0943\u0924\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Being plural and having odour prthv\u012b is the (material cause for the) cognition of odour (by the nose).",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na",commentary:"The olfactory sense (nose) is structurally made of the Earth element, allowing it to chemically interact with and perceive odors."}]},1136,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaSutrasBooks9And10",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:"9.1.1",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0915\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0935\u0926\u0947\u0936\u093e\u093d\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0917\u093d\u0938\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"In the absence of the interaction of kriya and gunas, before coming into being (an effect is said to be) non-existent.",topic:"Types of Non-Existence (Abhava)",commentary:"Defines 'Pragabhava' or antecedent non-existence. Before an object (like a pot) is created, it is non-existent. This type of non-existence has no beginning, but it comes to an end the moment the object is created."},{id:"9.1.2",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0926\u0938\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"Existent (becomes) non-existent.",topic:"Types of Non-Existence (Abhava)",commentary:"Defines 'Dhvamsabhava' or posterior non-existence (destruction). When an existing object is destroyed, it becomes non-existent. This type of non-existence has a definite beginning (the moment of destruction) but never ends."},{id:"9.1.3",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u0924\u0903 \u0915\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u093e\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0935\u094d\u092f\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"In the absence of interaction of kriy\xe4 and guna it (existence) is distinct from non-existence.",topic:"Types of Non-Existence (Abhava)",commentary:"Existence is fundamentally different from non-existence because a non-existent entity cannot possess actions (kriya) or qualities (guna)."},{id:"9.1.4",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0938\u091a\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0938\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Existent also (is) non-existent.",topic:"Types of Non-Existence (Abhava)",commentary:"Defines 'Anyonyabhava' or mutual non-existence (difference). A cow is non-existent as a horse, and a horse is non-existent as a cow. It denotes the fundamental separateness between two existing entities."},{id:"9.1.5",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u092f\u091a\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0926\u0938\u0926\u0924\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0926\u0938\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"And that which is different (from both existence and non-existence) is a non-existence (which will never be in existence).",topic:"Types of Non-Existence (Abhava)",commentary:"Defines 'Atyantabhava' or absolute non-existence. This refers to things that never existed, do not exist now, and will never exist (e.g., 'the horn of a hare' or 'color in the wind')."},{id:"9.1.6",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u0926\u093f\u0924\u093f \u092d\u0942\u0924\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u092d\u0942\u0924\u0938\u094d\u092e\u0943\u0924\u0947\u0930\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0930\u094b\u0927\u093f\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0935\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Perception of a non-existent thing may also result from non-existence of a past perception and a memory of the past, like the perception of an opposing object.",topic:"Perception of Non-Existence",commentary:"How do we perceive that something is missing? It arises from remembering the object that should be there and perceiving the empty space (the opposing object/locus) where it is currently absent."},{id:"9.1.7",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e\u093d\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u092d\u093e\u0935\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"Likewise too from perception of existence is non-existence.",topic:"Perception of Non-Existence",commentary:"The realization of non-existence relies fundamentally on the perception of the existent background or locus (e.g., seeing the empty floor to realize the pot is absent)."},{id:"9.1.8",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u090f\u0924\u0947\u0928\u093e\u0918\u091f\u094b\u093d\u0917\u094c\u0930\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u0903 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Hereby the non-pot, non-cow and adharma is explained.",topic:"Perception of Non-Existence",commentary:"Negative concepts\u2014such as 'not a pot', 'not a cow', or 'adharma' (non-merit)\u2014are understood through these very principles of mutual and absolute non-existence."},{id:"9.1.9",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092d\u0942\u0924\u0902 \u0928\u093e\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0940\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0928\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"There is no difference of meaning between that which has not been and that which is not.",topic:"Perception of Non-Existence",commentary:"Linguistically and logically, referring to something that 'has never occurred' is essentially the same as stating its absolute non-existence."},{id:"9.1.10",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0928\u093e\u0938\u094d\u0924\u093f \u0918\u091f\u094b\u0917\u0947\u0939\u0947 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0938\u0924\u094b \u0918\u091f\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0917\u0947\u0939\u0938\u0902\u0938\u0930\u094d\u0917 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u093f\u0937\u0947\u0927\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"There is no pot in the house is a negation of connection between an existent water-pot and the house.",topic:"Perception of Non-Existence",commentary:"When we say 'there is no pot in the house', we are not denying the existence of pots in the universe; we are specifically negating the conjunction (samsarga) between a pot and this specific house."},{id:"9.1.11",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u092e\u0928\u0938\u094b\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0926\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"Perceptual knowledge of atma is made possible by a particular conjunction between the atma and the mana.",topic:"Perception of Self and Other Substances",commentary:"The Soul (Atma) can perceive itself directly. This internal perception occurs when the Mind (Manas) makes a specific, focused conjunction with the Soul."},{id:"9.1.12",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"Like perception of the different dravyas.",topic:"Perception of Self and Other Substances",commentary:"Just as we can directly perceive external physical substances (dravyas) through our sensory organs, the Soul can be directly perceived internally."},{id:"9.1.13",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u092e\u093e\u0939\u093f\u0924\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0903 \u0915\u0930\u0923\u093e \u0909\u092a\u0938\u0939\u0943\u0924\u0938\u092e\u093e\u0927\u094d\u092f\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0947\u0937\u093e\u091e\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"In those whose mana is not always meditative and in those whose samadhi is consummated there is the perception of atm\u0430.",topic:"Perception of Self and Other Substances",commentary:"Yogis who have perfected their meditation (samadhi) have constant, direct perception of the Soul, whereas ordinary people may only catch fleeting glimpses when their mind turns inward."},{id:"9.1.14",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:14,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d \u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u0965 \u0967\u096a \u0965",text:"(They also perceive) karma and guna from the coinherence of things.",topic:"Perception of Self and Other Substances",commentary:"Through heightened yogic perception, one can also directly perceive the subtle attributes (gunas) and actions (karmas) inherent within substances."},{id:"9.1.15",book:9,chapter:1,sutra:15,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0926\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u0965 \u0967\u096b \u0965",text:"Atm\u0101 being in a form of co-inherence there is also perception of the guna of atm\u0103.",topic:"Perception of Self and Other Substances",commentary:"Because the Soul is the substratum for psychological qualities (like desire, pleasure, pain), yogic perception allows one to directly observe these qualities inhering within the Soul."},{id:"9.2.1",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0926\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093f \u0935\u093f\u0930\u094b\u0927\u093f \u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093f \u091a\u0947\u0924\u093f \u0932\u0948\u0902\u0917\u093f\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"Anything connected with any other thing as effect, cause, in contact, as contrary or as inseparably connected will serve as a mark.",topic:"Examination of Anumana (Inference)",commentary:"Outlines the foundational relationships used in logical inference. We can infer the unseen based on its relation to the seen: whether it is a cause, an effect, physically conjoined, diametrically opposed, or inherently connected."},{id:"9.2.2",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0926\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0938\u092e\u094d\u092c\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0935\u092f\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d \u092d\u0935\u0924\u093f \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"That this is associated to this and the cause and effect are connected such is known by examples.",topic:"Examination of Anumana (Inference)",commentary:"Inference requires a known universal concomitance (vyapti), which is established through observing prior examples (drishtanta) of cause and effect occurring together."},{id:"9.2.3",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u090f\u0924\u0947\u0928 \u0936\u092c\u094d\u0926 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"By this is \u015babda explained.",topic:"Examination of Anumana (Inference)",commentary:"Kanada asserts that Verbal Testimony (Shabda) is not a separate, independent means of knowledge (pramana), but is actually a form of Inference (Anumana), as we infer the truth based on the reliability of the speaker."},{id:"9.2.4",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0939\u0947\u0924\u0941\u0930\u092a\u0926\u0947\u0936\u094b\u0932\u093f\u0902\u0917 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u092e\u093e\u0923\u0902 \u0915\u0930\u0923\u092e\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0928\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"There is no difference of meaning in argument, reason, mark, evidence and instrument.",topic:"Examination of Anumana (Inference)",commentary:"Lists synonyms used in logic. Words like 'hetu' (reason), 'linga' (mark), and 'pramana' (evidence) all refer to the same logical mechanism: the middle term that proves the conclusion."},{id:"9.2.5",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0938\u094d\u092f\u0947\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u094d\u092f\u092a\u0947\u0915\u094d\u0937\u093f\u0924\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"(Because) they are implicated in the notion of belonging to that (sabda).",topic:"Examination of Anumana (Inference)",commentary:"The meaning of a word relies entirely on the intellect recognizing the conventional relationship established between the word and its object."},{id:"9.2.6",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u092e\u0928\u0938\u094b\u0903 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0938\u094d\u092e\u0943\u0924\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Smrti results from the particular conjunction of the atm\u0101 with the mana and from samsk\u0101ra.",topic:"Examination of Smrti (Memory)",commentary:"Memory (Smriti) requires three elements: the Soul, the Mind's focused attention (conjunction), and the subliminal impressions (samskaras) left on the Soul by past experiences."},{id:"9.2.7",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0938\u094d\u0935\u092a\u094d\u0928\u0903 \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"So too, dreams (result from conjunction of atma and man\u0430).",topic:"Examination of Smrti (Memory)",commentary:"Dreams are a psychological phenomenon operating on the same mechanics as memory. They occur when the Mind interacts with past impressions (samskaras) during sleep."},{id:"9.2.8",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0938\u094d\u0935\u092a\u094d\u0928\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"So too, consciousness in dreams.",topic:"Examination of Smrti (Memory)",commentary:"The subjective awareness and vivid consciousness experienced within a dream state are also manifestations of this mind-soul-impression interaction."},{id:"9.2.9",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"And also from dharma.",topic:"Examination of Smrti (Memory)",commentary:"Sometimes memories or prophetic dreams arise not just from worldly impressions, but are prompted by an individual's accumulated spiritual merit (dharma)."},{id:"9.2.10",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u0907\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u0926\u094b\u0937\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0926\u094b\u0937\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a\u093e\u093d \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"Ignorance results from the dosa of the organs of sense and from the dosa of samsk\u0101ra.",topic:"Enumeration of Avidya (Ignorance)",commentary:"Ignorance or false knowledge (Avidya) has two primary causes: physical defects in our sensory organs (like a diseased eye seeing two moons) or flaws in our mental impressions/memory."},{id:"9.2.11",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u094d \u0926\u0941\u0937\u094d\u091f\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"That (avidya) is imperfect cognition.",topic:"Enumeration of Avidya (Ignorance)",commentary:"Avidya is definitively characterized as flawed, corrupted, or imperfect knowledge that does not align with reality."},{id:"9.2.12",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:12,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0926\u0941\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0902 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0965 \u0967\u0968 \u0965",text:"Free from imperfection is knowledge.",topic:"Enumeration of Avidya (Ignorance)",commentary:"True knowledge (Vidya) is cognition that is entirely free from sensory or psychological defects, perfectly reflecting the true nature of categories."},{id:"9.2.13",book:9,chapter:2,sutra:13,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0930\u094d\u0937 \u0938\u093f\u0926\u094d\u0927\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0936\u0928\u091e\u094d\u091a \u0927\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0947\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0969 \u0965",text:"Knowledge of seers and the perfect vision results from dharma.",topic:"Enumeration of Avidya (Ignorance)",commentary:"The infallible, intuitive wisdom (Arsha Jnana) possessed by ancient seers is not derived through ordinary senses, but is a direct result of their immense accumulated spiritual merit (Dharma)."},{id:"10.1.1",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0907\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0935\u093f\u0930\u094b\u0927\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u092e\u093f\u0925\u0903 \u0938\u0941\u0916 \u0926\u0941\u0903\u0916\u092f\u094b\u0930\u0930\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"Due to the difference between desirable and undesirable causes and by opposition sukha and duhkha are different in their being.",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"Pleasure (Sukha) and Pain (Duhkha) are established as two completely distinct psychological qualities. They have opposing causes (desirable vs undesirable objects) and are mutually exclusive in their experience."},{id:"10.1.2",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u0936\u092f\u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0923\u092f\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0936\u094d\u091a\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0924\u094d\u0935\u0947 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"By their non-inclusion of doubt and assurance they are different from cognition.",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"Pleasure and pain are not mere 'cognitions' or thoughts. Cognitions involve doubt or certainty, whereas pleasure and pain are pure subjective experiences/feelings, categorically distinct from knowledge."},{id:"10.1.3",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0924\u092f\u094b\u0930\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092a\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0903 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0932\u0948\u0902\u0917\u093f\u0915\u093e\u092d\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"The production of them (doubt and assurance) is by means of perception and inference.",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"Doubt and certainty (which belong to cognition, not feeling) are produced through the standard epistemic mechanisms of direct perception and logical inference."},{id:"10.1.4",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0905\u092d\u0942\u0926\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u092a\u093f \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"The notion of the past too (is a difference between the cognition of sukha and duhkha).",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"We can have a cognition about a past event, but we cannot 'feel' a past pleasure or pain in the present moment in the exact same way; feelings are immediate."},{id:"10.1.5",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0924\u093f \u091a \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0926\u0930\u094d\u0936\u0928\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"And the effect (of such cognition) is not always observed.",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"Knowing something (cognition) does not always automatically result in a corresponding feeling (pleasure or pain), proving they are separate processes."},{id:"10.1.6",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u090f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093f\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0930\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"(Because sukha and duhkha) are observed to coinhere with the same object.",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"The exact same object can be a source of pleasure at one time and pain at another, meaning the feeling is not an intrinsic property of the object, but a subjective quality arising in the Soul."},{id:"10.1.7",book:10,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u090f\u0915\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0947 \u0907\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0915\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093f\u0928\u094d \u0936\u093f\u0930\u0903 \u092a\u0943\u0937\u094d\u0920\u092e\u0941\u0926\u0930\u0902 \u092e\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0924\u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0947\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0903 \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"The head, the back, the stomach and the vitals are in one (body) but their particularity results from the particularity (of their causes).",topic:"Examination of Sukha and Duhkha (Pleasure and Pain)",commentary:"Just as different parts of one body have distinct characteristics based on their specific causes, different feelings (pleasure/pain) arise in one Soul based on the specific varying causes affecting it."},{id:"10.2.1",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0947 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"That it is a cause, (this position) with regard to dravya (results) from the co-inherence of effects.",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"Reiterates the fundamental rule of material causality. A substance (like clay) is identified as the inherent cause because the effect (the pot) structurally coinheres within it."},{id:"10.2.2",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u0926 \u0935\u093e \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"Or from conjunction.",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"Conjunction (Samyoga) acts as the non-inherent cause. Without the physical bringing together of parts, the inherent material cannot manifest into a composite effect."},{id:"10.2.3",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0947 \u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u093e\u0923\u093f \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"Co-inherence in cause results in (non-coinherence in) karma.",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"Actions (karma) are non-inherent causes. They reside in the material cause (the moving object) and bring about conjunction, which then creates the new effect."},{id:"10.2.4",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e \u0930\u0942\u092a\u0947 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0948\u0915\u093e\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Similarly colour (results from) co-inherence of the cause in the object.",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"The attributes of the effect are derived from the cause. The color of the cloth exists because the color inherent in the threads transfers into the final composite object."},{id:"10.2.5",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0903 \u092a\u091f\u0938\u094d\u092f \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Conjunction of cloth due to the co-inherence of cause (is a non-coinherent cause).",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"The structural connection (conjunction) between the individual threads acts as the non-inherent cause that holds the resulting cloth together."},{id:"10.2.6",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Causation of contact also results from the co-inherence of cause in cause.",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"Causality can chain backward. The ultimate conjunctions tracing back through molecules and dyads ultimately rest on the foundational properties inherent within the eternal atoms."},{id:"10.2.7",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0938\u0902\u092f\u0941\u0915\u094d\u0924\u0938\u092e\u0935\u093e\u092f\u093e\u0926\u0917\u094d\u0928\u0947\u0930\u094d\u0935\u0948\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093f\u0915\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"(Because of) conjunct co-inherence there is a special feature of fire (heat).",topic:"Examination of Cause",commentary:"When we feel heat from a fire, we are perceiving it through a chain: our skin conjoins with the fire (substance), and the heat (attribute) is inherent in that fire."},{id:"10.2.8",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u094b\u091c\u0928\u093e\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0926\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u092f\u094b\u0917\u094b\u092d\u094d\u092f\u0941\u0926\u092f\u093e\u092f \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"Observances (according to the injunction of the scriptures) and those (karmas) that are seen since they have no visible effects are the cause of prosperity.",topic:"Authoritativeness of the Vedas",commentary:"Kanada returns to the theme of Dharma from Book 1. Vedic rituals and moral duties often produce no immediate, visible worldly results. Therefore, they must generate an unseen spiritual merit (Adrishta) that leads to ultimate exaltation (Abhyudaya)."},{id:"10.2.9",book:10,chapter:2,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u094d\u0935\u091a\u0928\u093e\u0926\u093e\u092e\u094d\u0928\u093e\u092f\u0938\u094d\u092f \u092a\u094d\u0930\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0923\u094d\u092f\u092e\u093f\u0924\u093f \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"Authoritativeness pertains to the teaching/tradition/collection since it is a declaration of that.",topic:"Authoritativeness of the Vedas",commentary:"The final sutra of the Vaisheshika system echoes the third sutra of the first book perfectly. The Vedas are an infallible source of knowledge precisely because they accurately declare the truths of Dharma and the mechanics of the universe, leading to ultimate liberation."}]},1137,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaSutrasBook8",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:"8.1.1",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"Cognition (Jnana) has been explained by the prior explanation of substances.",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"Kanada opens Book 8 by linking epistemology back to ontology. Cognition is not a floating, abstract phenomenon; it is a specific attribute that fundamentally inheres within a specific substance (the Soul/Atman), just as previously explained."},{id:"8.1.2",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0924\u094d\u092e\u093e \u092e\u0928\u0936\u094d\u091a\u093e\u092a\u094d\u0930\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0915\u094d\u0937\u0947 \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"In them (among the substances), \u0101tm\u0101 (Soul) and mana (Mind) are not visible.",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"While we can perceive external matter (like Earth or Fire), the Soul and the Mind are internal mechanisms. They lack the physical magnitude and color required for external visual perception."},{id:"8.1.3",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0926\u0947\u0936\u0947 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092a\u0924\u094d\u0924\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"The production of cognition occurs when there is a specific direction (contact).",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"Knowledge doesn't just happen passively. It requires a highly coordinated, directed chain of contact (sannikarsha): the Soul must connect to the Mind, the Mind to a sensory organ, and the sensory organ to the external object."},{id:"8.1.4",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0938\u0941 \u0938\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0915\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u092e\u0941\u0924\u094d\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Cognition arises when attributes and actions are in contact (with the senses).",topic:"Objects of Cognition",commentary:"We do not merely perceive raw substances. We perceive the universe through the attributes (color, taste, temperature) and actions (motion) that reside within those substances."},{id:"8.1.5",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0902 \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u0902 \u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Substance is the cause (of the cognition) of attributes and actions.",topic:"Objects of Cognition",commentary:"Because attributes and actions cannot exist independently, they must inhere in a substance. Therefore, the physical substance acts as the underlying material cause that allows us to perceive those attributes."},{id:"8.1.6",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0938\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0926\u094d\u0935\u093f\u0936\u0947\u0937\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"And (cognition arises) from Generality (Samanya) and Particularity (Vishesha).",topic:"Objects of Cognition",commentary:"Our brain processes reality by recognizing patterns. We perceive an object both by what category it belongs to (generality - e.g., 'this is a cow') and what makes it unique (particularity - e.g., 'this cow is black')."},{id:"8.1.7",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:7,sanskrit:"\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0947 \u0926\u094d\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0917\u0941\u0923\u0915\u0930\u094d\u092e\u0923\u093e\u092e\u0941\u092a\u0932\u092c\u094d\u0927\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096d \u0965",text:"The apprehension of substance, attribute, and action is dependent upon the substance.",topic:"Objects of Cognition",commentary:"Reiterates the primacy of the Dravya (Substance). Without the physical object serving as a foundational anchor, our senses would have nothing to interact with to perceive color, motion, or universals."},{id:"8.1.8",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:8,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0926\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0947 \u0938\u0902\u092f\u094b\u0917\u093e\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0903 \u0965 \u096e \u0965",text:"In the absence of that (contact), there is an absence of conjunction.",topic:"Mechanics of Perception",commentary:"If the chain of perception is broken anywhere\u2014if the mind is distracted, if the eye is closed, or if the object is hidden\u2014the necessary physical and psychological conjunction fails, and cognition does not occur."},{id:"8.1.9",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:9,sanskrit:"\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0947\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u092e\u0928\u094b\u0930\u094d\u0925\u0938\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0915\u0930\u094d\u0937\u093e\u0926\u094d \u092f\u0928\u094d\u0928\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0947 \u0924\u0926\u0928\u094d\u092f\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u096f \u0965",text:"That which is produced from the contact of the soul, sense, mind, and object is distinct (true perception).",topic:"Mechanics of Perception",commentary:"True, direct perceptual knowledge (Pratyaksha) is defined precisely as the successful, unbroken four-point connection between the Atma, Manas, Indriya, and Artha."},{id:"8.1.10",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:10,sanskrit:"\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u092f\u094c\u0917\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0926\u0947\u0915\u0902 \u092e\u0928\u0903 \u0965 \u0967\u0966 \u0965",text:"The Mind (Manas) is one, because of the non-simultaneity of cognitions.",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"A profound psychological observation. We cannot truly think two distinct thoughts or process two distinct sensory inputs at the exact same micro-second. This proves the Mind is a singular, atomic-sized gatekeeper."},{id:"8.1.11",book:8,chapter:1,sutra:11,sanskrit:"\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u093d\u092f\u094b\u0917\u092a\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0915\u093e\u0930\u0923\u093e\u0915\u094d\u0930\u092e\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u0918\u091f\u092a\u091f\u093e\u0926\u093f \u092c\u0941\u0926\u094d\u0927\u0940\u0928\u093e\u0902 \u0915\u094d\u0930\u092e\u094b \u0928 \u0939\u0947\u0924\u094d\u092b\u0932\u092d\u093e\u0935\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0965 \u0967\u0967 \u0965",text:"The non-simultaneous coming into being of causes, and the causes being sequential in nature, give rise to the sequence of cognition of pot, cloth, etc., not just as a direct result of a cause.",topic:"Examination of Cognition",commentary:"We perceive the world sequentially (experiencing a pot, then a cloth) because our point-sized mind must physically shift its connection from one sensory input to another. The sequence of our thoughts is dictated by the mechanical sequence of the Mind's movements."},{id:"8.2.1",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:1,sanskrit:"\u0907\u0928\u094d\u0926\u094d\u0930\u093f\u092f\u0926\u094b\u0937\u093e\u0924\u094d \u0938\u0902\u0938\u094d\u0915\u093e\u0930\u0926\u094b\u0937\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a\u093e\u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0965 \u0967 \u0965",text:"False knowledge (Avidya) arises from defects in the sense organs and defects in the impressions (memory).",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na",commentary:"Before detailing how senses work correctly, Kanada explains how they fail. Illusions or false perceptions happen when a sense organ is damaged (e.g., jaundice affecting vision) or when our memory misapplies past data to a current object."},{id:"8.2.2",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:2,sanskrit:"\u0905\u0926\u0941\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0902 \u0935\u093f\u0926\u094d\u092f\u093e \u0965 \u0968 \u0965",text:"That which is free from defects is true knowledge (Vidya).",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na",commentary:"Pramana (valid cognition) is simply the result of a sensory and mental apparatus functioning perfectly, without any physical or psychological obstructions."},{id:"8.2.3",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:3,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0924\u094d\u0930 \u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0935\u0924\u0940 \u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u0940 \u0965 \u0969 \u0965",text:"Among the elements, Earth possesses odor.",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na (Elemental Sense Organs)",commentary:"Establishing the foundational rule of elemental perception: specific physical elements uniquely possess specific physical attributes. Earth's defining attribute is smell."},{id:"8.2.4",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:4,sanskrit:"\u0930\u0942\u092a\u0930\u0938\u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0938\u094d\u092a\u0930\u094d\u0936\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0902 \u0935\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u092e\u094d \u0965 \u096a \u0965",text:"Cognition regarding color, taste, odor, and touch is hereby explained.",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na (Elemental Sense Organs)",commentary:"Our ability to perceive the external world is strictly limited to the elemental properties that our own sensory organs are composed of. Like perceives like."},{id:"8.2.5",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:5,sanskrit:"\u092d\u0942\u092f\u0938\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0926\u094d \u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u0935\u0924\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u091a\u094d\u091a \u092a\u0943\u0925\u093f\u0935\u0940 \u0917\u0928\u094d\u0927\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0947 \u092a\u094d\u0930\u0915\u0943\u0924\u093f\u0903 \u0965 \u096b \u0965",text:"Being plural and having odour, prthiv\u012b (Earth) is the natural material cause for the cognition of odour (by the nose).",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na (Elemental Sense Organs)",commentary:"The olfactory sense (the nose) can perceive smell precisely because it is structurally composed of the Earth element. The Earth atoms in the nose chemically interact with the Earth atoms of external objects."},{id:"8.2.6",book:8,chapter:2,sutra:6,sanskrit:"\u0924\u0925\u093e\u092a\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0947\u091c\u094b \u0935\u093e\u092f\u0941\u0936\u094d\u091a \u0930\u0938\u0942\u092a\u0938\u094d\u092a\u0930\u094d\u0936\u091c\u094d\u091e\u093e\u0928\u0947\u0937\u0941 \u0965 \u096c \u0965",text:"Likewise, Water, Fire, and Air are the material causes for the cognition of taste, color, and touch, respectively.",topic:"Types of J\xf1\u0101na (Elemental Sense Organs)",commentary:"Completes the physiological mapping of the Vaisheshika system. The tongue perceives taste because it is made of Water atoms; the eye perceives light/color because it is made of Fire atoms; the skin perceives touch/pressure because it is made of Air atoms."}]},1138,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaSutrasMl",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[]},1139,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaConceptsEn",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:"dharma",diagramId:"dharma",content:{en:{title:"Dharma",summary:"That from which true prosperity and ultimate good are achieved."}}},{id:"padartha",diagramId:"padartha",content:{en:{title:"Padartha",summary:"The categories of reality into which all existents can be classified."}}},{id:"dravya",diagramId:"dravya",content:{en:{title:"Dravya",summary:"Substance, the substrate where attributes and actions reside."}}},{id:"guna-karma",diagramId:"guna-karma",content:{en:{title:"Guna & Karma",summary:"Attributes (Guna) and Actions (Karma) that exist within substances."}}},{id:"paramanu",diagramId:"paramanu",content:{en:{title:"Paramanu",summary:"The indivisible, eternal, infinitesimal atoms that make up the material world."}}},{id:"samanya-visesa",diagramId:"samanya-visesa",content:{en:{title:"Samanya & Visesa",summary:"Universals (Samanya) and Particularities (Visesa)."}}},{id:"samavaya",diagramId:"samavaya",content:{en:{title:"Samavaya",summary:"Inherence, the intimate and inseparable relation between a whole and its parts."}}},{id:"abhava",diagramId:"abhava",content:{en:{title:"Abhava",summary:"Non-existence, treated as an ontological reality in later Vaisesika."}}},{id:"manas",diagramId:"manas",content:{en:{title:"Manas",summary:"The mind, an atomic substance responsible for internal perception."}}},{id:"asatkaryavada",diagramId:"asatkaryavada",content:{en:{title:"Asatkaryavada",summary:"The theory that the effect does not pre-exist in the cause, but is a completely new creation."}}},{id:"atman",diagramId:"atman",content:{en:{title:"Atman",summary:"The eternal soul, which is distinct from the mind and body."}}},{id:"moksha",diagramId:"moksha",content:{en:{title:"Moksha",summary:"Liberation, achieved through the true knowledge of the categories."}}}]},1140,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaThreadEn",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}});var t=r(d[0]);const n=[{id:"v-thread-1-dharma",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"dharma",verseIds:["1.1.1","1.1.2","1.1.4"],content:{en:{title:"The Physics of Liberation",narrative:"Welcome to Vai\u015be\u1e63ika, the ancient Indian school of atomic pluralism and rigorous ontology. Founded by Mahar\u1e63i Ka\u1e47\u0101da, it approaches ultimate liberation (Mok\u1e63a) not through mysticism, but through the uncompromising, objective analysis of reality. It begins with a profound claim: true spiritual exaltation (Abhyudaya) and final liberation (Ni\u1e25\u015breyasa) are achieved through 'Dharma'\u2014which Ka\u1e47\u0101da defines as the precise, categorical understanding of the physical and metaphysical universe."}}},{id:"v-thread-2-padarthas",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"padartha",verseIds:["1.1.4","1.1.8"],content:{en:{title:"The Six Categories of Reality",narrative:"To understand the universe, we must first map it. Vai\u015be\u1e63ika argues that absolutely everything that exists, can be known, and can be named falls into six fundamental categories (Pad\u0101rthas). These are the blueprints of existence: Substance (Dravya), Quality (Gu\u1e47a), Action (Karma), Generality (S\u0101m\u0101nya), Particularity (Vi\u015be\u1e63a), and Inherence (Samav\u0101ya). By grasping the shared and differing properties of these six, the intellect pierces through the illusion of reality."}}},{id:"v-thread-3-dravya",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"dravya",verseIds:["1.1.5","1.1.15","1.1.18"],content:{en:{title:"Dravya: The Substantive Foundation",narrative:"Of the six categories, Substance (Dravya) is the absolute foundation. Qualities and Actions cannot exist in a vacuum; they must inhabit a Substrate. Vai\u015be\u1e63ika identifies exactly nine eternal substrates that compose reality: The five elements (Earth, Water, Fire, Air, Ether), the coordinates of reality (Time and Space), the observing Soul (\u0100tman), and the instrument of thought (Mind/Manas). Nothing else physically exists."}}},{id:"v-thread-4-guna-karma",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"guna-karma",verseIds:["1.1.6","1.1.7","1.1.16","1.1.17"],content:{en:{title:"Gu\u1e47a & Karma: The Dynamics of Being",narrative:"Substances are static without attributes and movement. Gu\u1e47a (Quality) defines the nature of a substance\u2014such as color, taste, number, and dimension\u2014but also includes psychological qualities like pleasure, pain, and intellect (which inhere only in the Soul). Karma (Action) governs absolute motion: upward, downward, contraction, expansion, and locomotion. Together, Gu\u1e47a and Karma weave the static Substrates into a dynamic, living universe."}}},{id:"v-thread-5-paramanu",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"paramanu",verseIds:["4.1.1","4.1.2","4.1.4","4.1.5"],content:{en:{title:"Param\u0101\u1e47uv\u0101da: The Atomic Reality",narrative:"If we divide matter continually, do we reach nothingness? Vai\u015be\u1e63ika says no. At the absolute limit of physical division lies the Param\u0101\u1e47u (Atom)\u2014eternal, indestructible, spherical, and invisible. The four elements (Earth, Water, Fire, Air) are fundamentally composed of these distinct elemental atoms. Creation is simply the temporary conjunction of these eternal atoms; destruction is their disjunction. Matter is never destroyed, only rearranged."}}},{id:"v-thread-6-samanya-visesa",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"samanya-visesa",verseIds:["1.2.3","1.2.4","1.2.5","1.2.6"],content:{en:{title:"Universals and the Ultimate Particular",narrative:"How do we recognize a cow as a 'cow'? Through S\u0101m\u0101nya (Generality)\u2014the objective, universal essence shared by all instances of a class. But what makes two identical atoms different? That requires Vi\u015be\u1e63a (Particularity)\u2014the ultimate, indivisible uniqueness inherent in eternal substances. It is this intense focus on 'Vi\u015be\u1e63a' (the particular) that gives the Vai\u015be\u1e63ika system its very name."}}},{id:"v-thread-7-samavaya",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"samavaya",verseIds:["7.2.26"],content:{en:{title:"Samav\u0101ya: The Invisible Glue",narrative:"If a cloth is made of threads, how do they relate? They are bound by Samav\u0101ya (Inherence)\u2014the inseparable, eternal relationship between a whole and its parts, a substance and its qualities, or a motion and the moving object. Unlike a temporary contact (like a hand touching a table), Samav\u0101ya is the structural glue of reality. Without it, the universe would disintegrate into isolated, unrelated fragments."}}},{id:"v-thread-8-abhava",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"abhava",verseIds:["9.1.1","9.1.2","9.1.4","9.1.9"],content:{en:{title:"Abh\u0101va: The Power of Non-Existence",narrative:"Though originally possessing six categories, the tradition later formalized a seventh: Abh\u0101va (Non-existence). In Vai\u015be\u1e63ika logic, 'nothingness' is a knowable, observable reality. Knowing that 'a pot does not exist on this table' is a specific, valid form of cognition. Whether it is prior non-existence (before a pot is made) or posterior non-existence (after a pot is smashed), Abh\u0101va allows us to logically map the boundaries of existence."}}},{id:"v-thread-9-epistemology",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"manas",verseIds:["8.1.1","8.1.2","8.1.11","9.1.11"],content:{en:{title:"The Mechanics of the Mind",narrative:"How does the eternal Soul know the atomic world? Through the Manas (Mind). Unlike Vedanta, Vai\u015be\u1e63ika considers the Mind to be an atomic, internal substance. Because the Mind is infinitely small (atomic), it can only process one piece of sensory data at a time. The illusion of multitasking is merely the mind moving at incomprehensible speeds. Cognition is a mechanical spark resulting from the sequential contact of Soul, Mind, Sense Organ, and Object."}}},{id:"v-thread-10-causality",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"asatkaryavada",verseIds:["1.2.1","1.2.2","10.2.1","10.2.2"],content:{en:{title:"Asatk\u0101ryav\u0101da: The New Creation",narrative:"Unlike S\u0101\u1e43khya, which believes the effect already exists hidden inside the cause (the statue is hiding in the marble), Vai\u015be\u1e63ika champions Asatk\u0101ryav\u0101da (Non-existence of the effect). When atoms combine to form a new object, that object is a completely new, emergent entity that did not exist before. Reality is constantly generating novel phenomena through the relentless conjunction of eternal causes."}}},{id:"v-thread-11-atman",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"atman",verseIds:["3.2.4","3.2.19","3.2.20"],content:{en:{title:"\u0100tman: The Substrate of Consciousness",narrative:"The Soul (\u0100tman) in Vai\u015be\u1e63ika is a remarkable concept. It is eternal and all-pervading (infinite in size), yet inherently unconscious. Consciousness, desire, pleasure, and pain are not the essence of the Soul; they are adventitious 'Qualities' (Gu\u1e47as) that temporarily arise in the Soul only when it comes into contact with the atomic Mind (Manas) inside a physical body. Without the Mind, the Soul is entirely inert."}}},{id:"v-thread-12-moksha",textId:"vaisesika-sutras",conceptId:"moksha",verseIds:["5.2.16","5.2.18"],content:{en:{title:"Mok\u1e63a: The Grand Disjunction",narrative:"If consciousness and suffering are produced by the contact between the Soul and the Mind, then liberation (Mok\u1e63a) is their ultimate separation. When a yogi acquires absolute knowledge of the Pad\u0101rthas, the unseen forces (Ad\u1e5b\u1e63\u1e6da) of Karma cease. The atomic Mind detaches from the Soul. The Soul remains eternal, infinite, and utterly devoid of all qualities\u2014free from pain, but also free from consciousness itself. A state of pure, absolute stillness."}}}].map(n=>({...n,content:{...n.content,ml:t.vaisesikaThreadMl[n.id]}}))},1141,[1142]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"vaisesikaThreadMl",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={"v-thread-1-dharma":{title:"\u0d2e\u0d4b\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d37\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d47\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d2d\u0d57\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d36\u0d3e\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d02",narrative:"\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3e\u0d1a\u0d40\u0d28 \u0d2d\u0d3e\u0d30\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d46 \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d7c\u0d36\u0d28\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2a\u0d20\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 (Ontology) \u0d26\u0d7c\u0d36\u0d28\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d35\u0d48\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d47\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d38\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d17\u0d24\u0d02. \u0d2e\u0d39\u0d7c\u0d37\u0d3f \u0d15\u0d23\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d7b \u0d38\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a \u0d08 \u0d26\u0d7c\u0d36\u0d28\u0d02, \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15 \u0d2e\u0d4b\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d37\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d38\u0d2e\u0d40\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d17\u0d42\u0d22\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d42\u0d1f\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32, \u0d2e\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d4d \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4a\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d35\u0d40\u0d34\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d35\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d37\u0d4d\u0d20\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d15\u0d32\u0d28\u0d02 \u0d1a\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4a\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d35\u0d33\u0d30\u0d46 \u0d06\u0d34\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d16\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d2a\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4b\u0d1f\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d3e\u0d30\u0d02\u0d2d\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d: \u0d2f\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25 \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d40\u0d2f \u0d09\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d2e\u0d28\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 (\u0d05\u0d2d\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d26\u0d2f\u0d02) \u0d05\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d2e \u0d2e\u0d4b\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d37\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 (\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d03\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d47\u0d2f\u0d38\u0d02) \u0d32\u0d2d\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d '\u0d27\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e'\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d42\u0d1f\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d2d\u0d57\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d27\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d57\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2a\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d15\u0d43\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d7c\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d17\u0d40\u0d15\u0d43\u0d24\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d27\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d02 \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d15\u0d23\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d7b \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7c\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d1a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41."},"v-thread-2-padarthas":{title:"\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d06\u0d31\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d7c\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d17\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e",narrative:"\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2a\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d7b, \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d30\u0d47\u0d16 \u0d24\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d31\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d47\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d24\u0d41\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d4d. \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d05\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d2a\u0d47\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d0f\u0d24\u0d4a\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d30\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d06\u0d31\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d28 \u0d35\u0d7c\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d17\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d3f\u0d7d (\u0d2a\u0d26\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e) \u0d2a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d46\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d15\u0d23\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d7b \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41: \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d02, \u0d17\u0d41\u0d23\u0d02, \u0d15\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d02, \u0d38\u0d3e\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d02, \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d02, \u0d38\u0d2e\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d02. \u0d08 \u0d06\u0d31\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d31\u0d15\u0d33\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4a\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d09\u0d7e\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4a\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2f\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32."},"v-thread-3-dravyas":{title:"\u0d12\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e",narrative:"\u0d0f\u0d31\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d2a\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d35\u0d7c\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d17\u0d02 \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d, \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d30\u0d23\u0d02 \u0d17\u0d41\u0d23\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d36\u0d42\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32 - \u0d05\u0d35\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d12\u0d30\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d02 \u0d06\u0d35\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d08 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d02 \u0d2e\u0d41\u0d34\u0d41\u0d35\u0d7b \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d15\u0d43\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d02 \u0d12\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d28 \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d46 \u0d35\u0d48\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41: \u0d2a\u0d43\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d3f (\u0d2d\u0d42\u0d2e\u0d3f), \u0d05\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4d (\u0d1c\u0d32\u0d02), \u0d24\u0d47\u0d1c\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d (\u0d05\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f), \u0d35\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41, \u0d06\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d02, \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d32\u0d02, \u0d26\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d, \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d4d, \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d. \u0d12\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24 \u0d08 \u0d12\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d7b\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d38\u0d4d."},"v-thread-4-paramanu":{title:"\u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d7e",narrative:"\u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d3f \u0d2e\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d1c\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d15. \u0d35\u0d40\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d1c\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d15. \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d46 \u0d05\u0d28\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d1c\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4b? \u0d15\u0d23\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d7b \u0d2a\u0d31\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41: \u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32. \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d1c\u0d28\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d28\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d, \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d2a\u0d7c\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d15\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d2e\u0d23\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d41\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d33\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d05\u0d28\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d15\u0d23\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e \u0d09\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41, \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d7d \u0d05\u0d35\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d46 \u0d35\u0d32\u0d41\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d41\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41. \u0d05\u0d24\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4a\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d4d, \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d1c\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24, \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f, \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d05\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d28 \u0d15\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d05\u0d35\u0d38\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d23\u0d02: \u0d2a\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d41. \u0d2d\u0d42\u0d2e\u0d3f, \u0d1c\u0d32\u0d02, \u0d05\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f, \u0d35\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41 \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d35 \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d08 \u0d28\u0d36\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24 \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d33\u0d3e\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d."},"v-thread-5-karma":{title:"\u0d15\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d24",narrative:"\u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d32\u0d2e\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32; \u0d05\u0d35 \u0d1a\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41. \u0d07\u0d24\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d15\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d02 (\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d43\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f) \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d35\u0d7c\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d17\u0d02. \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d7d \u0d1a\u0d32\u0d28\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d30\u0d39\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32. \u0d0e\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d05\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d32\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d24\u0d3e\u0d34\u0d47\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d40\u0d34\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d15\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d32\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d12\u0d34\u0d41\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d35\u0d46\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d32\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d0e\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e \u0d2d\u0d57\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15 \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d43\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d33\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d1a\u0d47\u0d30\u0d32\u0d41\u0d15\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d15\u0d32\u0d32\u0d41\u0d15\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d27\u0d47\u0d2f\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d47\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d38\u0d42\u0d1a\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d46 \u0d06\u0d15\u0d7c\u0d37\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4b, \u0d1a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d46 \u0d35\u0d47\u0d30\u0d41\u0d15\u0d33\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d47\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d08\u0d7c\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d35\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4b \u0d06\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f \u0d36\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d7e (\u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d37\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d02) \u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d32\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d7c\u0d36\u0d28\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d2d\u0d57\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15 \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d28\u0d41\u0d38\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d."},"v-thread-6-samavaya":{title:"\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2a\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d12\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d2a\u0d36",narrative:"\u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d24\u0d41\u0d23\u0d3f \u0d28\u0d42\u0d32\u0d41\u0d15\u0d7e \u0d15\u0d4a\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d09\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d, \u0d06 \u0d24\u0d41\u0d23\u0d3f \u0d0e\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d? \u0d05\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d28\u0d42\u0d32\u0d41\u0d15\u0d33\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d15\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4a\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41. \u0d07\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d46 \u0d28\u0d3e\u0d02 \u0d38\u0d2e\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d7d (Inherence) \u0d0e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d47\u0d30\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41 - \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2a\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d2a\u0d30\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d30\u0d02 \u0d2c\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d27\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2a\u0d4a\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d27\u0d3f\u0d2d\u0d57\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15 \u0d2a\u0d36\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d4d. \u0d2a\u0d42\u0d7c\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d2d\u0d3e\u0d17\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d17\u0d41\u0d23\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d43\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d1a\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d33\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d2c\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d27\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d4d. \u0d38\u0d2e\u0d35\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d, \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2a\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d37\u0d23\u0d02 \u0d2a\u0d30\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d30\u0d2c\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d27\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24 \u0d2a\u0d4a\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d1f\u0d32\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d24\u0d15\u0d7c\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41."},"v-thread-7-visesa":{title:"\u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d38\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d24",narrative:"\u0d30\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d4d \u0d1c\u0d32 \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d7e \u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d38\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d12\u0d30\u0d47 \u0d17\u0d41\u0d23\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d, \u0d05\u0d35 \u0d0e\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d28\u0d46 \u0d12\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d30\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24 \u0d35\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d33\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41? \u0d07\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d15\u0d23\u0d3e\u0d26\u0d7b \u0d08 \u0d26\u0d7c\u0d36\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d2a\u0d47\u0d30\u0d4d \u0d28\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d2f \u0d06\u0d36\u0d2f\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d35\u0d24\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d: \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d02 (\u0d38\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d24). \u0d07\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d38\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d13\u0d30\u0d4b \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d13\u0d30\u0d4b \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2a\u0d30\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d30\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d35\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d1f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d08 \u0d05\u0d28\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d38\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d24\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d."},"v-thread-8-abhava":{title:"\u0d36\u0d42\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d46 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d02",narrative:"\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d32 \u0d35\u0d48\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d3f\u0d15 \u0d1a\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d15\u0d7c \u0d35\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d35\u0d15\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d0f\u0d34\u0d3e\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d7c\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d17\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d42\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d47\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41: \u0d05\u0d2d\u0d3e\u0d17\u0d02 (\u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2e). \u0d2e\u0d47\u0d36\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d41\u0d31\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d15\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d02 \u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d, \u0d2e\u0d47\u0d36 \u0d05\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d46 \u0d09\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d4d \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d32\u0d46 \u0d24\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d46 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d35\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41\u0d24\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d05\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d1f\u0d46 '\u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d' \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d46\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d47\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d24\u0d41\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d4d - \u0d2e\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d (\u0d09\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d2e\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d15\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d02), \u0d28\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d02 (\u0d2a\u0d4a\u0d1f\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d2f \u0d15\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d02), \u0d05\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d4b\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2e (\u0d15\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d02 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d24\u0d41\u0d23\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32), \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2e (\u0d2e\u0d41\u0d2f\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d15\u0d4a\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4d)."},"v-thread-9-manas":{title:"\u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d",narrative:"\u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d46 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d35\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d2f \u0d36\u0d43\u0d02\u0d16\u0d32\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d06\u0d27\u0d41\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d15 \u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d42\u0d31\u0d4b \u0d38\u0d2f\u0d7b\u0d38\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f, \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d24\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d2c\u0d4b\u0d27\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d46\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d48\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d02 \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7c\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d1a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41. \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d02 (\u0d35\u0d32\u0d41\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d) \u0d09\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d7d, \u0d12\u0d30\u0d47\u0d38\u0d2e\u0d2f\u0d02 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d07\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d41\u0d2d\u0d35\u0d02 \u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d47 \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d09\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4a\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d15\u0d34\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d42. \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d46 \u0d15\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d41\u0d15\u0d7e \u0d24\u0d41\u0d31\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d15\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d38\u0d02\u0d17\u0d40\u0d24\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d47\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d15\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d1a\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d34\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d \u0d15\u0d3e\u0d34\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d2a\u0d42\u0d7c\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d36\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d26\u0d4d\u0d27 \u0d15\u0d47\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d40\u0d15\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d15\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e \u0d06 \u0d38\u0d02\u0d17\u0d40\u0d24\u0d02 \u0d15\u0d47\u0d7e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4a\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d46\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d07\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41."},"v-thread-10-atman":{title:"\u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d18\u0d1f\u0d28",narrative:"\u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d \u0d35\u0d46\u0d31\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4a\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d4b\u0d38\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d31\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d, \u0d32\u0d4b\u0d15\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d05\u0d28\u0d41\u0d2d\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d06\u0d30\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d? \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d4d. \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d4d \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d0e\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d31\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1e\u0d41\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d7d, \u0d12\u0d31\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d4d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d7e \u0d05\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d2a\u0d42\u0d7c\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2c\u0d4b\u0d27\u0d30\u0d39\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d. \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d4d \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d36\u0d30\u0d40\u0d30\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d15\u0d42\u0d1f\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d47\u0d30\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d7e \u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d09\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d24\u0d3e\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3e\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d15 '\u0d17\u0d41\u0d23\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e' \u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d2c\u0d4b\u0d27\u0d02, \u0d06\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d39\u0d02, \u0d38\u0d41\u0d16\u0d02, \u0d26\u0d41\u0d03\u0d16\u0d02 \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d35."},"v-thread-11-adrista":{title:"\u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f \u0d36\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f",narrative:"\u0d36\u0d30\u0d40\u0d30\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e \u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d33\u0d46 \u0d1a\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4b, \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d1a\u0d47\u0d30\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d46 \u0d1a\u0d32\u0d3f\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d24\u0d4b \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d? \u0d05\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d37\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d - \u0d38\u0d1e\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d3f\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d2a\u0d41\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2a\u0d3e\u0d2a\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d41\u0d1f\u0d46 (\u0d15\u0d7c\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d02) \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d2f \u0d36\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f. \u0d13\u0d30\u0d4b \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d24\u0d3e\u0d7b \u0d05\u0d7c\u0d39\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d15\u0d43\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d30\u0d40\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d28\u0d41\u0d2d\u0d35\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d7e \u0d32\u0d2d\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d46\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d09\u0d31\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d41\u0d35\u0d30\u0d41\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d41\u0d15\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02, \u0d2a\u0d41\u0d28\u0d7c\u0d1c\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d46 \u0d05\u0d28\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d1a\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d46 \u0d28\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d15\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d1a\u0d46\u0d2f\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28 \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d06\u0d15\u0d7c\u0d37\u0d23\u0d36\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d23\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d37\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d02."},"v-thread-12-moksha":{title:"\u0d05\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d2e\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d35\u0d47\u0d7c\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d7d",narrative:"\u0d08 \u0d2f\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d24\u0d2f\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d12\u0d30\u0d3e\u0d7e \u0d0e\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d28\u0d46 \u0d30\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d37\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d46\u0d1f\u0d41\u0d02? \u0d2a\u0d26\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d46\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d1a\u0d4d\u0d1a\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d2a\u0d42\u0d7c\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d35\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d42\u0d1f\u0d46. \u0d2f\u0d25\u0d3e\u0d7c\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d25 \u0d05\u0d31\u0d3f\u0d35\u0d4d \u0d32\u0d2d\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d7e, \u0d0e\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e \u0d06\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d39\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d19\u0d33\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d35\u0d46\u0d31\u0d41\u0d2a\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d41\u0d15\u0d33\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d05\u0d35\u0d38\u0d3e\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41. \u0d06\u0d17\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d39\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d46\u0d19\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d2a\u0d41\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d37\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d02 \u0d09\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32. \u0d2a\u0d34\u0d2f \u0d05\u0d26\u0d43\u0d37\u0d4d\u0d1f\u0d02 \u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d3e\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d4d\u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d7e, \u0d05\u0d23\u0d41\u0d30\u0d42\u0d2a\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d41\u0d33\u0d4d\u0d33 \u0d2e\u0d28\u0d38\u0d4d\u0d38\u0d4d \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d3f\u0d7d \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d4d \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d35\u0d47\u0d7c\u0d2a\u0d3f\u0d30\u0d3f\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41. \u0d2e\u0d4b\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d37\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d3f\u0d7d, \u0d06\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d35\u0d4d \u0d36\u0d3e\u0d36\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d28\u0d3f\u0d7d\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41 - \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d3e\u0d7d \u0d2c\u0d4b\u0d27\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d26\u0d41\u0d03\u0d16\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d38\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4b\u0d37\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2a\u0d42\u0d7c\u0d23\u0d4d\u0d23\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d07\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d46. \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d15\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d4d \u0d2a\u0d4b\u0d32\u0d46 \u0d36\u0d41\u0d26\u0d4d\u0d27\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d28\u0d3f\u0d36\u0d2c\u0d4d\u0d26\u0d35\u0d41\u0d02 \u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d31\u0d4d\u0d31\u0d2e\u0d3f\u0d32\u0d4d\u0d32\u0d3e\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d24\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f \u0d12\u0d30\u0d41 \u0d26\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d35\u0d4d\u0d2f\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f, \u0d0e\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d46\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d47\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d38\u0d4d\u0d35\u0d24\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d24\u0d4d\u0d30\u0d2e\u0d3e\u0d2f\u0d3f \u0d05\u0d24\u0d4d \u0d05\u0d35\u0d36\u0d47\u0d37\u0d3f\u0d15\u0d4d\u0d15\u0d41\u0d28\u0d4d\u0d28\u0d41."}}},1142,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,_m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"mimamsaSutrasText",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return f}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"mimamsaSystem",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return h}});var t=r(d[0]),m=r(d[1]),s=r(d[2]),n=r(d[3]),l=r(d[4]),u=r(d[5]),c=r(d[6]);const o=s.mimamsaSutrasMl.map(t=>({id:t.id,translation:t.malayalamSutra,commentary:t.malayalamCommentary})),y=n.mimamsaConceptsEn.map(t=>({id:t.id,title:`${t.sanskrit} (${t.iast}) - ${t.english}`,summary:`**Category**: ${t.category}\n\n**Definition**: ${t.definition}\n\n**Significance**: ${t.significance}`,relatedVerseIds:[]})),f=(0,t.buildClassicalText)({id:'mimamsa-sutras',title:'M\u012bm\u0101\u1e43s\u0101 S\u016btras',transliteratedTitle:'M\u012bm\u0101\u1e43s\u0101 S\u016btras',author:'Jaimini',system:'mimamsa',sources:[{name:'\u015aabara Bh\u0101\u1e63ya',year:'c. 5th century CE',status:'integrated'}]},{en:m.mimamsaSutrasEn,ml:o},{en:y,ml:l.mimamsaConceptsMl}),h={id:'mimamsa',title:'P\u016brva M\u012bm\u0101\u1e43s\u0101',subtitle:'The Exegesis of Dharma',texts:[f],thread:(0,t.buildSystemThread)('mimamsa-sutras',{en:u.mimamsaThreadEn,ml:c.mimamsaThreadMl})}},1143,[1105,1144,1145,1146,1147,1148,1149]);
__d(function(a,e,t,i,n,s,r){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(s,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(s,"mimamsaSutrasEn",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});const o=[{id:"1.1.1",number:"I.1.1",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Ath\u0101to dharmajij\xf1\u0101s\u0101",conceptIds:["dharma-jijnasa"],translation:"Now therefore, the inquiry into Dharma (Duty/Righteousness).",commentary:"This foundational sutra sets the purpose of the entire Mimamsa system. 'Atha' (Now) implies a prerequisite: it assumes the student has already memorized the Vedas (Vedadhyayana) and now seeks to understand their meaning. 'Ata\u1e25' (Therefore) indicates the reason: because merely reciting the text does not automatically reveal its complex injunctions or the fruits of actions, a systematic philosophical inquiry (jij\xf1\u0101s\u0101) is absolutely necessary to correctly perform Dharma.",keyPoints:["The Prerequisite for Inquiry"]},{id:"1.1.2",number:"I.1.2",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Codan\u0101lak\u1e63a\u1e47o 'rtho dharma\u1e25",conceptIds:["codana","dharma"],translation:"Dharma is that objective (artha) which is indicated by Vedic injunction (codan\u0101).",commentary:"Jaimini defines Dharma strictly in terms of Vedic authority. 'Codan\u0101' refers to a prescriptive statement (an injunction like 'one who desires heaven should sacrifice'). 'Artha' means it must be conducive to the highest good (excluding harmful actions like black magic, even if mentioned in texts). Mimamsa radically asserts that Dharma cannot be known by logic, ethics, or mystical intuition; it is entirely defined by the prescriptive commands of the Veda.",keyPoints:["The Definition of Dharma"]},{id:"1.1.3",number:"I.1.3",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Tasya nimittapar\u012b\u1e63\u1e6di\u1e25",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"For that (Dharma), there must be an examination of its cause (the means of valid knowledge).",commentary:"Having established that Dharma rests on Vedic injunction, Jaimini now states that we must critically examine the 'nimitta' (the instruments or means of knowledge, known as Pramanas). Before accepting Vedic authority blindly, one must prove why other means of knowledge (like perception or inference) are incapable of revealing Dharma, and why the Veda alone is uniquely capable.",keyPoints:["The Examination of Pramanas"]},{id:"1.1.4",number:"I.1.4",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Satsamprayoge puru\u1e63asyendriy\u0101\u1e47\u0101\u1e43 buddhijanma tatpratyak\u1e63am animitta\u1e43 vidyam\u0101nopalambhanatv\u0101t",conceptIds:["pratyaksha"],translation:"Perception is that cognition which is born when there is contact of the senses of a person with the object; it is not the means (of knowing Dharma), because it apprehends only that which is existing (in the present).",commentary:"This sutra dismisses Pratyaksha (Sense Perception) as a tool for knowing Dharma. Perception requires the physical contact between a sense organ and a currently existing object. However, Dharma (e.g., the spiritual merit leading to heaven) is a future outcome (Apurva) that does not physically exist at the time of the action. Since perception cannot grasp the future or the non-physical, it is useless for discovering Dharma. This also refutes the yogic claim that ascetics can 'see' Dharma.",keyPoints:["The Incompetence of Perception"]},{id:"1.1.5",number:"I.1.5",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Autpattikastu \u015babdasy\u0101rthena sambandhas tasya j\xf1\u0101nam upade\u015bo 'vyatireka\u015b c\u0101rthe 'nupalabdhe tat pram\u0101\u1e47a\u1e43 b\u0101dar\u0101ya\u1e47asy\u0101napek\u1e63atv\u0101t",conceptIds:["autpattika","apurva"],translation:"But the connection of a word with its meaning is innate and eternal (autpattika). The knowledge of it is instruction (Veda); it is unerring regarding objects not perceived by other means; and it is a valid means of knowledge, according to Badarayana, because it is independent of other means.",commentary:"This is the central epistemological dogma of Mimamsa. 'Autpattika' means the relationship between a word (\u015aabda) and its meaning (Artha) was not created by humans or God; it is structural and eternal. Therefore, the Veda (which is pure 'Word') is 'Apaurusheya' (authorless). Because it is not authored by anyone, it is free from human defects (ignorance, deception). It does not need verification from perception, making it the supreme, independent (anapeksha) authority on the unseen realm.",keyPoints:["The Eternal Connection of Word and Meaning"]},{id:"1.1.6",number:"I.1.6",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Karmaike tatra dar\u015ban\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Some hold that sound is an action/product (karma), because it is seen to be produced (by human effort).",commentary:"Starting here, Jaimini presents the Purvapaksha (the opponent's view, primarily Nyaya philosophers) who argue that sound (and therefore the Veda) is not eternal. The first objection argues that sound is a product: we experience sound only when someone makes an effort to speak or strike an object. Whatever is produced must have a beginning and an end.",keyPoints:["Objection 1: Sound is a Product"]},{id:"1.1.7",number:"I.1.7",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Asth\u0101n\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because it does not persist (it is destroyed immediately after utterance).",commentary:"The second objection points out the ephemeral nature of sound. A spoken word ceases to exist the moment it is uttered. If sound were eternal (as Mimamsa claims), it would ring continuously in the air forever. Its transience implies it is a non-eternal entity.",keyPoints:["Objection 2: Sound is Transient"]},{id:"1.1.8",number:"I.1.8",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Karoti\u015babd\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because of the usage of the word 'makes' (karoti).",commentary:"The third objection relies on everyday linguistic usage. People say, 'He makes a sound' or 'Do not make a noise.' The use of the verb 'to make' (kr) implies that sound is manufactured or created, not something that pre-exists eternally.",keyPoints:["Objection 3: Linguistic Usage Implies Creation"]},{id:"1.1.9",number:"I.1.9",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Sattv\u0101ntare ca yaugapady\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): And because it is perceived simultaneously by different persons situated at different places.",commentary:"If the word 'Cow' is uttered by ten different people in ten different cities at the exact same time, ten different sounds are heard. The opponent argues that an eternal, singular entity cannot be in multiple places at once. The fact that sound has locality proves it is produced locally as multiple, distinct sounds.",keyPoints:["Objection 4: Simultaneous Perception Requires Plurality"]},{id:"1.1.10",number:"I.1.10",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Prak\u1e5btivik\u1e5btyau ca",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because of original (prakriti) and modified (vikriti) forms.",commentary:"In Sanskrit grammar (Sandhi), when 'Dadhi' (curd) and 'Atra' (here) combine, the 'i' changes into 'y', making 'Dadhyatra'. The opponent argues that if letters/sounds were eternal, they could not undergo modification or transformation into other letters. Mutability implies non-eternality.",keyPoints:["Objection 5: Sound Undergoes Modification"]},{id:"1.1.11",number:"I.1.11",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"V\u1e5bddhi\u015bca kart\u1e5bbh\u016bmn\u0101sya",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): And there is an increase (in its volume) owing to the multitude of makers.",commentary:"If one person shouts, the sound is small. If ten people shout the same word, the sound is massive. The opponent argues that an eternal, immutable entity cannot increase or decrease in size or volume. The fact that sound volume aggregates proves it is a physical, accumulative product.",keyPoints:["Objection 6: Increase in Volume"]},{id:"1.1.12",number:"I.1.12",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Sama\u1e43 tu tatra dar\u015banam",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But the perception is equal (in both theories); the effort merely manifests what is already there.",commentary:"Jaimini begins the Siddhanta (the established conclusion). He counters sutra 1.1.6 by introducing the distinction between 'Sabda' (the eternal word) and 'Nada' (the physical sound waves). Human effort does not *produce* the word; it merely *manifests* the eternally existing word, just as turning on a lamp does not create the objects in a room, but merely makes them visible.",keyPoints:["Refutation 1: Manifestation vs. Production"]},{id:"1.1.13",number:"I.1.13",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Sata\u1e25 paramadar\u015bana\u1e43 vi\u1e63ay\u0101n\u0101gam\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"The non-perception of the existing (sound) is due to the non-arrival of the object (the manifestor, to the ear).",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.7 (that sound is transient). Jaimini argues the sound does not 'die'. Instead, the physical medium (the air waves or Nada) that made the eternal word audible to the ear dissipates. The Word itself remains eternal in the ether, but without the physical acoustic manifestation, we simply cease to perceive it.",keyPoints:["Refutation 2: Non-Perception due to Lack of Stimulus"]},{id:"1.1.14",number:"I.1.14",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Prayogasy\u0101p\u016brvatv\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"The use of the word 'makes' refers to the application or utterance (prayoga), which is previously unaccomplished.",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.8. When people say 'he makes a sound,' they are using a colloquialism. What they are actually 'making' is the physical effort (the utterance or breath) required to manifest the sound. The effort is new and produced, but the sound it reveals is ancient and eternal.",keyPoints:["Refutation 3: The Verb 'To Make' Refers to Utterance"]},{id:"1.1.15",number:"I.1.15",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"\u0100dityavad yaugapadyam",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"The simultaneity of perception (in different places) is like that of the Sun.",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.9. How can one eternal word be heard in ten places at once? Shabara explains that just as there is only one eternal Sun, but it is seen simultaneously by millions of people across vast distances (or reflected in thousands of different pots of water), the singular eternal Word can be manifested and perceived by different people in different locations at the same time.",keyPoints:["Refutation 4: Simultaneity is like the Sun"]},{id:"1.1.16",number:"I.1.16",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Var\u1e47\u0101ntaram avik\u0101ra\u1e25",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"It is another letter, not a modification.",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.10. Regarding Sandhi (grammatical transformation): Mimamsa states that the letter 'i' does not physically melt or morph into the letter 'y'. Rather, the grammatical rule dictates a substitution. The eternal letter 'i' is dropped from usage in that instance, and the eternal letter 'y' is uttered in place. The letters themselves remain immutable.",keyPoints:["Refutation 5: Grammar Rules Substitute, Not Modify"]},{id:"1.1.17",number:"I.1.17",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"N\u0101dav\u1e5bddhi\u1e25 par\u0101m",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"The increase is of the noise (nada), which is distinct from the word.",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.11. When ten people shout, the eternal 'Word' does not grow larger. What grows is the 'Nada' (the acoustic air waves/noise acting as the medium). Just as a large mirror reflects a large face and a small mirror a small face without changing the face itself, the magnitude of the acoustic wave affects our perception, but the intrinsic Sabda remains unchanged.",keyPoints:["Refutation 6: Volume Belongs to the Medium, Not the Word"]},{id:"1.1.18",number:"I.1.18",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Nityastu sy\u0101d dar\u015banasya par\u0101rthatv\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Sound must be eternal, because its utterance is for the sake of another (to convey meaning).",commentary:"Having refuted the opponent, Jaimini provides logical proofs for eternality. The sole purpose of language is communication. If a word flashes into existence and instantly vanishes the moment it is born, it could never be grasped by the hearer, cross the space between them, and impart meaning. Communication relies on the fact that the word the speaker intends is the exact same, persisting word the listener receives.",keyPoints:["Positive Argument 1: Utterance is for Another"]},{id:"1.1.19",number:"I.1.19",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Sarvatra yaugapady\u0101t / Sarvatra pratyabhij\xf1\u0101n\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Because there is a sameness of recognition everywhere.",commentary:"When someone says 'Cow' today, and 'Cow' tomorrow, we do not think 'these are two similar words.' We inherently recognize it as the exact *same* word. This psychological phenomenon of recognition (Pratyabhijna) proves that the word is a singular, continuous, eternal entity that endures across time and space.",keyPoints:["Positive Argument 2: Identity Across Time"]},{id:"1.1.20",number:"I.1.20",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Sa\u1e45khy\u0101bh\u0101v\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Because of the absence of numbering (applied to the word itself).",commentary:"When a person says 'Cow' eight times, people naturally say, 'He uttered the word *Cow* eight times.' They do not say, 'He uttered eight *Cows* (eight different words).' The grammatical instinct to apply plural numbering to the *utterances* (the event) rather than the *word* (the object) proves that human consciousness inherently perceives the word as a singular, eternal unit.",keyPoints:["Positive Argument 3: Absence of Numbering"]},{id:"1.1.21",number:"I.1.21",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Anapek\u1e63atv\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Because there is no basis to assume a creator (of the relationship between word and meaning).",commentary:"Unlike human laws or modern programming languages, there is no historical record of a convention where humans gathered to assign meanings to all words. The relationship is organic and uncreated. If a convention creator existed, how did they communicate the convention without preexisting language? The regress forces the conclusion that word-meaning relationships are eternal and unauthored (Apaurusheya).",keyPoints:["Positive Argument 4: Absence of a Creator"]},{id:"1.1.22",number:"I.1.22",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Prakhy\u0101n\u0101c ca dharmasya / Anapek\u1e63atv\u0101t",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Because there is no conflict/sublation of this cognition.",commentary:"An illusion (like seeing a snake in a rope) is proven false when sublated by a later, clearer cognition (seeing it is actually a rope). The cognition generated by Vedic words regarding Dharma is never sublated by any subsequent empirical experience, because Dharma belongs to the unseen realm. It stands as absolute truth.",keyPoints:["Positive Argument 5: No Conflict in Cognition"]},{id:"1.1.23",number:"I.1.23",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"Li\u1e45gadar\u015ban\u0101c ca",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"And because the Vedic texts themselves show signs (corroborating its eternality).",commentary:"Jaimini concludes the debate by showing that the Veda itself contains statements pointing to its own eternal nature. For instance, mantras speak of the 'eternal speech' (V\u0101c\u0101 Vir\u016bpa Nityay\u0101). Having proved logically that sound is eternal, Jaimini now confidently points to the scripture itself as the final seal of validation.",keyPoints:["Positive Argument 6: Corroboration by Vedic Texts"]},{id:"1.1.24",number:"I.1.24",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Even if words are eternal, their arrangement (into sentences) must be a human product, because the meaning (of individual words) is not the cause of their arrangement. [Utpattau v\u0101 racan\u0101\u1e25 syur arthasy\u0101tannimittatv\u0101t]",commentary:"Having conceded that individual words and their meanings might be eternally connected, the opponent (Purvapaksha) now attacks the eternality of sentences (Vakya). They argue that a pile of bricks does not spontaneously form a house; an architect is needed. Similarly, individual words like 'bring', 'the', and 'cow' do not automatically arrange themselves into syntax. Sentences require a conscious, intelligent agent (Purusha) to string them together based on intent. Therefore, the Vedas, being collections of sentences, must have an author.",keyPoints:["Objection: Sentences Require an Author"]},{id:"1.1.25",number:"I.1.25",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Siddhanta): The synthesis (of these words) is for the sake of action, because the sentence meaning is based upon that. [Tadbh\u016bt\u0101n\u0101\u1e43 kriy\u0101rthena sam\u0101mn\u0101yo 'rthasya tannimittatv\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini counters that Vedic sentences are fundamentally different from descriptive human sentences. The Veda is primarily prescriptive (Vidhi); it commands action (Dharma/Kriya). In Vedic syntax, nouns denoting existing things naturally and logically coalesce around the central verb that commands the ritual action. The unified meaning of the sentence organically arises from this eternal syntactic relationship. It does not require an external human author to 'intend' the connection; the connection is inherent in the very structure of the imperative.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Vedic Sentences are Action-Driven"]},{id:"1.1.26",number:"I.1.26",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"In ordinary language, because of convention, the application (and composition) may be of recent origin. [Loke sanniyam\u0101t prayogasannikar\u1e63a\u1e25 sy\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini draws a strict line between 'Loka' (the mundane world) and the 'Veda'. He admits that in everyday human speech, sentences are indeed authored. Humans intentionally combine words to express subjective, temporary thoughts based on current conventions. However, this worldly rule cannot be superimposed onto the Veda. The Veda is the unauthored, beginningless blueprint of the universe, where the syntax itself is structurally inherent and utterly independent of any human speaker's mind.",keyPoints:["Worldly Speech vs. Vedic Speech"]},{id:"1.1.27",number:"I.1.27",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"(Opponent says): Some hold that the Vedas are of recent origin, because they are named after men. [Ved\u0101\u1e43\u015bcaike sannikar\u1e63a\u1e43 puru\u1e63\u0101khy\u0101\u1e25]",commentary:"This sutra begins the final topic of the chapter (Ved\u0101pauru\u1e63eyatv\u0101dhikara\u1e47a) defending the authorlessness of the Veda. The opponent argues that specific branches of the Veda are explicitly named after human sages\u2014such as the 'K\u0101\u1e6dhaka' (named after Sage Ka\u1e6dha) or the 'Taittir\u012bya'. If the Veda is eternal and authorless, why does it bear the copyright signatures of mortal men?",keyPoints:["Objection: The Veda Bears Human Names"]},{id:"1.1.28",number:"I.1.28",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"(Opponent says): And because transient things (historical persons and events) are seen in them. [Anityadar\u015ban\u0101c ca]",commentary:"The opponent deals a seemingly fatal blow: the Veda actually contains historical narratives. For instance, a passage reads, 'Babara Pr\u0101v\u0101ha\u1e47i desired...' The opponent argues that if the Veda mentions a specific human being who was born and later died, the text must necessarily have been composed *after* that person lived. Therefore, the Veda is a historical, non-eternal document.",keyPoints:["Objection: The Veda Mentions Historical Events"]},{id:"1.1.29",number:"I.1.29",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But the prior existence (eternality) of the word has already been established. [Ukta\u1e43 tu \u015babdap\u016brvatvam]",commentary:"Jaimini begins his defense by refusing to abandon established logic. He states that the absolute eternality of the word and its relationship to meaning has already been rigorously proven (in sutras 1.1.5 through 1.1.23). Since this philosophical foundation is unshakeable, any apparent contradictions (like names or historical references) must be resolved through proper hermeneutical interpretation, rather than throwing out the proven universal rule.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Eternality is Already Proven"]},{id:"1.1.30",number:"I.1.30",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"The names (attached to Vedic branches) are due to excellent exposition (by those teachers). [\u0100khy\u0101 pravacan\u0101t]",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.27, Jaimini explains the presence of sages' names. Sage Ka\u1e6dha did not *author* the K\u0101\u1e6dhaka branch of the Veda. Rather, he was an exceptionally dedicated teacher who flawlessly memorized, preserved, and instituted a continuous lineage for teaching that specific eternal section. The name honors the transmitter (Pravacana) for their peerless exposition, not a creator.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Names Denote Transmission, Not Authorship"]},{id:"1.1.31",number:"I.1.31",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"But (the apparent mention of historical names) is merely a similarity of sound. [Para\u1e43 tu \u015brutis\u0101m\u0101nyam\u0101tram]",commentary:"Refuting sutra 1.1.28, Jaimini provides a brilliant principle of Vedic interpretation. When the Veda seems to mention a historical king named 'Babara Pr\u0101v\u0101ha\u1e47i', it is an illusion caused by a similarity in sound. Using strict etymology, Mimamsa reveals that 'Babara' is an onomatopoeic word for the howling sound of the wind, and 'Pr\u0101v\u0101ha\u1e47i' means 'that which carries things forward'. The text is scientifically describing the eternal wind, not a mortal man. The confusion arises because humans later foolishly adopted these eternal Vedic words as personal names for their children.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Proper Names are Etymological Descriptions"]},{id:"1.1.32",number:"I.1.32",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 (Tarka-Pada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apaurusheya"],translation:"Or, the application (of these seemingly historical words) is meant (to praise) the action, due to their connection with the ritual. [K\u1e5bte v\u0101 viniyoga\u1e25 sy\u0101t karma\u1e47a\u1e25 sambandh\u0101t]",commentary:"This final sutra of Adhyaya 1, Pada 1 concludes the Tarka (polemical) section. Jaimini establishes that even when a Vedic passage looks like a historical narrative or a story (known as Arthavada), its true, structural purpose is not to teach history. Instead, these passages are intrinsically connected to a ritual injunction (Karma). Their sole purpose is to praise, encourage, or contextualize the righteous action that is being commanded. Every syllable of the Veda is eternal, and every syllable ultimately serves Dharma.",keyPoints:["Conclusion: All Statements Serve the Ritual Action"]},{id:"1.2.1",number:"I.2.1",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): Since the purpose of the Veda is action, those parts which do not serve that purpose (i.e., do not denote action) are useless; therefore, they are said to be unauthoritative. [\u0100mn\u0101yasya kriy\u0101rthatv\u0101d \u0101narthakyam atadarth\u0101n\u0101m tasm\u0101d anityam ucyate]",commentary:"The opponent (Purvapaksha) brilliantly uses Jaimini's own rule against him. In 1.1.25, Jaimini declared that the Veda is entirely action-oriented (commanding Dharma). But vast sections of the Veda are merely descriptive ('Arthavada' - stories, myths, descriptions of gods). Since these sentences do not contain a command (Vidhi) like 'one should sacrifice,' the opponent argues they are useless filler and hold no scriptural authority.",keyPoints:["Objection: Descriptive Texts are Useless"]},{id:"1.2.2",number:"I.2.2",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): And because we see them contradicting other scriptures and direct perception. [\u015a\u0101strad\u1e5b\u1e63\u1e6davirodh\u0101c ca]",commentary:"The opponent intensifies the attack. Not only do descriptive texts fail to command action, they often state absurdities that violate basic empirical perception. For example, a Vedic Arthavada says, 'The mind is a thief' or 'The sun is a pillar.' Another says, 'Fire is the remedy for cold.' The opponent argues that these are either scientifically false or state the blindingly obvious, making them unworthy of eternal scripture.",keyPoints:["Objection: Arthavadas Contradict Reality"]},{id:"1.2.3",number:"I.2.3",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): Similarly, because there is an absence of any fruit (result). [Tath\u0101 phal\u0101bh\u0101v\u0101t]",commentary:"Vedic injunctions always promise a result (e.g., 'Perform the Jyotishtoma sacrifice to attain heaven'). The opponent points out that simply reading a story about the gods\u2014like 'Rudra wept, and his tears became silver'\u2014does not promise any heavenly reward or spiritual merit. Without a fruit, the text is purposeless.",keyPoints:["Objection: Absence of a Result"]},{id:"1.2.4",number:"I.2.4",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they would be useless for any other independent purpose. [Any\u0101narthaky\u0101t]",commentary:"Even if one tries to creatively reinterpret these descriptive sentences to give them some independent philosophical or meditative purpose, the opponent argues they fail. They are structurally incapable of standing on their own as independent sources of Dharma.",keyPoints:["Objection: Inability to be Repurposed"]},{id:"1.2.5",number:"I.2.5",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they would be indistinguishable from actual injunctions (leading to confusion). [Avibh\u0101g\u0101d vidh\u012bn\u0101m]",commentary:"If we artificially force these descriptive texts to act as commands, it destroys the science of syntax. If 'Rudra wept' is treated with the same instructional weight as 'Sacrifice a goat,' a priest wouldn't know which sentences actually require him to perform a physical act. It blurs the line between a strict rule and a mere story.",keyPoints:["Objection: Confusion with True Injunctions"]},{id:"1.2.6",number:"I.2.6",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): And because they sometimes mention actions that involve transient (impossible) things. [Anityasa\u1e43yog\u0101t]",commentary:"The opponent's final blow: Arthavadas sometimes describe absurd or historically impossible actions. For instance, 'Prajapati cut out his own omentum (stomach lining) and offered it to the fire.' If this is an injunction, it commands the priest to perform an impossible, suicidal act. Therefore, Arthavadas must be rejected as unauthoritative.",keyPoints:["Objection: Mentioning Impossible Acts"]},{id:"1.2.7",number:"I.2.7",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But because they form a syntactical whole (a single sentence) with the injunctions, they must serve the purpose of praising those injunctions. [Vidhin\u0101 tvekav\u0101kyatv\u0101t stutyarthena vidh\u012bn\u0101\u1e43 syu\u1e25]",commentary:"This is one of Jaimini's most famous and revolutionary sutras. He introduces the principle of 'Ekav\u0101kyat\u0101' (Syntactical Unity). He agrees that Arthavadas (stories/descriptions) cannot stand alone. However, they are NOT standalone sentences; they are dependent clauses tied to a primary injunction (Vidhi). The injunction commands the action, and the Arthavada provides the 'Prarocan\u0101' (psychological motivation/praise). E.g., 'Vayu is the swiftest god' (Arthavada) + 'Sacrifice a white goat to Vayu' (Vidhi) forms one unified thought: 'Because Vayu is swift, sacrifice to him to get quick results.' Every descriptive word in the Veda serves to glorify and enforce an action.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Syntactical Unity (Ekav\u0101kyat\u0101)"]},{id:"1.2.8",number:"I.2.8",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"The traditional transmission is exactly the same for both. [Tulyam ca s\u0101mprad\u0101yikam]",commentary:"Jaimini appeals to the unbroken tradition of Vedic recitation (Adhyayana). For thousands of years, teachers have rigorously taught students to memorize the Arthavada passages with the exact same phonetic precision and reverence as the core Vidhi (injunction) passages. If the Arthavadas were truly useless filler, the ancient sages would have discarded them to save effort.",keyPoints:["Refutation: The Evidence of Tradition"]},{id:"1.2.9",number:"I.2.9",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"And the supposed inconsistency does not arise in practical application. [Apr\u0101pt\u0101 c\u0101nupapatti\u1e25 prayoge]",commentary:"Because the Arthavada is subservient to the Vidhi, it does not create a conflicting command. You do not actually try to 'weep like Rudra' or 'cut out your own stomach like Prajapati.' You simply perform the actual commanded ritual, inspired by the profound dedication or history illustrated in the story.",keyPoints:["Refutation: No Conflict in Application"]},{id:"1.2.10",number:"I.2.10",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"Where there is a contradiction (with perception), it is a figurative expression (Gu\u1e47av\u0101da) based on secondary qualities. [Gu\u1e47av\u0101das tu]",commentary:"Jaimini resolves the opponent's strongest point (from sutra 1.2.2). When a Vedic text says 'The mind is a thief' or 'The sun is a pillar,' it is not making a false scientific claim. It is using 'Lakshana' (metaphorical/secondary signification). This is called 'Gu\u1e47av\u0101da'. The mind is *like* a thief because it moves stealthily and conceals things. The sun is *like* a pillar because it appears to hold up the sky. Recognizing poetry and metaphor is essential to Vedic hermeneutics.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Figurative Expression (Gu\u1e47av\u0101da)"]},{id:"1.2.11",number:"I.2.11",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Regarding identifying one thing with another), it is based on the multiplicity of forms or similarity of function. [R\u016bpapr\u0101y\u0101t]",commentary:"The opponent objected to the text: 'The sacrificer is the Prastara (a bundle of sacred grass).' Jaimini explains this Gu\u1e47av\u0101da. The sacrificer is obviously a human, not grass. But just as the grass bundle acts as the physical foundation upon which the ritual implements rest, the human sacrificer is the financial and spiritual foundation of the entire sacrifice. The metaphor honors his foundational role.",keyPoints:["Resolving Metaphors: Similarity of Function"]},{id:"1.2.12",number:"I.2.12",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Regarding the praising of a small thing), it is based on extreme distance or hyperbole. [D\u016brasth\u0101n\u0101t]",commentary:"If an Arthavada says something seemingly absurd like 'These pebbles are the thunderbolts of Indra,' it is hyperbole to praise the ritual pebbles. By comparing mundane ritual objects to divine, distant weapons, the text elevates the psychological state of the priest, making him treat the pebbles with immense care and reverence.",keyPoints:["Resolving Metaphors: Praise through Exaggeration"]},{id:"1.2.13",number:"I.2.13",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"(Regarding the mention of faults), it is meant to praise the necessity of strict adherence, not to literally condemn. [Str\u012b-apar\u0101dh\u0101t...]",commentary:"Sometimes the Veda says seemingly negative things. This is the mechanism of 'Nind\u0101' (Condemnation). If the text condemns a particular action or entity, its true structural purpose is to passionately praise the *opposite* action (the actual injunction). Condemning the wrong path is just a rhetorical device to motivate the sacrificer to stay on the commanded path.",keyPoints:["Resolving Contradictions: Praising by Condemning the Opposite (Nind\u0101)"]},{id:"1.2.14",number:"I.2.14",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"As for the mention of historical impossibilities, they are intended to illustrate the supremacy of the act. [Tat-siddhi\u1e25...]",commentary:"Addressing the 'Prajapati cutting out his omentum' objection (from 1.2.6). Jaimini states this is not a history lesson or a literal command for self-harm. It is an extreme, mythological Arthavada meant to show that the ritual offering is so profoundly important that the Creator himself sacrificed his own essence to perform it. It demands total devotion from the human priest.",keyPoints:["Resolving Historical Absurdities"]},{id:"1.2.15",number:"I.2.15",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"And as for statements of known facts, they are merely for the sake of establishing the context. [Anuv\u0101do v\u0101...]",commentary:"The opponent complained that texts like 'Fire is the remedy for cold' are useless because even an idiot knows fire is warm. Jaimini explains that this is 'Anuv\u0101da' (Restatement). It is not teaching you that fire is warm; it is using a universally accepted fact to smoothly transition into commanding a specific ritual involving fire. It builds rhetorical momentum.",keyPoints:["Resolving Redundancy: Mentioning the Obvious"]},{id:"1.2.16",number:"I.2.16",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"Because of the connection with the context, it is not meaningless. [Prakara\u1e47asambandh\u0101t]",commentary:"A sentence must be judged by its context (Prakarana), not in isolation. A universally known fact stated in the Veda acts as the grounding premise in a logical or rhetorical argument, directly supporting the primary ritual injunction.",keyPoints:["The Mechanism of Restatement"]},{id:"1.2.17",number:"I.2.17",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"Therefore, all such passages are authoritative because they serve a purpose in relation to the injunction. [Tasm\u0101t sarvam pr\u0101m\u0101\u1e47yam...]",commentary:"Jaimini closes the defense. Since every single descriptive, mythological, or poetic passage can be syntactically linked (Ekavakyata) to an action (Vidhi)\u2014acting as praise, condemnation of the alternative, or contextual setup\u2014the entire Veda, without exception, is purposeful, authoritative, and infallible.",keyPoints:["General Conclusion on Arthav\u0101da"]},{id:"1.2.18",number:"I.2.18",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["arthavada"],translation:"Their primary function is the engendering of motivation (Prarocan\u0101) in the agent. [Prarocan\u0101rtham...]",commentary:"This marks the end of the Arthavada Adhikarana. Rituals are difficult, expensive, and time-consuming, and their results (heaven) are invisible. Human beings require psychological motivation to undertake hard tasks. Arthavadas provide this 'Prarocan\u0101' (enticement/glorification), making them an indispensable psychological engine for the mechanics of Dharma.",keyPoints:["Arthav\u0101da as Psychological Motivation"]},{id:"1.2.19",number:"I.2.19",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"(Opponent says): The restatement (Anuv\u0101da) of an action already enjoined elsewhere is entirely redundant and useless. [Vidhitve c\u0101narthakyam...]",commentary:"Having resolved Arthavada (stories), the opponent raises a new, highly technical objection regarding 'Anuv\u0101da' (Restatements). Sometimes the Veda commands a sacrifice in one chapter, and then casually mentions that same sacrifice again in a later chapter. The opponent argues that since the command was already given, the second mention is useless repetition (Punarukti).",keyPoints:["Objection: Redundancy in Mentioning the Act"]},{id:"1.2.20",number:"I.2.20",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But it is not redundant, because the restatement is for the sake of laying down a new accessory detail (Gu\u1e47a). [Gu\u1e47\u0101rtham v\u0101 puna\u1e25 \u015bruti\u1e25]",commentary:"Jaimini counters that the Veda never repeats itself without a purpose. If a known sacrifice is mentioned again, it is an 'Anuvada' serving as the subject for a new predicate. The text brings up the old ritual merely as a grammatical anchor to attach a brand new, previously unmentioned accessory\u2014like a specific type of ladel, a specific chant, or a secondary deity. The restatement is the bridge to the new instruction.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Restatement Serves a New Purpose"]},{id:"1.2.21",number:"I.2.21",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"Or, the restatement is for the sake of praising the action itself. [Stutyartha\u1e43 v\u0101]",commentary:"If no new accessory detail is found, Jaimini provides a fallback rule: the repetition is meant for emphasis and extreme praise. Just as in common language we might repeat a request ('Please, please do this') to show urgency, the Veda repeats an injunction to elevate its importance in the mind of the sacrificer.",keyPoints:["Restatement for the Sake of Praise"]},{id:"1.2.22",number:"I.2.22",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"Or, it may be for the purpose of indicating the sequential order of performance. [Kram\u0101rtha\u1e43 v\u0101]",commentary:"A third valid reason for Anuvada (repetition): Rituals involve hundreds of micro-steps. By restating an action that was enjoined generally elsewhere, the Veda locks that action into a highly specific chronological sequence within the current ritual context. 'Having done X (restatement), one does Y (new command).'",keyPoints:["Restatement to Indicate a Sequence"]},{id:"1.2.23",number:"I.2.23",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"Or, it is for the sake of restriction (Niyama). [Niyam\u0101rtha\u1e43 v\u0101]",commentary:"A fourth reason: Sometimes there are multiple possible ways to perform an action. The Veda might restate the action along with one specific method to 'restrict' (Niyama) the priest from choosing the other alternatives. The repetition narrows the scope of the original, broader command.",keyPoints:["Restatement to Restrict the Application"]},{id:"1.2.24",number:"I.2.24",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"Where a plurality is mentioned (regarding a singular act), it refers to the multiplicity of its accessory details or performers. [Anekasminn api...]",commentary:"If a text seems to speak of one specific ritual in the plural ('they perform the sacrifices'), it is not contradicting the singular nature of the event. The plural simply acknowledges the many priests involved, or the many sub-offerings made during that single event.",keyPoints:["Resolving Apparent Plurality"]},{id:"1.2.25",number:"I.2.25",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Arthavada)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["gunavada"],translation:"Thus, a restatement is always purposeful, serving either to enjoin a detail, indicate order, restrict application, or offer praise. [Tasm\u0101t sarva\u1e43 prayojanavat...]",commentary:"Jaimini concludes this sub-section. The hermeneutical rule is absolute: there is zero redundancy in the Veda. Every apparent repetition (Anuvada) is actually a highly efficient grammatical vehicle designed to introduce a new rule, enforce a timeline, or psychologically prepare the practitioner. The perfection of the Vedic text is maintained.",keyPoints:["Conclusion on Restatements"]},{id:"1.2.26",number:"I.2.26",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["hetu"],translation:"(Opponent says): A statement containing a reason ('because') must be intended as a logical justification, otherwise it would be meaningless. [Hetur v\u0101 sy\u0101d anarthakatv\u0101t]",commentary:"This begins the Hetumadnigada Adhikarana. The Veda sometimes gives a reason for a command, e.g., 'One should winnow with the winnowing-basket, because food is prepared with it.' The opponent argues this is not mere praise (Arthavada); it is a logical premise. If the Veda relies on worldly logic, then Dharma is not strictly based on the unauthored Word alone, but on human reasoning.",keyPoints:["Objection: Sentences Stating a Reason (Hetu)"]},{id:"1.2.27",number:"I.2.27",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["hetu"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But it is merely praise (Stuti), because the knowledge of Dharma is entirely dependent on the Word alone. [Stutis tu \u015babdap\u016brvatv\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini radically rejects the idea that the Veda justifies its commands using human logic. Dharma is known *only* through the Vedic injunction (1.1.2). If 'food is prepared with it' were the actual, binding reason to use the basket, one could substitute a cooking pot (which also prepares food) and still claim to be following the Veda. To prevent the destruction of the strict ritual rule, the 'reason' must be interpreted merely as a rhetorical device to praise the winnowing basket.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Reasons in the Veda are Mere Praise"]},{id:"1.2.28",number:"I.2.28",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["hetu"],translation:"It is called 'praise' because it serves the purpose of the primary object. [Arthe stutir ity\u0101cak\u1e63ate]",commentary:"The pseudo-reason exists solely to motivate the priest to use the specific object commanded. It has no independent logical weight.",keyPoints:["Worldly Reasons are Expendable"]},{id:"1.2.29",number:"I.2.29",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["hetu"],translation:"If the reason were established as binding, one could deviate from the specific direction (injunction). [Yadi ca hetur avati\u1e63\u1e6dheta nirde\u015b\u0101d vyatitheta]",commentary:"Jaimini points out the danger of relying on logic in ritual. If the underlying logic is the *cause* of the action, humans will inevitably find alternative, easier ways to fulfill that logic, thereby abandoning the specific prescribed Vedic action. Dharma would become a matter of human convenience.",keyPoints:["The Danger of Logical Substitution"]},{id:"1.2.30",number:"I.2.30",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["hetu"],translation:"And because we see inconsistencies (if taken logically). [Vyabhic\u0101r\u0101c ca]",commentary:"Many 'reasons' given in the Veda are empirically absurd. For example, 'Sacrifice with a loud voice, because the gods do not hear a soft voice.' Taken literally, this implies gods have poor hearing, which contradicts other texts. Therefore, these clauses are definitely not logical axioms; they are motivational poetry (Stuti).",keyPoints:["Vedic Reasons are Logically Inconsistent"]},{id:"1.2.31",number:"I.2.31",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Mantras are not meant to convey meaning, because their literal meaning contradicts established facts. [Tadarth\u0101cch\u0101strad\u1e5b\u1e63\u1e6divirodh\u0101t]",commentary:"This begins the famous Mantr\u0101dhikara\u1e47a, addressing the view of Kautsa (an ancient skeptic). The opponent argues that the mantras chanted during rituals (e.g., 'O Fire, you are the head of the sky') are just magical acoustic vibrations. They do not convey semantic meaning. If they did, they would be absurd, as fire does not literally have a head.",keyPoints:["Objection: Mantras are Meaningless Sounds"]},{id:"1.2.32",number:"I.2.32",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because their sentence structure is absolutely fixed. [V\u0101kyaniyam\u0101t]",commentary:"In ordinary language, if I want to say 'Bring the cow,' I can use different word orders or synonyms, and the meaning remains intact. In the Veda, altering a single syllable of a mantra ruins the ritual. The opponent argues this proves that the *sound-sequence* is the only thing that matters, not the meaning.",keyPoints:["Objection: The Syntax is Rigidly Fixed"]},{id:"1.2.33",number:"I.2.33",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they address things devoid of intelligence. [Buddhi\u015b\u016bnyatv\u0101t / Buddhi\u015b\u0101str\u0101t]",commentary:"Mantras often speak to grass, stones, or wooden posts ('O Herb, protect him!'). The opponent argues that since grass cannot hear or understand, the sentence cannot be meant for communication. It must be a meaningless chant.",keyPoints:["Objection: Addressing Inanimate Objects"]},{id:"1.2.34",number:"I.2.34",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because the meaning they supposedly convey is already stated elsewhere (in the Brahmana injunctions). [Avidyam\u0101navacan\u0101t / or similar variants indicating redundancy]",commentary:"The prose section (Brahmana) already says 'Cut the grass.' Then the priest recites a mantra saying 'I cut the grass.' If the mantra's purpose is to teach the meaning, it is utterly redundant. Therefore, its purpose must be purely phonetic/mystical.",keyPoints:["Objection: Redundancy of Meaning"]},{id:"1.2.35",number:"I.2.35",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they state things that are already directly perceived. [Acetan\u0101rthabandhan\u0101t / Pratyak\u1e63atv\u0101t]",commentary:"A mantra says, 'I take this stone in my hand.' The opponent points out that everyone can see the priest holding the stone. Language is meant to reveal the unknown; reciting what is obvious is senseless unless the sound itself is the goal.",keyPoints:["Objection: Stating the Blindingly Obvious"]},{id:"1.2.36",number:"I.2.36",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they contain contradictory or impossible meanings. [Arthaviprati\u1e63edh\u0101t]",commentary:"Mantras contain lines like 'Aditi is everything; she is the sky, the father, the son.' The opponent notes that one entity cannot simultaneously be the sky and a father. The semantic meaning is chaotic.",keyPoints:["Objection: Mentioning Impossible Things"]},{id:"1.2.37",number:"I.2.37",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because there are injunctions commanding mere recitation. [Sv\u0101dhy\u0101yavad vacan\u0101t]",commentary:"The Veda commands 'One should recite his Veda daily.' It does not command 'One should understand it.' The focus on phonetic memorization implies meaning is irrelevant.",keyPoints:["Objection: Recitation is Taught as an Independent Act"]},{id:"1.2.38",number:"I.2.38",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they contain totally unintelligible words. [Avij\xf1ey\u0101t]",commentary:"Mantras use archaic, obsolete words (like 'Amayak' or 'S\u1e5b\u1e47yeva') that ordinary people do not understand. A word that communicates nothing to the listener has no semantic purpose.",keyPoints:["Objection: Unintelligible Words"]},{id:"1.2.39",number:"I.2.39",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because they mention transient things, making them un-eternal. [Anityasa\u1e43yog\u0101t]",commentary:"The final purvapaksha argument: Mantras mention names of historical kings or specific rivers. If they denote transient historical facts, they cannot be eternal. Therefore, to save the Veda's eternality, we must conclude these are not names of real things, but just meaningless sound-combinations.",keyPoints:["Objection: Mentioning Historical Transient Things"]},{id:"1.2.40",number:"I.2.40",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But there is no difference in the meaning of words used here and in ordinary language. [Avi\u015bi\u1e63\u1e6das tu v\u0101ky\u0101rtha\u1e25]",commentary:"Jaimini begins the defense. He declares a fundamental hermeneutical rule: Vedic words and grammar operate exactly like ordinary human language. If 'cow' means an animal with a dewlap in the village, it means the exact same thing in the Veda. Mantras absolutely convey meaning (vivak\u1e63it\u0101rth\u0101\u1e25).",keyPoints:["Refutation: Sentences Have the Same Meaning as Ordinary Language"]},{id:"1.2.41",number:"I.2.41",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"As for contradictions with perception, they are resolved as figurative expressions. [Gu\u1e47av\u0101dena...]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.31. When a mantra says 'Fire has a head,' it is using metaphor (Gu\u1e47av\u0101da), just like we say 'the head of the table.' It is not meaningless; it is poetry.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Contradictions are Resolved Figuratively"]},{id:"1.2.42",number:"I.2.42",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"The restriction of word-sequence is for the sake of an unseen spiritual result (Adrishta). [Vidy\u0101vacanam niyam\u0101d ad\u1e5b\u1e63\u1e6dam / V\u0101kyaniyamo 'd\u1e5b\u1e63\u1e6d\u0101rtha\u1e25]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.32. Jaimini agrees that you cannot use synonyms in a mantra. But this doesn't mean the words lack meaning! The meaning is the primary function, but the *exact phonetics* are an added restriction (Niyama) required to generate the spiritual battery charge (Adrishta) of the ritual. Both meaning and sound matter.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Fixed Sequence is for Unseen Merit"]},{id:"1.2.43",number:"I.2.43",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"Addressing inanimate objects is done by attributing consciousness to them (personification) for the sake of praise. [Cetanavad upac\u0101r\u0101t]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.33. Speaking to grass or stones is a literary device to elevate their importance in the ritual context. It shows reverence for the instruments of Dharma.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Addressing Inanimate Objects is Personification"]},{id:"1.2.44",number:"I.2.44",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"Or, it refers to the presiding deity of that element. [Adhi\u1e63\u1e6dh\u0101n\u0101d v\u0101]",commentary:"Alternatively, when addressing a plant, the mantra is semantically directed not at the conscious deity governing that aspect of nature.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Addressing the Presiding Deity"]},{id:"1.2.45",number:"I.2.45",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"The restatement of what is already known is for the sake of recalling it at the time of performance. [Arthokti\u1e25 smara\u1e47\u0101rtham]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.34 & 35. The Brahmana rule tells you *what* to do beforehand. The Mantra is recited *while* doing it. Its semantic purpose is to lock the priest's mind onto the specific deity and material in real-time, preventing mental wandering. Stating the obvious is psychologically necessary for focus.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Repetition Aids Memory During Action"]},{id:"1.2.46",number:"I.2.46",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"Specific injunctions regarding mantras serve to restrict their application. [Niyam\u0101rtham v\u0101]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.35. If there are five different mantras about cutting grass, the injunction specifies *which* one to use for a particular sacrifice, creating order out of potential chaos.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Restricting the Choice of Mantras"]},{id:"1.2.47",number:"I.2.47",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"Impossible or contradictory statements are to be understood allegorically. [Upac\u0101r\u0101t...]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.36. 'Aditi is everything' is an allegory for the omnipresence of the divine principle. It is profound philosophy, not gibberish.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Impossible Descriptions are Allegorical"]},{id:"1.2.48",number:"I.2.48",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"The unintelligibility of words is the fault of the ignorant person, not the fault of the word. [Sato 'par\u0101dha\u1e25...]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.38. Jaimini brilliantly retorts: if a blind man cannot see a pillar, it is not the pillar's fault! The archaic words have strict etymological meanings known to grammarians (Nirukta). Just because an uneducated priest doesn't know the meaning doesn't mean the word lacks one.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Ignorance Does Not Make Words Meaningless"]},{id:"1.2.49",number:"I.2.49",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"Because the meanings of these obscure words are explained in the Nirukta and grammar. [Niruktagr\u0101m\u0101nyatv\u0101t]",commentary:"The meaning is objectively verifiable by consulting the auxiliary sciences of the Veda (Vedangas).",keyPoints:["Refutation: Meaning is Taught Elsewhere"]},{id:"1.2.50",number:"I.2.50",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"The injunction to recite implies comprehension, because learning is for the sake of application. [Prayog\u0101rthatv\u0101t]",commentary:"Answering 1.2.37. You do not just memorize a recipe to hum the sounds; you memorize it to cook. The Veda is learned by heart so that the student can later apply its meaning to perform Dharma.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Recitation Implies Understanding"]},{id:"1.2.51",number:"I.2.51",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"The mention of unreal things is meant for comparison. [Upam\u0101n\u0101rtham]",commentary:"When a mantra speaks of something fantastical, it is an Upamana (simile or metaphor) used to shed light on a real, abstract concept.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Unreal Things are for Comparison"]},{id:"1.2.52",number:"I.2.52",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"And we see that the one who knows the meaning attains the true result. [Vidv\u0101\u1e43sam ca...]",commentary:"While mere recitation might produce a minor unseen result, the highest spiritual efficacy of the sacrifice is achieved only when the priest fully comprehends the semantics of the mantra he is chanting.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Understanding Enhances the Result"]},{id:"1.2.53",number:"I.2.53",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 2 (Mantra)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["mantra"],translation:"Therefore, Mantras serve the primary purpose of recalling the elements connected with the action (through their meaning). [Tasm\u0101t smara\u1e47\u0101rtham...]",commentary:"Jaimini concludes the chapter. Mantras are not magical noise. They are deeply meaningful sentences whose structural purpose in the Veda is 'Smarana'\u2014to intellectually illuminate the deity, the material, and the action precisely at the moment the ritual requires them. This cements the entire Veda (both Injunctions and Mantras) as purposeful and meaningful.",keyPoints:["Conclusion on Mantras"]},{id:"1.3.1",number:"I.3.1",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Since Dharma is rooted solely in the Veda (\u015aabda), that which is not Veda (i.e., Smriti) should be disregarded. [Dharmasya \u015babdam\u016blatv\u0101d a\u015babdam anapek\u1e63ya\u1e43 sy\u0101t]",commentary:"Opening the Smriti Adhikarana. 'Smriti' refers to texts authored by humans (like the Laws of Manu). The opponent uses Jaimini's own rule from 1.1.2 against him: since only the eternal, unauthored Veda reveals Dharma, human-authored texts are fallible and therefore hold no authority in matters of Dharma.",keyPoints:["Objection: Smriti (Human Texts) is Unauthoritative"]},{id:"1.3.2",number:"I.3.2",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Siddhanta): But because the authors are the same (those who knew the Veda), the Smriti is a valid means of inference (to a lost Vedic text). [Api v\u0101 kart\u1e5bs\u0101m\u0101ny\u0101t pram\u0101\u1e47am anum\u0101na\u1e43 sy\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini defends Smriti through the logic of inference. The ancient sages (like Manu) who wrote the Smritis were the exact same people who flawlessly memorized the Vedas. If Manu wrote a rule not currently found in our existing Vedic branches, we logically infer that he read it in a branch of the Veda that is now lost to us. Therefore, Smriti has derivative authority; it is obeyed as if it were the Veda.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Smriti is Inferred Veda"]},{id:"1.3.3",number:"I.3.3",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"But when there is a contradiction (between Veda and Smriti), the Smriti is to be disregarded, because the inference (of a lost Vedic text) operates only when there is no such contradiction. [Virodhe tv anapek\u1e63ya\u1e43 sy\u0101d asati hy anum\u0101nam]",commentary:"This is the Virodh\u0101dhikara\u1e47a, establishing a hierarchy of texts. If a Smriti says 'Do X', but an existing Vedic text says 'Do not do X', the Smriti is immediately thrown out. You cannot infer a 'lost' Vedic text to support the Smriti when an actual, visible Vedic text already directly contradicts it.",keyPoints:["Contradiction between Veda and Smriti"]},{id:"1.3.4",number:"I.3.4",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"And (a Smriti is unauthoritative) when a worldly motive is perceived. [Hetudar\u015ban\u0101c ca]",commentary:"This is the Hetudar\u015ban\u0101dhikara\u1e47a. If a human text makes a rule like 'The sacrificer must give his entire property to the priest,' Mimamsa rejects it. Why? Because we can see a clear worldly motive (human greed). We only infer a lost Vedic text if there is absolutely no worldly/selfish reason for the human author to have invented the rule.",keyPoints:["Rejection of Smritis with Worldly Motives"]},{id:"1.3.5",number:"I.3.5",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"In the case of customs (of good people) which are not explicitly commanded, they are authoritative for the same reason (inference of Veda). [\u015ai\u1e63\u1e6d\u0101kop\u0101c c\u0101prati\u1e63iddham...]",commentary:"Moving from written texts to unwritten customs (Sadachara). If generations of highly ethical, Veda-knowing people (\u015ai\u1e63\u1e6das) practice a specific custom (like wearing a sacred thread in a certain way), it is authoritative. We infer that their unbroken tradition is based on a forgotten Vedic injunction.",keyPoints:["Authority of Sadachara (Customs of the Good)"]},{id:"1.3.6",number:"I.3.6",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"But it (a custom) is not authoritative if it contradicts an explicit Vedic text or a Smriti. [Te\u1e63v adar\u015ban\u0101virodh\u0101t sam\u0101nam]",commentary:"Just like Smriti, customs are at the bottom of the hierarchy. Veda > Smriti > Custom. If a local custom contradicts scripture, the custom is deemed corrupt and rejected.",keyPoints:["Limits of Customary Authority"]},{id:"1.3.7",number:"I.3.7",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"But where there is no such contradiction (with \u015aruti or Smriti), the custom is authoritative, and an underlying Vedic injunction is inferred from it. [Avidyam\u0101ne tu sm\u1e5btyanum\u0101na\u1e43 sy\u0101t]",commentary:"This immediately follows 1.3.6 (which rejected bad customs). It confirms that if a local custom of righteous people does not violate written laws, it holds the weight of Dharma.",keyPoints:["Customs Unopposed to Scripture"]},{id:"1.3.8",number:"I.3.8",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"The names applied to such customs by specific groups do not invalidate their general authority. [Te\u1e63v adar\u015ban\u0101d virodhasya sam\u0101nam]",commentary:"Even if a righteous custom is known by a regional slang name or practiced by a specific clan, its ethical/karmic validity is universal as long as it implies a lost Vedic text.",keyPoints:["Names Given to Customs"]},{id:"1.3.9",number:"I.3.9",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"However, if the custom is strictly localized and not recognized by the wider community of the righteous, its inferred authority is weaker. [Tadabh\u0101ve tu...]",commentary:"Jaimini refines the scope of custom. A universally practiced righteous custom strongly implies a lost Veda. A highly localized, obscure custom implies a much weaker connection, and cannot overrule broader Dharma.",keyPoints:["Local Limitations of Custom"]},{id:"1.3.10",number:"I.3.10",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"When words are used by Mlecchas (foreigners) in a specific sense not found in Aryan usage, that meaning should be accepted. [Mlecch\u0101prasiddh\u0101rthapr\u0101m\u0101\u1e47y\u0101dhikara\u1e47am / Coda\u1e47\u0101 hi...]",commentary:"This sutra deals with linguistics. Some words in the Veda (like 'pika' for cuckoo, or 't\u0101marasa' for lotus) were not used in standard Aryan Sanskrit, but were common in foreign/indigenous languages. Shabara (the commentator) explains that Jaimini accepts borrowing the meaning from foreigners, proving Mimamsa was highly pragmatic about linguistics when necessary to understand the text.",keyPoints:["Usage of Mleccha (Foreign) Words"]},{id:"1.3.11",number:"I.3.11",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["smriti"],translation:"(Opponent says): The Kalpa Sutras (manuals on ritual execution) should be considered as having independent authority like the Veda, because they directly teach the details of sacrifices. [Kalpas\u016btr\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - Prayogotpatti-\u015b\u0101stratv\u0101t...]",commentary:"The opponent suggests that texts like the Shrauta Sutras, which give step-by-step instructions for rituals, are so authoritative they should be treated as actual Veda (\u015aruti) rather than human memory (Smriti).",keyPoints:["Authority of the Kalpa Sutras: Objection"]},{id:"1.3.12",number:"I.3.12",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["smriti"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, they are merely Smriti (recollections), because their human authorship is known and remembered. [Na, sm\u1e5btitv\u0101t kart\u1e5bs\u0101m\u0101ny\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini firmly categorizes the Kalpa Sutras as human-authored texts. Because we know historically who wrote them (e.g., Baudhayana, Apastamba), they derive their authority purely from the Veda they summarize, not from themselves.",keyPoints:["Authority of the Kalpa Sutras: Refutation"]},{id:"1.3.13",number:"I.3.13",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["smriti"],translation:"Their validity rests entirely on the fact that their authors had direct knowledge of the Vedic texts they were organizing. [Tad-dar\u015ban\u0101t...]",commentary:"The Kalpa Sutras are brilliant organizational tools, but they do not reveal new Dharma. They merely systematize what is already in the \u015aruti.",keyPoints:["Kalpa Sutras Depend on Veda"]},{id:"1.3.14",number:"I.3.14",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["smriti"],translation:"They are composed merely to simplify the practical execution of the highly complex rites for the priests. [Prayog\u0101rtham...]",commentary:"The Veda is scattered and non-linear. The human authors of the Kalpa Sutras did the hard work of stringing the steps into a timeline. Their purpose is utility, not divine revelation.",keyPoints:["Practical Utility of Kalpa Sutras"]},{id:"1.3.15",number:"I.3.15",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Customs like the Hol\u0101k\u0101 (spring festival) are binding on all, because the inferred Vedic text would be general. [Anum\u0101nik\u012b\u1e63u \u015bruti\u1e63u sarvadharmatvam...]",commentary:"If a valid custom is observed only in Eastern India (like a specific spring festival), is it a duty only for Easterners, or for everyone? Jaimini rules that since the custom implies a lost Vedic injunction, and Vedic injunctions are universal (applying to all humanity/Aryans), the custom is theoretically binding on everyone, not just locals.",keyPoints:["Universal vs. Local Customs (Hol\u0101k\u0101)"]},{id:"1.3.16",number:"I.3.16",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"If a custom is completely devoid of Vedic basis and practiced by Mlecchas (foreigners), it cannot be considered Dharma. [Tadabh\u0101ve tu mlecch\u0101c\u0101ra\u1e25...]",commentary:"While Jaimini allowed borrowing foreign words (in 1.3.10) for linguistic clarity, he strictly forbids adopting foreign religious customs as Dharma, because they are structurally disconnected from the Vedic chain of inference.",keyPoints:["Application of Foreign/Mleccha Customs"]},{id:"1.3.17",number:"I.3.17",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"When a word is used in one sense by the Aryans and in a completely different sense by the Mlecchas, the Aryan usage is authoritative for Vedic interpretation. [\u0100ryamlecch\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - \u015aabd\u0101rthe tu virodhe...]",commentary:"Opening the section on semantic conflict. If the Veda uses the word 'Yava', and Aryans use it to mean 'barley' while foreigners use it to mean 'long-pepper', which definition applies to the ritual? Jaimini rules in favor of the Aryan definition.",keyPoints:["Conflicting Word Meanings (Arya vs. Mleccha)"]},{id:"1.3.18",number:"I.3.18",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"Because the Aryans are the ones explicitly recognized by the texts as the authoritative performers and custodians of the Vedic ritual. [Te\u1e63\u0101m eva adhik\u0101r\u0101t...]",commentary:"The logic is pragmatic: since the Aryans are the ones actually performing the specific Vedic sacrifices mandated by the texts, their native linguistic conventions regarding the materials used must be the intended ones.",keyPoints:["The Aryans as Custodians of Ritual"]},{id:"1.3.19",number:"I.3.19",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"Furthermore, the supplementary Vedic passages (V\u0101kya\u015be\u1e63a) confirm the Aryan meaning. [V\u0101kya\u015be\u1e63\u0101c ca...]",commentary:"Jaimini proves his point not just by cultural authority, but by textual analysis. He states that if you look at the surrounding descriptive sentences in the Veda, they physically describe the item in a way that matches the Aryan definition.",keyPoints:["Corroboration by Context"]},{id:"1.3.20",number:"I.3.20",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"For instance, the text describes 'Yava' as thriving in the spring when other plants wither; this perfectly applies to barley, not long-pepper. [Yav\u0101di\u1e63u...]",commentary:"Applying the rule from 1.3.19. The contextual clue 'thrives in spring' scientifically eliminates the Mleccha definition (long-pepper) and confirms the Aryan one (barley).",keyPoints:["Example: The Word 'Yava'"]},{id:"1.3.21",number:"I.3.21",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"Similarly, 'Var\u0101ha' means a boar, not a crow, based on contextual descriptions of it tearing up the earth. [Var\u0101he ca...]",commentary:"Another example of semantic conflict resolved by context. A crow does not tear up the earth with its snout; a boar does.",keyPoints:["Example: The Word 'Var\u0101ha'"]},{id:"1.3.22",number:"I.3.22",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"And 'Vetasa' means a cane/reed, not a blackberry (jambu), based on its strict association with water in the supplementary texts. [Vetase ca...]",commentary:"The third classic example. The Veda mentions Vetasa growing in water, which fits the cane/reed, solidifying the correct material to be used in the sacrifice.",keyPoints:["Example: The Word 'Vetasa'"]},{id:"1.3.23",number:"I.3.23",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sadachara"],translation:"Thus, contextual passages are the ultimate arbiter in resolving any ambiguity in terminology. [Tasm\u0101t pr\u0101m\u0101\u1e47yam...]",commentary:"Concluding the Adhikarana: linguistic disputes in scriptural interpretation must always be resolved by analyzing the internal evidence (context/Vakyasesha) of the scripture itself.",keyPoints:["Conclusion on Conflicting Meanings"]},{id:"1.3.24",number:"I.3.24",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apabhramsa"],translation:"Only correct words (as defined by grammar) produce the unseen result; corrupt words do not. [\u015aabdaprayog\u0101dhikara\u1e47am / S\u0101dhu\u015babdaprayoga\u1e25...]",commentary:"Ordinary people often use corrupt slang (Apabhramsa) instead of correct Sanskrit (e.g., saying 'g\u0101vi' instead of 'gau\u1e25' for cow). The opponent argues that since slang conveys the meaning, it is fine for rituals. Jaimini decrees that while slang conveys meaning in the marketplace, only grammatically perfect words generate the spiritual merit (Adrishta) required in a Vedic sacrifice.",keyPoints:["The Authority of Grammar (Vy\u0101kara\u1e47a)"]},{id:"1.3.25",number:"I.3.25",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apabhramsa"],translation:"Regarding corrupt words (like 'G\u0101vi' instead of 'Gau\u1e25'), they are not distinct, beginningless words, but arise from human inability to pronounce correctly. [Apabhra\u1e43\u015b\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - Te\u1e63\u0101m an\u0101dir iti cet... na, a\u015bakte\u1e25]",commentary:"Following 1.3.24 (which mandated grammatically correct words). The opponent asks: 'If people have been using slang for centuries, isn't slang also eternal?' Jaimini says no. Slang/corrupt words are born purely from laziness or phonetic failure by humans.",keyPoints:["Corrupt Words (Apabhra\u1e43\u015ba)"]},{id:"1.3.26",number:"I.3.26",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apabhramsa"],translation:"They are merely mispronunciations of the correct Sanskrit word, not independent synonyms possessing their own expressive power. [Na \u015babd\u0101ntaram...]",commentary:"If you mispronounce 'Cow' as 'Caw', you haven't invented a new word for cow; you've just broken the real word. Mimamsa insists on strict phonetic purity for the Veda.",keyPoints:["Corruptions are not Synonyms"]},{id:"1.3.27",number:"I.3.27",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apabhramsa"],translation:"They manage to convey meaning in everyday life only by indirectly reminding the listener of the original, correct word. [Tad-anum\u0101n\u0101t...]",commentary:"How does slang work if it has no real power? Jaimini uses psychology: when someone hears 'Gavi', their brain autocorrects it to 'Gauh' (the eternal word), and *that* eternal word delivers the meaning.",keyPoints:["How Corrupt Words Convey Meaning"]},{id:"1.3.28",number:"I.3.28",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apabhramsa"],translation:"Therefore, corrupt words do not have an innate, eternal connection to the object (Autpattika). [Tasm\u0101t na \u015b\u0101bdam...]",commentary:"Because they rely on a psychological middle-step (reminding you of the real word), their connection to meaning is artificial and broken, not eternal.",keyPoints:["Lack of Independent Expressiveness"]},{id:"1.3.29",number:"I.3.29",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Custom)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apabhramsa"],translation:"Consequently, they cannot be used in Vedic rituals, as only the use of the eternally correct words (S\u0101dhu \u015babda) generates the unseen merit. [Evam ca sati...]",commentary:"Concluding the Adhikarana. While slang is fine for buying vegetables, bringing it into a Vedic sacrifice destroys the precise sonic geometry required to generate Apurva (spiritual merit).",keyPoints:["Rejection of Corrupt Words in Ritual"]},{id:"1.3.30",number:"I.3.30",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): A word denotes a specific individual object (Vyakti), because actions can only be performed on individuals. [Prayogacodan\u0101bh\u0101v\u0101d arthaikatvam avibh\u0101g\u0101t]",commentary:"This opens the profound \u0100k\u1e5btyadhikara\u1e47a (The Universal vs. Particular debate). The opponent (Vyaktivadin) argues that the word 'cow' refers to a specific, physical cow. Their logic: The Veda commands 'Sacrifice a cow.' You can only physically tie up, cut, and sacrifice a flesh-and-blood individual. You cannot tie up the abstract concept of 'cow-ness'. Therefore, words denote particulars.",keyPoints:["Objection: Words Denote Particular Individuals (Vyakti)"]},{id:"1.3.31",number:"I.3.31",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): Because the texts describe physical characteristics (like being old or hornless). [Adravyashabd\u0101t...]",commentary:"The Veda says 'Buy the soma with a red, one-year-old cow.' The opponent argues that an abstract universal ('cow-ness') doesn't have a color or an age. Only a specific individual does. Thus, the word 'cow' must mean the individual.",keyPoints:["Objection: Description of Physical Traits"]},{id:"1.3.32",number:"I.3.32",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Opponent says): And because numbers are applied to them. [Sa\u1e45khy\u0101bh\u0101v\u0101c ca...]",commentary:"The Veda says 'He gives six cows.' A universal class is singular (there is only one 'cow-ness'). You cannot have six 'cow-nesses'. Therefore, words denote plural individuals.",keyPoints:["Objection: Plurality and Numbers"]},{id:"1.3.33",number:"I.3.33",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"(Siddhanta): Rather, the word denotes the Class/Universal (\u0100k\u1e5bti), because the action is commanded with reference to the class. [\u0100k\u1e5btis tu kriy\u0101rthatv\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini delivers the Mimamsa masterstroke. The word 'cow' denotes the eternal, abstract Universal (\u0100k\u1e5bti/J\u0101ti). Why? If the word 'cow' meant one specific cow (Cow A), then the rule would not apply to Cow B in the next town. To command a ritual that any priest anywhere can follow, the Veda must use the word to mean the *class* of cows. The priest understands the eternal rule via the Universal, and then *incidentally* brings a physical individual to execute the act. The Universal is primary; the individual is implied by necessity.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Words Denote Universals (\u0100k\u1e5bti)"]},{id:"1.3.34",number:"I.3.34",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"And because only the Universal is eternal. [Nityatv\u0101t / or related phrasing from Sabara]",commentary:"This is the ultimate theological reason. In sutra 1.1.5, Jaimini proved that the relationship between a word and its meaning is eternal (Autpattika). Individual cows die. If the word 'cow' meant a physical cow, the meaning would perish when the cow dies, destroying the eternal connection. But 'cow-ness' is eternal. It exists before a calf is born and after it dies. Therefore, words must denote eternal Universals to preserve the eternality of language.",keyPoints:["Preserving the Eternality of the Word"]},{id:"1.3.35",number:"I.3.35",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 3 (Smriti & Vyakarana)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"The application of numbers and physical traits refers to the individuals that are invariably connected to the universal. [Gu\u1e47asa\u1e43yog\u0101t...]",commentary:"Addressing the opponent's objections from 1.3.31 & 32. Jaimini explains that while the word 'cow' *denotes* the universal, the universal can only physically manifest inside individuals. Therefore, when the Veda says 'six cows' or 'red cow', the grammar seamlessly applies the number and color to the *implied* individuals acting as the substrate for the eternal universal. The semantic denotation remains firmly with the Akriti.",keyPoints:["Resolution of Plurality and Traits"]},{id:"1.4.1",number:"I.4.1",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"(Opponent says): The word 'Udbhid' (in the text 'Udbhida yajeta') should be understood as enjoining a material accessory, because it is grammatically an instrumental means.",commentary:"This opens the N\u0101madhey\u0101dhikara\u1e47a (The Section on Names). The Veda says 'One desiring cattle should sacrifice with the Udbhid.' The opponent argues that 'Udbhid' literally means 'that which digs up' (like a shovel or a sprout). Since it is in the instrumental case ('with the Udbhid'), it must be commanding the priest to use a specific physical tool or plant as an accessory (Gu\u1e47a) in the sacrifice, rather than naming the sacrifice itself.",keyPoints:["Objection: Words like 'Udbhid' Enjoin Accessories"]},{id:"1.4.2",number:"I.4.2",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"(Siddhanta): Rather, it is the name of the sacrifice itself, to avoid the fault of secondary implication (Matvarthalak\u1e63a\u1e47\u0101).",commentary:"Jaimini establishes the first ground for identifying a Namadheya. If 'Udbhid' were an accessory, the sentence would have to mean 'He should achieve his desire by means of a sacrifice *possessing* the Udbhid.' This forces us to assume a possessive suffix (matvartha) to the word, changing its direct meaning. Mimamsa logic forbids distorting the direct meaning of a word if it can be avoided. Therefore, 'Udbhid' is simply the proper name of the sacrifice: 'He should achieve his desire by means of the Udbhid sacrifice.' Proper names serve a structural purpose in the Veda by specifically identifying rites.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Udbhid is a Proper Name (N\u0101madheya)"]},{id:"1.4.3",number:"I.4.3",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"Similarly, the word 'Citra' is a name (not an accessory), because otherwise there would be a syntactical split (V\u0101kyabheda).",commentary:"This is the Citr\u0101dhikara\u1e47a. The text says, 'One desiring offspring should sacrifice with the Citra.' The opponent claims 'Citra' means 'variegated/spotted' and enjoins a spotted animal. Jaimini refutes this. The text is already enjoining a sacrifice for a specific result (offspring). If it simultaneously enjoined a specific accessory (a spotted female animal), the sentence would be doing two distinct things at once: commanding the act AND commanding the accessory. This commits the severe logical fallacy of 'V\u0101kyabheda' (syntactical split). To keep the sentence unified, 'Citra' must be read merely as the title of the sacrifice.",keyPoints:["The Name 'Citra' and Syntactical Split"]},{id:"1.4.4",number:"I.4.4",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"A word like 'Agnihotra' is a name because the accessory (the deity) is already established by another text (Tatprakhy\u0101tva).",commentary:"This is the Agnihotr\u0101dhikara\u1e47a. The text says 'One should offer the Agnihotra.' Does this mean 'One should make an offering to the deity Agni'? No. Another text already explicitly says 'He offers to Agni.' Since the deity is already supplied elsewhere, enjoining it again here would be entirely redundant. Therefore, the word 'Agnihotra' does not enjoin the deity; it is merely the proper name applied to the entire ritual.",keyPoints:["The Name 'Agnihotra' (Tatprakhy\u0101tva)"]},{id:"1.4.5",number:"I.4.5",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"Words like 'Syena' (Hawk) are names because of a specific descriptive reference (Tadvyapade\u015ba) comparing the sacrifice to the object.",commentary:"This is the Syen\u0101dhikara\u1e47a. The text says, 'One desiring to destroy his enemy should sacrifice with the Syena.' Does this mean the priest should sacrifice a literal hawk? No. A subsequent Arthavada passage says, 'Just as a hawk swoops down and seizes its prey, so does this sacrifice swoop down and destroy the enemy.' Because the Veda uses a simile comparing the ritual to a hawk, the word 'Syena' in the main injunction must be the title of the ritual, not a command to kill a bird.",keyPoints:["The Name 'Syena' (Tadvyapade\u015ba)"]},{id:"1.4.6",number:"I.4.6",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"(Opponent says): The word 'V\u0101japeya' denotes an accessory material (a drink of strength/gruel), because of its etymology. [V\u0101japey\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - Gu\u1e47acodana...]",commentary:"The opponent argues that 'Vajapeya' literally translates to 'the drinking of strength/gruel'. Therefore, it commands the priest to use gruel as a material accessory.",keyPoints:["The V\u0101japeya Sacrifice: Objection"]},{id:"1.4.7",number:"I.4.7",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, it is a proper name, because the material (Soma) is already enjoined elsewhere for this specific rite. [Na, n\u0101madheya\u1e43...]",commentary:"Since the V\u0101japeya is actually a Soma-sacrifice, the liquid being offered is Soma, not gruel. Therefore, 'V\u0101japeya' is just a grand, poetic proper name for the sacrifice itself, avoiding a syntactical contradiction.",keyPoints:["The V\u0101japeya Sacrifice: Refutation"]},{id:"1.4.8",number:"I.4.8",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Names & Words)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["namadheya"],translation:"The opponent's etymological argument is weak because treating it as an accessory would require implying a possessive suffix (Matvartha-lak\u1e63a\u1e47\u0101), which is a logical flaw. [Matvarthalak\u1e63a\u1e47\u0101-do\u1e63\u0101t...]",commentary:"If Vajapeya meant gruel, the sentence 'He sacrifices with the Vajapeya' would literally mean 'He sacrifices *by means of a sacrifice possessing* gruel.' Mimamsa logic forbids twisting words with implied suffixes when treating it as a simple Proper Name works perfectly.",keyPoints:["Flaw of Assuming an Accessory"]},{id:"1.4.9",number:"I.4.9",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["akriti"],translation:"A word like 'Barhis' denotes the specific genus (a type of grass), not a generic functional role (anything used to strew).",commentary:"This begins the section on linguistic denotation (Barhiradhikarana). In rituals, 'Barhis' (Kusha grass) is used to make a seat. The opponent argues that any material used to make the seat should be called 'Barhis' based on its functional role (Yaugika). Jaimini refutes this: nouns in the Veda denote their established, conventional class (Rudi/Jati). 'Barhis' strictly means Kusha grass. Even if a priest uses different grass for the seat in a pinch, the mantras containing the word 'Barhis' cannot be arbitrarily applied to it.",keyPoints:["Words Denoting Classes vs. Functional Roles"]},{id:"1.4.10",number:"I.4.10",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["akriti"],translation:"When a class-name (like 'Barhis') is used in the plural, it refers to the multiple individual objects manifesting that class in the ritual. [Bahutva\u1e43 ca...]",commentary:"A follow up to 1.4.9. The opponent asked how a singular class/universal can be pluralized. Jaimini explains that the plural suffix grammatically attaches to the physical individuals, while the core word still denotes the universal class.",keyPoints:["Usage of Plurality in Class Names"]},{id:"1.4.11",number:"I.4.11",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Names & Words)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["akriti"],translation:"The plural number is not contradictory to the universal class, because the class requires individuals to be physically grasped. [Vyakt\u012bn\u0101m eva...]",commentary:"You cannot physically hold 'grass-ness'. You must hold multiple blades of grass. The grammar reflects the physical reality of the ritual execution.",keyPoints:["Plurality is Not Contradictory"]},{id:"1.4.12",number:"I.4.12",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Names & Words)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["akriti"],translation:"The ritual command applies to the individuals manifesting the class, because action is impossible on an abstract universal. [Kriy\u0101 hi vyakti\u1e63u...]",commentary:"Solidifying the Mimamsa balance between ideal and real. The eternal word means the Universal, but the human priest's knife cuts the Individual.",keyPoints:["Action is Performed on Individuals"]},{id:"1.4.13",number:"I.4.13",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["yaugika"],translation:"But words like 'Prok\u1e63a\u1e47\u012b' (sprinkling water) are used in their functional/derivative sense, because they are intrinsically connected to the action.",commentary:"Unlike 'Barhis' (which is a fixed noun), words formed directly from verb roots, like 'Prokshani' (from pra-uksh, to sprinkle), denote anything that fulfills that functional role in the ritual. If the ritual requires sprinkling water, that specific water becomes the 'Prokshani' by virtue of its action. Here, Mimamsa establishes the boundary between conventional nouns (Rudi) and functionally derived words (Yaugika).",keyPoints:["Derivative vs. Conventional Meaning (Prok\u1e63a\u1e47\u012b)"]},{id:"1.4.14",number:"I.4.14",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Names & Words)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["yaugika"],translation:"When a functionally derived word (like Prok\u1e63a\u1e47\u012b) is used, it applies strictly based on the action being performed. [Yaugika-\u015babd\u0101n\u0101m...]",commentary:"Expanding on 1.4.13. 'Prokshani' means 'that which is sprinkled'. Water is only 'Prokshani' at the exact moment the priest is using it to sprinkle. Before and after, it is just water.",keyPoints:["Limits of Derivative Words (Yaugika)"]},{id:"1.4.15",number:"I.4.15",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Names & Words)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["yaugika"],translation:"Once the action is completed, the derivative name ceases to apply, completely differentiating them from permanent proper nouns. [Karma-vyapagam\u0101t...]",commentary:"A proper noun like 'Jyotishtoma' applies permanently to that ritual. A functional noun like 'sprinkling-water' is temporary. This prevents priests from treating used ritual water as permanently sacred.",keyPoints:["Derivative vs. Proper Nouns"]},{id:"1.4.16",number:"I.4.16",section:"Adhyaya 1, Pada 4 (Namadheya)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["yaugika"],translation:"Therefore, all Vedic words\u2014whether Injunctions, Explanatory passages, Mantras, or Names\u2014possess absolute authority in revealing Dharma.",commentary:"This concludes the entirety of Book 1 (Adhyaya 1) of the Purva Mimamsa Sutras. Jaimini has systematically defended the Veda against all epistemological and structural attacks. He proved that Dharma is known only through the unauthored Word (Pada 1), that stories and myths are authoritative as praise (Pada 2), that Smriti and customs are valid as inferred Veda (Pada 3), and that even proper names and obscure nouns serve an exact, logical purpose in the ritual syntax (Pada 4). The foundation is now set to begin analyzing the actual mechanics and rules of the rituals in Book 2.",keyPoints:["Conclusion of Adhyaya 1: The Authority of the Word"]},{id:"2.1.1",number:"II.1.1",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Words denoting 'becoming' (verbs) denote action (Kriya), because the knowledge of an action to be accomplished depends upon them. [Bh\u0101v\u0101rth\u0101\u1e25 karma\u015babd\u0101s tebhya\u1e25 kriy\u0101 prat\u012byetai\u1e63ap\u016brvatv\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini opens Book 2 by defining the core engine of Vedic syntax: the verb. In Mimamsa, a verb does not just describe a state of being; it expresses 'Bh\u0101van\u0101' (productive energy/action). When the Veda says 'Yajeta' (One should sacrifice), the verb is the beating heart of the sentence. All other words (nouns, adjectives, deities, materials) are subordinate elements drawn in by the verb to answer three questions: What is to be done? By what means? And how? The verb is the sole instigator of Dharma.",keyPoints:["The Definition of Verbs (Bh\u0101van\u0101)"]},{id:"2.1.2",number:"II.1.2",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Nouns, on the other hand, denote completed (existing) entities, and are therefore subordinate to the action. [Dravy\u0101\u1e47\u0101\u1e43 tu kriy\u0101rth\u0101n\u0101m...]",commentary:"A noun (like 'rice', 'cow', or 'fire') refers to a static, already-existing entity (Siddha). You cannot 'do' a noun. Therefore, nouns in the Veda do not command Dharma. They only serve a structural purpose by acting as the instruments or materials required to accomplish the primary action commanded by the verb (Sadhya).",keyPoints:["Nouns are Subordinate to Verbs"]},{id:"2.1.3",number:"II.1.3",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"Because of this distinction, a verb cannot be construed as enjoining a substance. [Tanni\u015bcayas tena...]",commentary:"This reinforces the boundary between action and material. A verb commands the energetic performance of a rite. It cannot simultaneously be interpreted as commanding the creation of a physical substance, otherwise syntactical chaos (Vakyabheda) would ensue.",keyPoints:["Verbs Do Not Denote Nouns"]},{id:"2.1.4",number:"II.1.4",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["bhavana"],translation:"If a verb were construed as enjoining both the action and the substance simultaneously, there would be a syntactical split, which is a flaw. [Vyavatisthate v\u0101kyabhed\u0101t]",commentary:"Fleshing out the logic between 2.1.3 and 2.1.5. A single Vedic sentence can only issue one primary command. If 'Sacrifice with curds' meant 'Perform a sacrifice AND create curds', it commands two things at once, breaking the law of Ekavakyata (syntactical unity).",keyPoints:["Syntactical Split (V\u0101kyabheda)"]},{id:"2.1.5",number:"II.1.5",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apurva"],translation:"There is an Unseen Force (Ap\u016brva) generated by the injunction, because the action itself is transient and the result (heaven) is future. [Codan\u0101 punar \u0101rambha\u1e25...]",commentary:"This is one of the most important concepts in Indian philosophy: The Ap\u016brv\u0101dhikara\u1e47a. The opponent asks: 'A sacrifice is a physical act that ends the moment the fire goes out. How can a dead, vanished action produce Heaven twenty years later when the sacrificer dies? A non-existent cause cannot produce an effect.' Jaimini answers: The physical action perishes, but it leaves behind an invisible, transcendent potency called 'Ap\u016brva' (literally 'that which did not exist before') attached to the soul of the performer. This Apurva acts as a metaphysical battery, storing the energy of the ritual and eventually discharging it as the promised fruit (Heaven) at the appointed time.",keyPoints:["The Unseen Force (Ap\u016brva)"]},{id:"2.1.6",number:"II.1.6",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apurva"],translation:"Actions are of two kinds: Primary (Pradh\u0101na) and Secondary/Subordinate (Gu\u1e47a). [T\u0101ni dvaidh\u0101 gu\u1e47apradh\u0101nabh\u016bt\u0101ni...]",commentary:"Having established that rituals generate Apurva, Jaimini now categorizes the actions within a ritual. A single sacrifice involves hundreds of micro-actions (sweeping the floor, cutting grass, pouring ghee). Are they all equal? Do they all generate the main Apurva? No. They must be hierarchically divided.",keyPoints:["Division of Actions: Primary and Secondary"]},{id:"2.1.7",number:"II.1.7",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apurva"],translation:"Those actions which subserve a physical substance are secondary (Gu\u1e47akarma), because they are for the sake of something else. [Yair dravya\u1e43 cik\u012br\u1e63yate gu\u1e47as tatra prat\u012byeta...]",commentary:"If an action's direct physical result is a change in a material object, it is a 'Gu\u1e47akarma' (Subordinate Action). Examples: washing the rice, pounding the grain, or tying up the animal. These acts do not generate the heavenly Apurva; their only purpose is to prepare the physical materials (the rice) so that the materials can be used in the main sacrifice.",keyPoints:["Definition of Secondary Action (Gu\u1e47akarma)"]},{id:"2.1.8",number:"II.1.8",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["apurva"],translation:"Those actions which do not subserve a substance are Primary (Pradh\u0101nakarma); the substance is merely an instrument for them. [Yais tu dravya\u1e43 na cik\u012br\u1e63yate t\u0101ni pradh\u0101nabh\u016bt\u0101ni...]",commentary:"Primary actions are those where the material is completely consumed or given away, not prepared. For example, the actual pouring of the ghee into the fire with the utterance of 'Svaha'. The pouring does not 'improve' the ghee; it destroys it. The physical action has no worldly productive purpose. Therefore, its true purpose must be transcendent: generating the Apurva that leads to heaven. This is the Pradh\u0101na.",keyPoints:["Definition of Primary Action (Pradh\u0101nakarma)"]},{id:"2.1.9",number:"II.1.9",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["pradhana-karma"],translation:"The primary Apurva arises from the principal action, while minor (subordinate) apurvas may arise from the preparatory acts to assist the main one. [Dharmam\u0101tre tu karmasy\u0101d anirv\u1e5btte\u1e25...]",commentary:"The entire ritual is a mechanism. The primary action (Pradhana) creates the 'Maha-Apurva' (the grand unseen force). The secondary actions (like chanting a specific preparatory mantra) create tiny, temporary 'Utpatti-Apurvas' that conceptually link together to support the generation of the Maha-Apurva. Every command in the Veda is thus logically accounted for in the chain of causality.",keyPoints:["The Scope of the Primary Action"]},{id:"2.1.10",number:"II.1.10",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["pradhana-karma"],translation:"If the action is primary, the material exists solely for its sake and takes on the unseen result (Utpatti-Apurva) of being utilized. [Dravya\u1e43 ca pradh\u0101nasy\u0101rthe...]",commentary:"Bridging 2.1.9 and 2.1.13. Materials like rice or ghee have no spiritual value on their own. Their entire purpose is to be subordinated to the verb (the energetic action). Only through the action do they contribute to the final Apurva.",keyPoints:["Purpose of Subordinate Materials"]},{id:"2.1.11",number:"II.1.11",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["pradhana-karma"],translation:"Therefore, only the primary actions directly yield the principal Apurva (Paramapurva) which brings the final reward. [T\u0101ni eva pradh\u0101n\u0101ni ap\u016brvam...]",commentary:"Reiterating the hierarchy of ritual mechanics. The main sacrifice (Pradhana Yaga) is the engine; all other acts are just building the car.",keyPoints:["Primary Acts Produce the Main Apurva"]},{id:"2.1.12",number:"II.1.12",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["pradhana-karma"],translation:"The secondary acts (Gu\u1e47a-karmas) produce a visible, preparatory effect on the substance, rendering it fit for the primary act. [Dravyasa\u1e43sk\u0101rakarm\u0101\u1e47i...]",commentary:"While primary acts create invisible magic (Apurva), secondary acts (like husking rice) create visible change. If the rice isn't husked, you can't cook it for the primary offering.",keyPoints:["Secondary Acts Produce Visible Effects"]},{id:"2.1.13",number:"II.1.13",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"Distinct names indicate distinct actions, because of the difference in terms. [N\u0101madheye gu\u1e47a\u015brutir...]",commentary:"How do we know if we are looking at one big sacrifice, or two different sacrifices? Jaimini begins laying down the tests for 'Karma-bheda' (Differentiation of Action). The first test is 'Name' (N\u0101ma). If the Veda mentions the 'Jyotishtoma' and the 'Agnihotra', the differing proper names prove these are entirely distinct primary rituals generating different Apurvas, not variations of the same ritual.",keyPoints:["Differentiation of Action by Names"]},{id:"2.1.14",number:"II.1.14",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"When a name is derived from a deity, it indicates the specific action directed towards that deity, differentiating it from others. [Devat\u0101y\u0101\u015bca karmabheda\u1e25...]",commentary:"Expanding on the 'Differentiation by Name' rule (2.1.13). Names like 'Aindra' (relating to Indra) serve to split an action away from an 'Agneya' (relating to Agni) action, proving they are distinct rites.",keyPoints:["Names Denoting Action vs. Deity"]},{id:"2.1.15",number:"II.1.15",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"(Opponent says): The text 'Somena yajeta' (He sacrifices with Soma) enjoins a completely new primary sacrifice. [Som\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - Somena yajeteti karm\u0101ntaram...]",commentary:"A major test case in Mimamsa. The opponent argues that since the material (Soma) is introduced here with a verb, it must be commanding a brand new ritual, separate from the main Jyotishtoma.",keyPoints:["The Soma Sacrifice: Objection"]},{id:"2.1.16",number:"II.1.16",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, it merely enjoins a material (Soma) for a sacrifice that is already established (the Jyotishtoma). [Na, dravyacodanam...]",commentary:"Jaimini rejects this. The Jyotishtoma is already known. This sentence is simply providing the crucial accessory (the Soma plant) required to execute it.",keyPoints:["The Soma Sacrifice: Refutation"]},{id:"2.1.17",number:"II.1.17",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"Because the verb 'yajeta' (sacrifices) is an Anuv\u0101da (restatement) serving purely as an anchor for the new material. [Yajatir anuv\u0101da\u1e25...]",commentary:"If you want to tell someone *what* to sacrifice, you have to use the word 'sacrifice'. The verb isn't issuing a new command; it's a grammatical placeholder to attach the word 'Soma'.",keyPoints:["The Verb as an Anchor (Anuv\u0101da)"]},{id:"2.1.18",number:"II.1.18",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"If it were interpreted as enjoining both the sacrifice and the material simultaneously, it would cause a syntactical split (V\u0101kyabheda). [V\u0101kyabhedaprasa\u1e45g\u0101t...]",commentary:"If 'Somena yajeta' meant 'Perform a new sacrifice AND use Soma', the single sentence breaks into two independent commands, destroying its unity.",keyPoints:["Avoiding Syntactical Split"]},{id:"2.1.19",number:"II.1.19",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"A single sentence can only establish one unestablished fact at a time. [Ekam eva hi v\u0101kyam...]",commentary:"A core axiom of Mimamsa linguistics. A sentence has one primary payload. Everything else must be logically subordinate to that single payload.",keyPoints:["One Command Per Sentence"]},{id:"2.1.20",number:"II.1.20",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"Thus, the material is subordinate to the already recognized action. [Tasm\u0101t dravya\u1e43 karma\u1e47i...]",commentary:"Concluding the Soma debate: the action (Jyotishtoma) is the king, and Soma is merely its servant, attached via a restated verb.",keyPoints:["Material is Subordinate"]},{id:"2.1.21",number:"II.1.21",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"When multiple accessories are mentioned in a single sentence without their own main verb, they are all collectively subordinate to the main act. [Gu\u1e47\u0101n\u0101\u1e43 ca...]",commentary:"If a text says 'With curds, milk, and ghee', because there is no new verb splitting them up, they all funnel together to serve the single primary verb found nearby.",keyPoints:["Multiple Accessories Without a Verb"]},{id:"2.1.22",number:"II.1.22",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"If there are multiple primary verbs, each constitutes a distinct injunction. [\u0100khy\u0101tabhede...]",commentary:"Conversely, if the Veda says 'He sacrifices, he pours, he gives', these are three distinct verbs, therefore they command three distinct actions.",keyPoints:["Multiple Verbs Imply Multiple Actions"]},{id:"2.1.23",number:"II.1.23",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"Unless they are syntactically dependent on a single primary verb. [Na tu ekav\u0101kyatve...]",commentary:"If it says 'While pouring, he sacrifices', 'pouring' is a participle/dependent verb serving the main verb 'sacrifices'. It remains one action.",keyPoints:["Exception for Dependent Verbs"]},{id:"2.1.24",number:"II.1.24",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["vakyabheda"],translation:"This establishes the fundamental rule of Syntactical Unity (Ekav\u0101kyat\u0101): when a group of words serves a single purpose and requires each other to be complete, they form one sentence. [Arthaikatv\u0101d eka\u1e43 v\u0101kyam s\u0101k\u0101\u1e45k\u1e63a\u1e43 ced vibh\u0101ge sy\u0101t]",commentary:"One of the most famous sutras in Mimamsa. A sentence is defined not by punctuation, but by purpose (Artha) and mutual expectancy (Akanksha). If separating the words leaves them meaningless, they are one sentence.",keyPoints:["Definition of Syntactical Unity (Ekav\u0101kyat\u0101)"]},{id:"2.1.25",number:"II.1.25",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"If they can be split and still make independent sense, they are distinct sentences (V\u0101kyabheda). [Vibh\u0101ge tu...]",commentary:"If breaking a long Vedic passage yields two perfectly complete, independent commands, then it must be treated as two sentences commanding two distinct acts.",keyPoints:["Definition of Syntactical Split (V\u0101kyabheda)"]},{id:"2.1.26",number:"II.1.26",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"Therefore, one must carefully identify the primary verb to avoid interpreting one act as many, or many acts as one. [Tasm\u0101t kriy\u0101\u1e43...]",commentary:"This is the prime directive for the priest and the philosopher: find the main verb, and build the entire ritual structure around it.",keyPoints:["Practical Application of Syntax Rules"]},{id:"2.1.27",number:"II.1.27",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"Subordinate clauses are recognized by the use of relative pronouns (like 'yad' - 'that which' or 'tad' - 'that'). [Yat-tat-\u015babdopabandh\u0101t...]",commentary:"Grammar lesson: 'That which he offers...' is a subordinate clause. It cannot issue a primary command (Vidhi).",keyPoints:["Subordinate Clauses (Relative Pronouns)"]},{id:"2.1.28",number:"II.1.28",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"They serve merely to qualify or restrict the main injunction. [Vise\u1e63a\u1e47\u0101rtham...]",commentary:"These clauses act like adjectives, narrowing down the scope of the main command so the priest knows exactly *how* or *when* to execute it.",keyPoints:["Function of Subordinate Clauses"]},{id:"2.1.29",number:"II.1.29",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Syntax)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"By maintaining these syntactic rules, the integrity and singular focus of the Vedic command is preserved. [Iti v\u0101kya-niyama\u1e25...]",commentary:"Concluding the massive section on syntax. Without these strict grammatical rules, interpreters could infinitely splinter or merge rituals, destroying the Veda's precision.",keyPoints:["Integrity of the Command"]},{id:"2.1.30",number:"II.1.30",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"Those Vedic texts which serve merely to assert or recall things (deities, materials) at the time of performance are termed 'Mantras'. [Tacodake\u1e63u mantr\u0101khy\u0101]",commentary:"Jaimini provides a strict, structural definition for classifying the contents of the Veda. A Mantra is not defined by its meter or mysticism; it is defined by its ritual function. If a sentence's purpose is to be recited *during* the physical execution of an act to keep the priest's mind fixed on the relevant deity or object, it is a Mantra.",keyPoints:["Definition of Mantras"]},{id:"2.1.31",number:"II.1.31",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"Mantras do not enjoin action, because they lack the force of an injunction (Vidhi). [Amantr\u0101\u1e47\u0101m...]",commentary:"Mantras often use imperative verbs (e.g., 'O Agni, burn the enemy!'). However, because they are structurally subservient to the main ritual command (which is found elsewhere in the prose section), these imperatives are considered figurative or meditative. You cannot derive a new rule of Dharma directly from a Mantra.",keyPoints:["Mantras are not Injunctions"]},{id:"2.1.32",number:"II.1.32",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"The rest (of the Veda) is termed 'Br\u0101hma\u1e47a'. [\u015ae\u1e63e br\u0101hma\u1e47a\u015babda\u1e25]",commentary:"This is a brilliant, exclusionary definition. Having defined what a Mantra is, Jaimini declares that absolutely everything else in the Veda (the injunctions, the explanatory stories/Arthavadas, the theological discussions) falls under the category of 'Br\u0101hma\u1e47a'. The Br\u0101hma\u1e47a section is the true source of Vidhi (the commands that dictate Dharma).",keyPoints:["Definition of Brahmana (Prose Injunctions)"]},{id:"2.1.33",number:"II.1.33",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"Among the Mantras, those which are divided into metrical feet (p\u0101das) according to meaning are called '\u1e5ac'. [Te\u1e63\u0101m \u1e5bgyatra arthava\u015bena p\u0101davyavasth\u0101]",commentary:"Jaimini sub-classifies the Mantras. If a Mantra is poetic, highly structured in specific meters (like Gayatri or Trishtubh), and divided into strict syllables and feet (padas), it is classified as an \u1e5ac. These form the Rig Veda.",keyPoints:["Definition of an \u1e5ac (Rig Veda Verse)"]},{id:"2.1.34",number:"II.1.34",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"The term 'S\u0101man' applies where the text is set to music (melody). [G\u012bti\u1e63u s\u0101m\u0101khy\u0101]",commentary:"If a Mantra (usually an \u1e5ac) is modified, stretched, and sung according to complex musical notation and melodies for specific rituals, it becomes a S\u0101man. These form the Sama Veda.",keyPoints:["Definition of a S\u0101man (Sama Veda Chant)"]},{id:"2.1.35",number:"II.1.35",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 1 (Bhavana & Apurva)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["ekavakyata"],translation:"The rest of the Mantras (prose formulas, lacking strict meter and melody) are called 'Yajus'. [\u015ae\u1e63e yaju\u1e25\u015babda\u1e25]",commentary:"Another exclusionary definition. Any Mantra that is not musically chanted (Saman) and not bound by strict metrical feet (Ric) is a prose formula called a Yajus. These form the Yajur Veda. With these sutras, Jaimini has cleanly mapped the entire structural taxonomy of the Vedic corpus based purely on performative utility.",keyPoints:["Definition of a Yajus (Yajur Veda Formula)"]},{id:"2.2.1",number:"II.2.1",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"When there is a difference of words (verbs), there is a difference of action, because they are attached to different roots. [\u015aabd\u0101ntare karmabheda\u1e25 k\u1e5bt\u0101nubandhatv\u0101t]",commentary:"This is the first and most fundamental proof for differentiating actions (Karma-bheda). The opponent argues that all sacrifices are essentially the same act of 'worship'. Jaimini refutes this. If the Veda uses different verb roots\u2014such as 'Yajati' (he sacrifices), 'Juhoti' (he pours an oblation), and 'Dad\u0101ti' (he gives a gift)\u2014these command fundamentally different physical mechanics. A 'Yaga' involves dedicating something to a deity, a 'Homa' requires pouring it into fire, and a 'Dana' involves transferring ownership. Different verbs generate different Apurvas (unseen results). They are distinct actions.",keyPoints:["1. Difference of Words/Verbs (\u015aabd\u0101ntara)"]},{id:"2.2.2",number:"II.2.2",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"The repetition of the same word (verb) indicates a difference of action, because without a distinction, the repetition would be meaningless. [Ekasyaiva puna\u1e25 \u015brutir avi\u015be\u1e63\u0101d anarthaka\u1e43 hi sy\u0101t]",commentary:"This introduces the second proof: Abhy\u0101sa (Repetition). In the Pray\u0101ja offerings, the text repeats the same verb five times: 'He sacrifices to the Samidhs... He sacrifices to Tanunapat...' The opponent asks: Since the verb 'sacrifices' is identical, isn't this just one single long sacrifice? Jaimini answers: No. The Veda is never redundant (as proven in Adhyaya 1). If it were one single action, mentioning the verb five times would be useless. The deliberate repetition of the verb proves it is a sequence of five distinct, separate micro-sacrifices.",keyPoints:["2. Repetition of the Same Verb (Abhy\u0101sa)"]},{id:"2.2.3",number:"II.2.3",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"(Opponent says): The \u0100gh\u0101ra libations must be separate primary actions because the text repeats the pouring in different directions. [\u0100gh\u0101r\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - Bheda\u1e25 sy\u0101t...]",commentary:"The text says 'He pours the Aghara crossing straight' and 'He pours the Aghara continuously'. The opponent thinks these are two distinct sacrifices due to the differing directional qualities.",keyPoints:["The \u0100gh\u0101ra Libations: Objection"]},{id:"2.2.4",number:"II.2.4",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, it is a single action, because the name '\u0100gh\u0101ra' is singular and connects them to a single unseen result. [Na, eka\u1e43 v\u0101...]",commentary:"Despite the physical differences in how the ghee is poured, they share the same title, same material, and same context. Therefore, it is one continuous action (like drawing a shape in two strokes) rather than two separate sacrifices.",keyPoints:["The \u0100gh\u0101ra Libations: Refutation"]},{id:"2.2.5",number:"II.2.5",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"The \u0100gh\u0101ra is singular because there is no distinct deity mentioned for each directional pouring. [Devat\u0101-bhed\u0101bh\u0101v\u0101t...]",commentary:"If one pour was for Agni and the other for Indra, they would be two acts. Without distinct deities, there is nothing to split the internal unity of the act.",keyPoints:["Lack of Distinct Deities"]},{id:"2.2.6",number:"II.2.6",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"The different directions (straight, continuous) are merely physical qualities (Guna) of a single act of pouring. [Gu\u1e47am\u0101tram...]",commentary:"Changing the angle of your arm does not change the metaphysical nature of the ritual if the core intent, name, and deity remain the same.",keyPoints:["Directions are Merely Physical Qualities"]},{id:"2.2.7",number:"II.2.7",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sabdantara"],translation:"Therefore, they do not split the action. [Tasm\u0101t na bheda\u1e25]",commentary:"Concluding the Aghara debate. Physical variations within a continuous sequence do not constitute Karma-bheda (difference of action).",keyPoints:["Qualities Do Not Split the Action"]},{id:"2.2.8",number:"II.2.8",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"(Opponent): The Pray\u0101jas (fore-offerings) should be considered a single action because the verb 'yajati' (he sacrifices) is identical in all five statements. [Pray\u0101j\u0101dhikara\u1e47am - Ekam...]",commentary:"The text says 'He sacrifices to the Samidhs', 'He sacrifices to Tanunapat', etc., five times. The opponent applies the Aghara logic here, assuming it's one long act.",keyPoints:["The Pray\u0101ja Offerings: Objection"]},{id:"2.2.9",number:"II.2.9",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, they are distinct actions because they are connected with distinct, incompatible names (Samidh, Tan\u016bnap\u0101t, etc.). [Na, n\u0101ma-bhed\u0101t...]",commentary:"Jaimini flips the logic. Unlike the Aghara, the Prayajas specifically name completely different entities for each offering. This forces a split.",keyPoints:["The Pray\u0101ja Offerings: Refutation"]},{id:"2.2.10",number:"II.2.10",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"The differing names indicate differing deities (or specific aspects of deity) for each offering. [Devat\u0101-vi\u015be\u1e63a-pratipatte\u1e25...]",commentary:"Samidhs (the fuel sticks) and Tanunapat (the self-born fire) are treated as distinct divine recipients.",keyPoints:["Names Indicate Distinct Deities"]},{id:"2.2.11",number:"II.2.11",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"Since one discrete offering cannot be simultaneously given to two distinct, sequential deities, the acts must be separate. [Yugapat-asambhav\u0101t...]",commentary:"You can't pour one spoonful of ghee and say 'This is exclusively for A, and also exclusively for B.' The mechanics require five separate spoonfuls, hence five separate acts.",keyPoints:["Incompatibility of Deities"]},{id:"2.2.12",number:"II.2.12",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"The deliberate repetition of the verb 'yajati' five times structurally confirms this separation. [Abhy\u0101s\u0101c ca...]",commentary:"If it were one act, the Veda would just say 'He sacrifices to Samidhs, Tanunapat, etc.' The fact that it re-states the verb every single time proves it wants five distinct events.",keyPoints:["Repetition Confirms Separation"]},{id:"2.2.13",number:"II.2.13",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"The unseen result (Utpatti-Ap\u016brva) of each Pray\u0101ja contributes sequentially to the main sacrifice. [P\u1e5bthak-ap\u016brvatv\u0101t...]",commentary:"Each of the five acts generates a mini-battery charge. Together, they prepare the metaphysical ground for the main event.",keyPoints:["Apurvas of the Prayajas"]},{id:"2.2.14",number:"II.2.14",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"They are primary in their own local sphere, but entirely subordinate to the whole Dar\u015bap\u016br\u1e47am\u0101sa. [Pradh\u0101na-\u015be\u1e63atv\u0101t...]",commentary:"They are not 'Guna-karmas' (like washing rice) because they don't visibly alter a physical substance. They are minor primary acts (Sannipatyopak\u0101raka) serving the whole.",keyPoints:["Hierarchy of the Prayajas"]},{id:"2.2.15",number:"II.2.15",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"Thus, a distinction of deity is a strict and absolute proof of a distinction of action. [Iti devat\u0101-bheda\u1e25 pram\u0101\u1e47am]",commentary:"Concluding the Prayaja Adhikarana. This locks in one of the core proofs of Karma-bheda (Action Differentiation).",keyPoints:["Deity Distinguishes Action"]},{id:"2.2.16",number:"II.2.16",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"Repetition does not differentiate an act if it is done merely to enumerate a sequence of mantras or specific participants. [Sa\u1e45khy\u0101su ca...]",commentary:"Qualifying the Abhyasa (repetition) rule from 2.2.2. If a priest is told to 'offer' while reciting Mantra A, and then 'offer' while reciting Mantra B, the act of offering is singular, the repetition is just a grammatical vehicle to attach the two different mantras sequentially.",keyPoints:["Exceptions to the Repetition Rule"]},{id:"2.2.17",number:"II.2.17",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"If a repetition is accompanied by a new accessory detail, it is not a new action, but a restatement for the sake of that detail. [Gu\u1e47\u0101rtha\u1e43 v\u0101 puna\u1e25 \u015bruti\u1e25]",commentary:"A vital caveat to the Abhy\u0101sa rule. If a text says 'He offers the Agnihotra', and later says, 'He offers the Agnihotra with curds,' the second sentence is NOT commanding a second, different Agnihotra. Because the second sentence introduces a specific material ('curds'), the verb is merely being repeated (Anuv\u0101da) to serve as an anchor for the new accessory (Gu\u1e47a), keeping the action singular.",keyPoints:["Resolving Apparent Repetition (Anuv\u0101da)"]},{id:"2.2.18",number:"II.2.18",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"When a text repeats an action merely to praise it, it does not create a new act. [Stutyartha\u1e43 v\u0101...]",commentary:"Following 2.2.17. Sometimes the Veda says 'He sacrifices' again just to hype it up. This does not mean you have to sacrifice twice.",keyPoints:["Repetition for Praise"]},{id:"2.2.19",number:"II.2.19",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"Such repetition is called Anuv\u0101da (restatement), and it is dependent on the original injunction. [Anuv\u0101do v\u0101...]",commentary:"Solidifying the hermeneutic concept of Anuvada. It has no independent commanding power.",keyPoints:["The Mechanism of Anuv\u0101da"]},{id:"2.2.20",number:"II.2.20",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["abhyasa"],translation:"It serves to emphasize the importance of the primary injunction in the mind of the performer. [Pradh\u0101na-d\u0101r\u1e0dhy\u0101rtham...]",commentary:"The psychology of ritual: repetition burns the importance of the act into the priest's memory.",keyPoints:["Emphasis of the Primary Injunction"]},{id:"2.2.21",number:"II.2.21",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sankhya"],translation:"Action is differentiated by number, because of the explicit introduction of separateness. [P\u1e5bthaktvanive\u015b\u0101t sa\u1e45khyay\u0101 karmabheda\u1e25]",commentary:"The third proof of Karma-bheda. The text commands: 'He offers three libations (tisra \u0101hut\u012br juhoti).' The opponent suggests this is one offering done three times. Jaimini clarifies that the explicit use of the number 'three' mathematically splits the event into three distinct, primary actions, each generating its own localized result that contributes to the whole. Number strictly divides action.",keyPoints:["3. Separation by Number (Sa\u1e45khy\u0101)"]},{id:"2.2.22",number:"II.2.22",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sankhya"],translation:"A proper name also differentiates actions, because it is connected with the origin of the act. [Sa\u1e43j\xf1\u0101 ca utpattisa\u1e43yog\u0101t]",commentary:"The fourth proof. If the Veda mentions the 'Jyoti\u1e63\u1e6doma' (The Praise of Light) in one place, and the 'A\u015bvamedha' (The Horse Sacrifice) in another, the differing proper titles are not merely alternative labels for the same generic ritual. The name acts as a rigid boundary. Different names signify completely different primary rituals with different structures and outcomes.",keyPoints:["4. Separation by Proper Name (Sa\u1e43j\xf1\u0101)"]},{id:"2.2.23",number:"II.2.23",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sankhya"],translation:"An accessory/quality (Gu\u1e47a) also differentiates actions, when it is connected with the origin (and is incompatible with another). [Gu\u1e47a\u015b ca ap\u016brvasa\u1e43yog\u0101t]",commentary:"The fifth proof. In a ritual text, we read 'He offers \u0100mik\u1e63\u0101 (solid curds) to the Vi\u015bvedevas' and 'He offers V\u0101jina (liquid whey) to the V\u0101jins.' Can these be the same sacrifice? No. You cannot simultaneously offer solid curds and liquid whey as the primary material in the exact same moment. Because the specific materials (Gu\u1e47as) and the specific deities are completely distinct and incompatible in a single act, they force a split in the syntax. They must be two distinct sacrifices.",keyPoints:["5. Separation by Incompatible Accessories (Gu\u1e47a)"]},{id:"2.2.24",number:"II.2.24",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["sankhya"],translation:"If a text enjoins a totally different, incompatible material (like liquid whey instead of solid curds) for the same apparent offering, the action is split into two. [Gu\u1e47a-virodh\u0101t ca...]",commentary:"Fleshing out 2.2.23. You cannot physically offer a solid and a liquid simultaneously as the singular primary material. Physics forces the logic to acknowledge two distinct sacrifices.",keyPoints:["Splitting Action by Incompatible Material"]},{id:"2.2.25",number:"II.2.25",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["samjna"],translation:"When there is a different context, there is a difference of action, because the purpose (or overarching domain) is different. [Prakara\u1e47\u0101ntare prayojan\u0101nyatvam]",commentary:"The sixth and final proof. The Veda describes an 'Agnihotra' (fire offering) in the daily, lifelong ritual section. Many pages later, in a section describing a specific month-long sacrifice (the Ku\u1e47\u1e0dap\u0101yina), it says 'He offers the Agnihotra for a month.' Is this a command to do the daily Agnihotra for a month? No. Because it is found in a completely walled-off, separate chapter/context (Prakara\u1e47a) dealing with a special massive ritual, this second 'Agnihotra' is a totally new, distinct subordinate action that simply borrowed the name of the daily one based on similarity. Context builds walls between actions.",keyPoints:["6. Separation by Context (Prakara\u1e47\u0101ntara)"]},{id:"2.2.26",number:"II.2.26",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["samjna"],translation:"(Opponent says): But because the ultimate result (e.g., Heaven) is one, the action should be considered one, despite differences in context or name. [Phalaikatv\u0101t karmaikatyam iti cet]",commentary:"The opponent argues against all six proofs of Karma-bheda. They point out that whether you perform the Jyotishtoma, the Darshapurnamasa, or the Agnihotra, the Veda promises the exact same fruit: 'Heaven' (Svarga). Since all rivers flow to the same ocean, they argue that conceptually, all these rituals are just variations of one single 'Sacrifice' aimed at Heaven.",keyPoints:["Objection: The Result Unifies the Action"]},{id:"2.2.27",number:"II.2.27",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["samjna"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, the actions are distinct because their direct causal mechanisms (Ap\u016brvas) are different, even if the final destination is the same. [Na, ap\u016brvabhed\u0101t]",commentary:"Jaimini rejects the opponent's view. You can travel to a city by walking, by riding a horse, or by taking a chariot. The destination is the same, but the actions, rules, and mechanisms of travel are completely different. Similarly, different rituals generate unique, specific metaphysical potencies (Apurvas). Because their internal mechanics and structural rules differ, they must be rigorously classified as separate actions.",keyPoints:["Refutation: Distinct Apurvas for Distinct Acts"]},{id:"2.2.28",number:"II.2.28",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda & Repetition)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["samjna"],translation:"The specific Apurvas (unseen forces) of these distinct actions merge to form the Paramap\u016brva (final unseen force) that yields the singular result. [Ap\u016brva-samuccay\u0101t...]",commentary:"Resolving the opponent's claim (in 2.2.26) that a singular reward (Heaven) implies a singular action. Jaimini explains that many separate rivers (minor Apurvas from distinct acts) combine to fill one ocean (the Paramapurva that grants Heaven).",keyPoints:["Merging of Distinct Apurvas"]},{id:"2.2.29",number:"II.2.29",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 2 (Karmabheda)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["samjna"],translation:"Subordinate acts (Gu\u1e47akarmas) do not have independent fruits; they serve only to fulfill the primary action. [Gu\u1e47akarma\u1e47\u0101\u1e43 tu pradh\u0101n\u0101rthatv\u0101t na p\u1e5bthak phalam]",commentary:"Having shown how to differentiate primary actions, Jaimini clarifies the nature of secondary actions (like washing rice or cutting grass). Even though we can differentiate them using the six proofs, we must remember they do not produce 'Heaven'. Their only 'fruit' is the physical preparation of the material used in the primary act. The entire complex is a highly structured, hierarchical mechanism.",keyPoints:["Subordinate Acts Do Not Have Independent Results"]},{id:"2.3.1",number:"II.3.1",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"(Opponent says): An accessory detail (Gu\u1e47a) and a new connection (\u015aruti) should dictate a difference of action, because of the introduction of a new property. [Gu\u1e47as tu \u015brutisa\u1e43yog\u0101t karm\u0101ntara\u1e43 prayojayet...]",commentary:"This opens Book 2, Chapter 3 (Adhyaya 2, Pada 3), focusing on the highly technical nuances of Karma-bheda (differentiation of actions) using accessories. The Veda says, 'Athai\u1e63a jyoti\u1e25' (Now this is the Jyotis), mentioning a reward of a thousand cows. The opponent argues that because 'Jyotis' is a new name and the 'thousand cows' is a new accessory reward, this must be an entirely distinct primary sacrifice from the standard Spring Jyotishtoma.",keyPoints:["Objection: A New Name and Accessory Create a New Action"]},{id:"2.3.2",number:"II.3.2",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"(Siddhanta): No, it is a restatement (Anuv\u0101da) of the original sacrifice, and the word 'Jyotis' is merely a figurative reference. [Avibh\u0101g\u0101t tu naiva\u1e43 sy\u0101t...]",commentary:"Jaimini refutes the opponent. The word 'Jyotis' (Light) is already a well-known alternate name for the standard Jyotishtoma sacrifice. Because the fundamental structure of the act is not split by incompatible deities or materials, the text is merely restating the main Jyotishtoma in order to prescribe a specific optional reward (a thousand cows) for a specific variation of it. It is not a new primary sacrifice; it is the old sacrifice with a new accessory attached.",keyPoints:["Refutation: It is a Restatement of the Known Sacrifice"]},{id:"2.3.3",number:"II.3.3",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"(Opponent says): The D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a sacrifice must be a distinct action because a specific distinct result is enjoined. [Phala\u015brutir yadi karm\u0101ntaram...]",commentary:"Another vital test case. The text says, 'He who desires heaven should perform the D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a sacrifice.' The opponent argues that since it promises a specific result and has a highly unique proper name, it must be a completely separate primary ritual from the standard Darshapurnamasa (New and Full Moon sacrifices).",keyPoints:["The D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a Sacrifice: Objection"]},{id:"2.3.4",number:"II.3.4",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"(Siddhanta): It is not a different action, because it merely enjoins a modification (Gu\u1e47a) of the existing New and Full Moon sacrifices. [Na, gu\u1e47a\u015brutir hi...]",commentary:"Jaimini lays down a crucial rule of Vedic interpretation. The D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a is not a distinct primary act. It is simply a highly specific, intensive method of performing the standard Darshapurnamasa sacrifices (doing them twice instead of once, with modified offerings). The name 'D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a' applies to the *method* or *modification*, not to a brand new primary entity. It's a variation on a theme, not a newly composed ritual.",keyPoints:["Refutation: The D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a is a Modification"]},{id:"2.3.5",number:"II.3.5",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"The word 'D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a' denotes a modification based on the specific time of performance (ayana). [K\u0101l\u0101bhy\u0101se v\u0101...]",commentary:"Elaborating on 2.3.4. Jaimini explains *why* D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a is not a new sacrifice. The name derives from a specific temporal rhythm (doing the New Moon sacrifice twice in a specific phase). It modifies the *when*, not the *what*.",keyPoints:["D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a as a Temporal Modification"]},{id:"2.3.6",number:"II.3.6",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"The specific result (Heaven/Cattle) mentioned is for the sake of adopting this specific modification, not for a new act. [Phala\u1e43 tu gu\u1e47acodanam...]",commentary:"The opponent in 2.3.3 asked: 'Why is a specific reward promised if it's not a new sacrifice?' Jaimini answers: The Veda is incentivizing you to perform the standard sacrifice *in this harder, modified way*. The reward is for the extra effort (the Guna), attached to the base action.",keyPoints:["Reward for Modifications"]},{id:"2.3.7",number:"II.3.7",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"The D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a modification specifically involves offering curds and milk on specific days. [Dadhi-paya\u1e25-sa\u1e43yog\u0101t...]",commentary:"Detailing the physical nature of the Guna (modification). It is a variation in the material schedule.",keyPoints:["Specifics of the D\u0101k\u1e63\u0101ya\u1e47a Modification"]},{id:"2.3.8",number:"II.3.8",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"It does not abandon or change the original deities of the standard Dar\u015bap\u016br\u1e47am\u0101sa sacrifices. [Devat\u0101-avyatikram\u0101t...]",commentary:"Because the core recipients (Agni, etc.) remain identical, the foundational structure of the sacrifice is unbroken.",keyPoints:["Original Deities Remain Intact"]},{id:"2.3.9",number:"II.3.9",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"It simply intensifies the performance through repetition and specific materials. [Abhy\u0101sa-vise\u1e63\u0101t...]",commentary:"It is the 'hard mode' of the standard sacrifice.",keyPoints:["Intensification of the Act"]},{id:"2.3.10",number:"II.3.10",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"The mention of 'Heaven' as a reward is an additional incentive for undertaking this intensified, grueling version. [Phal\u0101dhikya\u1e43 tu...]",commentary:"The Veda understands human nature. Extra effort requires extra reward (or an expedited reward).",keyPoints:["Reward as an Incentive"]},{id:"2.3.11",number:"II.3.11",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-vikara"],translation:"Therefore, it does not sever the act from its original context (Prakara\u1e47a) as a New and Full Moon sacrifice. [Prakara\u1e47a-anatikram\u0101t]",commentary:"Concluding the Dakshayana section. A modification (Guna) does not pull an act out of its home chapter.",keyPoints:["Context Remains Unbroken"]},{id:"2.3.12",number:"II.3.12",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["adhikara-bheda"],translation:"But the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di is an independent sacrifice, because of the connection with all three castes. [\u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6dau yaj\xf1asa\u1e43yog\u0101t...]",commentary:"This is a famous ruling (the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6dyadhikara\u1e47a). The \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di is a sequence of offerings found embedded directly inside the text describing the massive R\u0101jas\u016bya (Royal Consecration) sacrifice. The opponent assumes the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di is just a subordinate, dependent part of the R\u0101jas\u016bya. However, Jaimini points out that another text says: 'If a Brahmana performs the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di... if a Vaishya performs the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di...' Since the primary R\u0101jas\u016bya is strictly restricted only to the Kshatriya (Warrior/King) caste, the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di must be an independent primary sacrifice that can be performed by anyone, which is only *incidentally* inserted into the Kshatriya's R\u0101jas\u016bya.",keyPoints:["The \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di Sacrifice: An Independent Action"]},{id:"2.3.13",number:"II.3.13",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["adhikara-bheda"],translation:"Direct statement (\u015aruti) of the caste connection is stronger than the Context (Prakara\u1e47a) of the R\u0101jas\u016bya sacrifice. [\u015arutir hi bal\u012byas\u012b...]",commentary:"Elaborating on the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di ruling (2.3.12). The opponent argues that because the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di is written inside the chapter (Context/Prakarana) of the King's sacrifice, it must be restricted to Kings. Jaimini invokes the hierarchy of evidence: Direct Text (Shruti) always overrules Context (Prakarana). Since the text directly says 'Brahmana', the context is broken.",keyPoints:["\u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di: Context Cannot Override Direct Statement"]},{id:"2.3.14",number:"II.3.14",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["adhikara-bheda"],translation:"When two materials are mentioned, that which is physically connected with the act is primary, and the other is subordinate. [Dravyayo\u1e25 karmasa\u1e43yoge...]",commentary:"Moving to syntax and physical execution. If a text says, 'He offers curds with the cup,' both curds and the cup are nouns in the instrumental case. How do we know which is the actual offering? Jaimini establishes that 'curds' physically touch the fire and are consumed, directly fulfilling the verb 'offer'. The cup merely holds the curds. Thus, logically, curds are the primary material (Dravya) and the cup is the subordinate instrument.",keyPoints:["Establishing the Primary vs. Subordinate Material"]},{id:"2.3.15",number:"II.3.15",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["adhikara-bheda"],translation:"Regarding the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di, since it is an independent act, the specific reward mentioned for it (food/prosperity) is also independently attainable. [Svata\u1e47tra-phalatv\u0101t...]",commentary:"Since it's not a mere subordinate part of the Rajasuya, its promised reward actually works on its own.",keyPoints:["Independence of the \u0100ve\u1e63\u1e6di Reward"]},{id:"2.3.16",number:"II.3.16",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["adhikara-bheda"],translation:"When a Kshatriya performs it embedded within the R\u0101jas\u016bya, it serves the overall R\u0101jas\u016bya reward (imperial sovereignty). [K\u1e63atriyasya tu...]",commentary:"For the King, the Aveshti just acts as one brick in the massive wall of the Rajasuya. He doesn't get a separate side-reward; it fuels the main imperial Apurva.",keyPoints:["Performance by a Kshatriya"]},{id:"2.3.17",number:"II.3.17",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["adhikara-bheda"],translation:"While a Br\u0101hma\u1e47a performing it independently reaps its specific, isolated reward (food/prosperity). [Br\u0101hma\u1e47asya p\u1e5bthak...]",commentary:"For the priest, who cannot do the Rajasuya, the Aveshti functions as a standalone, desire-driven (Kamya) sacrifice for prosperity.",keyPoints:["Performance by a Brahmana"]},{id:"2.3.18",number:"II.3.18",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"The primary material is determined by its direct, undeniable syntactical connection to the verb of offering. [S\u0101k\u1e63\u0101t-sa\u1e43yog\u0101t...]",commentary:"Returning to syntax (following 2.3.14). The thing that gets thrown in the fire is the primary material, because it directly fulfills the verb 'offer'.",keyPoints:["Identifying the Primary Material"]},{id:"2.3.19",number:"II.3.19",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"Secondary materials (like the holding vessel) are recognized by their instrumental or locative nature in the sentence. [Tad-upak\u0101rakatv\u0101t...]",commentary:"The cup is in the instrumental case ('with the cup'). It helps the offering happen, but it isn't the offering itself.",keyPoints:["Identifying Secondary Materials"]},{id:"2.3.20",number:"II.3.20",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"If two materials are equally prominent and explicitly enjoined for the same exact act, there is an option (Vikalpa) to use either. [Tulya-balatve vikalpa\u1e25...]",commentary:"If the text says 'Offer rice' and later says 'Offer barley' for the identical rite, you can choose one.",keyPoints:["The Problem of Option (Vikalpa)"]},{id:"2.3.21",number:"II.3.21",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"But option is considered a flaw (Do\u1e63a) in ritual logic, because acting on one nullifies the scriptural authority of the other at that moment. [Vikalpasya a\u1e63\u1e6da-do\u1e63atv\u0101t...]",commentary:"Mimamsa hates 'options'. If you choose rice, you are effectively disobeying the verse that commanded barley. This is the 'Eight-fold flaw of Vikalpa'.",keyPoints:["Option is a Flaw in Ritual"]},{id:"2.3.22",number:"II.3.22",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"Therefore, one must always strive to establish a primary-subordinate relationship or a contextual difference to avoid an option. [Tasm\u0101t apav\u0101da\u1e25...]",commentary:"The philosopher must dig deep into the text to prove that 'rice' is for the main sacrifice, and 'barley' is maybe for a specific modification or a different context entirely, thus saving both verses from being disobeyed.",keyPoints:["Avoidance of Option"]},{id:"2.3.23",number:"II.3.23",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Variations & Option)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"Only when such a hierarchical or contextual relationship is completely impossible to establish does a true option exist. [Asambhave tu...]",commentary:"Vikalpa (Option) is the absolute last resort in Mimamsa interpretation.",keyPoints:["True Option is Rare"]},{id:"2.3.24",number:"II.3.24",section:"Adhyaya 2, Pada 3 (Anuvada & Variations)",devanagari:"",iast:"",conceptIds:["guna-proof"],translation:"An action mentioned in a specific context (Prakara\u1e47a) without an independent result is subordinate to the principal action of that context. [Phal\u0101bh\u0101v\u0101t tu prakara\u1e47am...]",commentary:"This reinforces the rule of Context (Prakara\u1e47a) from Book 2 Chapter 2. If you are reading the chapter on the Darshapurnamasa sacrifice, and you suddenly read the injunction, 'He sweeps the fire-altar,' you do not assume this sweeping grants Heaven on its own. Because it lacks a stated reward (Phala), the gravitational pull of the chapter's Context forces it to become a subordinate, preparatory act serving the main sacrifice of that specific chapter.",keyPoints:["Actions Enjoined by Context (Prakara\u1e47a)"]}]},1144,[]);
